// Generated by scripts/gen-l10n.mjs (gdetail n4, en) — 不要手改;改翻譯請改 scripts/out/l10n/en/ 再重 build。
window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N=window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N||{};window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]=Object.assign(window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]||{},{"n4-17":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ために」 means <b>\"in order to ～\"</b> or <b>\"for the sake of ～\"</b>. Before it, put the goal you want to reach; after it, put what you <b>deliberately do</b> to reach it. For example, \"I'm saving money in order to buy a car\" is: <span class='gd-jp'>車を買うために、お金をためています。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: use the dictionary form (the base form you find in a dictionary) + ために.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買うために</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強するために</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add の in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家族</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家族のために</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>健康</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>健康のために</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In order to do something</span>After a verb, it states the goal you want to accomplish.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語を勉強するために、日本へ来ました。</span>（I came to Japan to study Japanese.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>資格試験に合格するために、毎日図書館へ通っています。</span>（To pass the certification exam, I go to the library every day.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>For someone's or something's benefit</span>After a noun, it means \"for the sake of it.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族のために、頑張って働いています。</span>（I work hard for my family.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>健康のために、毎朝走っています。</span>（I run every morning for my health.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun must be followed by の: <span class='gd-jp'>家族ために</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>家族のために</span>.<br>・Before ために, the verb is <b>always in dictionary form</b>, even when the whole sentence is about the past: <span class='gd-jp'>切符を買うために、早く家を出ました。</span>（I left home early to buy a ticket.）You would never say 買ったために.<br>・The subject before and after is usually <b>the same person</b>: I do something myself for my own goal. For something like \"so that my kids can go to college,\" where someone else is the one who achieves it, use ように instead: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもが大学に行けるように、働いています。</span>（I work so that my kids can go to college.）<br>・Don't put a \"can ～\" expression (potential form) before it: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるために</span> sounds unnatural; say <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるように</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ために vs ように (purpose)</b><br>Both can be translated as \"in order to,\" and the difference is the word before them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語の新聞を読むために、漢字を勉強しています。</span>（I'm studying kanji in order to read Japanese newspapers.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語の新聞が読めるように、漢字を勉強しています。</span>（I'm studying kanji so that I can read Japanese newspapers.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before ために comes \"<b>doing</b> something\" (<span class='gd-jp'>読む</span>); before ように comes \"<b>being able to ～</b>\" or \"becoming a certain state\" (<span class='gd-jp'>読める</span>).</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ために (purpose) vs ために (cause)</b><br>The form is exactly the same, but it can also mean \"because of.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>健康のために、野菜を食べています。</span>（I eat vegetables for my health.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨のために、試合が中止になりました。</span>（Because of the rain, the game was canceled.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>If what follows is something you <b>choose to do</b>, it means \"in order to.\" If it's a result that <b>already happened and wasn't a choice</b>, it means \"because of.\" The cause usage is rather formal; in everyday speech, \"because\" is usually expressed with <span class='gd-jp'>から</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>ので</span>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what comes before is \"doing ～,\" use ために; if it's \"being able to ～,\" use ように. If what follows is something you do on purpose, ために means \"in order to\"; if it's a result that happened, ために means \"because of.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change ために to 「ための＋noun」 to mean \"a … for ～\": <span class='gd-jp'>留学のためのお金をためています。</span>（I'm saving money for studying abroad.）</div>","n4-18":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ために」 goes after a cause and means <b>\"because of ~\" or \"due to ~\"</b>. It's more formal and objective, like a subway announcement: \"Due to a signal failure, trains will be delayed.\" You'll see it a lot in news, notices, and when explaining a situation to customers or your boss.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>News & notices</span>Reporting what happened and what resulted.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大雪のために、新幹線が止まりました。</span>(Due to heavy snow, the Shinkansen stopped running.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Formal explanation</span>Explaining to a teacher, boss, or customer why something went wrong. It sounds objective, not like an excuse.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車が遅れたために、授業に間に合いませんでした。</span>(Because the train was delayed, I didn't make it to class.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The result that follows is usually something that <b>has already happened and is out of your control</b>, and often something bad, like a suspension, delay, or cancellation.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the \"cause\" before ために; almost any kind of word can go there. Just remember: nouns need an extra 「の」, and な-adjectives need an extra 「な」.<br>Verb た-form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きたために</span></span><br>Verb ない-form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>足りない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>足りないために</span></span><br>In progress (ている): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>工事をしている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>工事をしているために</span></span><br>State verbs (ある、いる、できる): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>用事がある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>用事があるために</span></span><br>い-adjectives attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>狭い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>狭いために</span></span><br>な-adjectives add な: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便なために</span></span><br>Nouns add の: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大雪</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大雪のために</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道が狭いために、大きい車は通れません。</span>(Because the road is narrow, large vehicles can't get through.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>用事があるために、会議に出られません。</span>(Because I have something to attend to, I can't take part in the meeting.)</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>のため</span>Notices and announcements often drop 「に」, giving 「〜のため」 or 「〜ため」. The meaning is the same, but it sounds even more formal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>工事中のため、この道は通れません。</span>(Road under construction; this road is closed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風のため、明日の授業は休みです。</span>(Because of the typhoon, tomorrow's class is cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun must be followed by 「の」: say <span class='gd-jp'>大雪のために</span>, not 大雪ために. A な-adjective needs 「な」: <span class='gd-jp'>不便なために</span>.<br>・The second half can't be a request or an invitation such as 「～てください」 or 「～ましょう」.<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>雨のために、傘を持っていってください。</span><br>○<span class='gd-jp'>雨なので、傘を持っていってください。</span>(Since it's raining, please take an umbrella.)<br>・The tone is stiff. If you tell a friend <span class='gd-jp'>雨のために行かない</span>, it sounds like you're reading a press release. Between friends, just use 「から」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ために (purpose) vs ために (cause)</b>: It's the same word, so you tell them apart by what comes before it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くために、お金をためています。</span>(I'm saving money in order to go to Japan: purpose)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雪が降ったために、電車が遅れました。</span>(Because it snowed, the train was delayed: cause)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「Noun + のために」 can mean either. It depends on whether the noun is a goal you're aiming for or an event that has already happened:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>健康のために、毎朝走っています。</span>(I run every morning for my health.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>病気のために、学校を休みました。</span>(Because of illness, I stayed home from school.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: look at what comes before ために. If it's an action you control and are trying to achieve (e.g., the dictionary form of <span class='gd-jp'>合格する</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>買う</span>), it means \"in order to\". If it's a た-form, なかった, or ている, a state verb or adjective such as <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>いる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span>, or a noun for an event or state such as <span class='gd-jp'>台風</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>病気</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>事故</span>, it means \"because\".<br>Be careful with the ない-form. After an action you control, it often means \"in order not to ~\", as in <span class='gd-jp'>遅れないために、早く家を出ます</span> (I leave home early so as not to be late). It only means \"because\" after a state you can't control, such as <span class='gd-jp'>足りない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>わからない</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ために vs から／ので</b>: Take \"It's raining, so the match is cancelled.\" You choose the wording by situation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨だから、試合は中止だって。</span>(It's raining, so I hear the match is cancelled: chatting with a friend)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨なので、試合は中止です。</span>(Because it's raining, the match is cancelled: polite explanation)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大雨のため、試合は中止になりました。</span>(Due to heavy rain, the match has been cancelled: notice or news)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: use 「から」 with friends, 「ので」 for polite explanations, and 「ため（に）」 only in news, notices, and formal documents. When the second half asks the listener to do something, use ので or から, not ために.</div>","n4-19":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ように</span>」 means <b>\"so that I can ~\" or \"so that ~ doesn't happen\"</b>. The key is that the result you want is <b>not fully up to you</b>, so all you can do is prepare. Think of \"I set two alarms so I won't oversleep\": you can't control whether you oversleep, but you can set the alarms.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first half states the \"state you hope for\", and the second half states \"what you do for it\".<br><b>Hoping you'll be able to ~</b>: potential form + ように. The potential form is the \"can ~\" form, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span></span> (can speak).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せるように</span></span> (so that I can speak)<br><b>Hoping something won't happen</b>: ない-form + ように<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように</span></span> (so that I don't forget)<br><b>Verbs that aren't under your control by nature</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>治る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜になる</span>): use the dictionary form + ように directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>治る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>治るように</span></span> (so that it gets better)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>So that you can do it</span>You want to build a certain ability.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるように、毎日練習しています。</span>(I practice every day so that I can speak Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>So that it doesn't happen</span>You prepare ahead to avoid something unwanted.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモしておきます。</span>(I'll write it down so I don't forget.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪をひかないように、暖かくして寝てください。</span>(Please keep warm when you sleep so you don't catch a cold.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>So that someone else can ~</span>The two halves can have different subjects: the first half is about someone else, and the second half is what you do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供が食べられるように、小さく切ります。</span>(I cut it small so the kids can eat it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>後ろの人にも聞こえるように、大きい声で話します。</span>(I speak loudly so the people in the back can hear too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・To say \"so that I <b>can</b> speak\", you need the potential form <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span>. With plain <span class='gd-jp'>話す</span>, the \"can\" meaning is gone.<br>・Before ように you always use the dictionary form or the ない-form, even when the whole sentence is about the past: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモしました。</span> (I wrote it down so I wouldn't forget.) You never say 「忘れなかったように」.<br>・English uses \"in order to\" for everything, but Japanese doesn't use ように every time. For actions you can simply do whenever you choose, like buying, going, or studying, use <span class='gd-jp'>ために</span> (see below).<br>・ように itself has no politeness level. Politeness is decided by the sentence ending: <span class='gd-jp'>メモします</span> is polite, and <span class='gd-jp'>メモする</span> is the casual version.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both of these talk about \"why I study Japanese\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本で働くために、日本語を勉強しています。</span> (I study Japanese in order to work in Japan.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本人と話せるように、日本語を勉強しています。</span> (I study Japanese so that I can talk with Japanese people.)<br>\"Going to Japan to work\" is an action you can carry out once you decide to, so use <b>ために</b>. \"Being able to talk with Japanese people\" is an ability that only develops gradually after you study, and you can't just do it on command, so use <b>ように</b>. Saying 「話せるために」 would sound very unnatural.<br>One more point: when the two halves have different subjects, as in <span class='gd-jp'>子供が食べられるように、小さく切ります</span> (the kids eat, I cut), ように is the right choice. ために usually has the same subject in both halves.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is \"can ~\" or \"not ~\", or something you can't control, use ように. If it's an action you can simply go and do when you want, use ために.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When ように is followed directly by <span class='gd-jp'>する</span>, the meaning becomes \"try to ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>毎日野菜を食べるようにしています。</span> (I try to eat vegetables every day.)<br>When ように on its own is followed by another action, it means \"so that ~\", while <span class='gd-jp'>ようにする</span> means \"make an effort to ~\". Don't mix the two up.</div>","n4-20":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>These two look alike. The difference is whether the ending is <b>なる</b> or <b>する</b>.<br><b>ようになる</b> = “<b>come to</b> ~”. It describes a <b>result</b>: you couldn't before, and now you can.<br><b>ようにする</b> = “I <b>try to</b> ~”. It describes your own <b>ongoing effort</b>: you're still working at it, and there's no guarantee you'll succeed every time.<br>English “become / come to” and “try to” line up neatly with なる and する.</div><div class='gd-h'>ようになる: you couldn't before, now you can</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Change in ability</span>This is the most common use. It follows the <b>potential form</b> (the form meaning “can ~ / be able to ~”, like <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span>), or <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>練習して、日本語が少し話せるようになりました。</span>(After practicing, I've become able to speak a little Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>辛い物が食べられるようになった。</span>(I can eat spicy food now.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Change in habit</span>This follows the plain dictionary form, and it means “I've come to do this regularly”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来てから、毎朝コーヒーを飲むようになった。</span>(Since coming to Japan, I've started drinking coffee every morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The change may come from deliberate practice, or it may happen without you noticing. Either way, the focus is on the <b>result as it stands now</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>ようにする: I try to do this</div><div class='gd-sec'>Things you want to do: dictionary form + ようにする　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べるようにする</span></span><br>Things you want to avoid: ない form + ようにする　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲まない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まないようにする</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにしている</span>A habit you've been keeping up lately. This is the most common form.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日野菜を食べるようにしています。</span>(I try to eat vegetables every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜はコーヒーを飲まないようにしている。</span>(I try not to drink coffee at night.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにします</span>“From now on I'll be careful.” It's often used in apologies and promises.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからは遅れないようにします。</span>(From now on, I'll make sure not to be late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようにしてください</span>You're asking the other person to make an effort. Doctors and teachers say this a lot.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬は毎日飲むようにしてください。</span>(Please make sure to take your medicine every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・To say you've become <b>able</b> to do something, use the potential form before it. <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるようになった</span> means “I've become able to speak Japanese.” If you write <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すようになった</span>, it means “I've started speaking Japanese,” which is about a habit, not an ability.<br>・When you use the potential form, the 「を」 before it usually changes to 「が」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せる</span>.<br>・To say you've <b>stopped</b> doing something, Japanese people usually say <b>〜なくなる</b> directly: <span class='gd-jp'>最近、テレビを見なくなった。</span> (I've stopped watching TV lately.) “見ないようになった” is less common.<br>・Adjectives and nouns can't take ようになる. “Get bigger” is <span class='gd-jp'>大きくなる</span>, “become quiet” is <span class='gd-jp'>静かになる</span>, and “become a teacher” is <span class='gd-jp'>先生になる</span>. ようになる only attaches to verbs.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to talk about getting up early:<br><b>ようになった</b> = a result: <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きられるようになった。</span> (I've become able to get up early.)<br><b>ようにしている</b> = ongoing effort: <span class='gd-jp'>早く起きるようにしている。</span> (I try to get up early.)<br><b>ことにした</b> = a decision: <span class='gd-jp'>明日から早く起きることにした。</span> (I've decided to get up early starting tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>One more: <b>ことになった</b> means “it has been decided / arranged that ~”. You didn't change by yourself, and it isn't your own effort: <span class='gd-jp'>来月から大阪で働くことになった。</span> (It's been decided that I'll work in Osaka starting next month.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: “have come to ~” takes ようになる, “try to ~” takes ようにする, “decide to ~” takes ことにする, and “it's been arranged that ~” takes ことになる.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you drop なる or する, a standalone「ように」can mean “so that ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>忘れないように、メモします。</span> (I'll write it down so I don't forget.)</div>","n4-8":"<div class='gd-h'>The short version</div><div class='gd-sec'>「のに」 is like English <b>“even though ~, …”</b> or <b>“and yet …”</b>. The first part is something certain, but the result doesn't match what you'd expect, so you feel “how can that be?” That feeling might be annoyance, surprise, or disappointment.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだのに、熱が下がらない。</span>(Even though I took medicine, my fever won't go down.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The first part is an <b>established fact</b>. It can be something that already happened, a current state, or something that is sure to happen. The second part is a result that “shouldn't really be that way”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束したのに、彼は来なかった。</span>(Even though we'd agreed, he didn't come.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いのに、おいしい。</span>(This place is cheap, and yet it's delicious.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日テストなのに、全然勉強していない。</span>(Even though there's a test tomorrow, I haven't studied at all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The cheap-but-delicious example is a pleasant surprise, so のに isn't always a complaint. The key is <b>“contrary to expectation”</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the plain form before it (the form without ます).<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったのに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないのに</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いのに</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: add 「な」 first.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなのに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日曜日</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日曜日なのに</span></span><br>For the past tense, use だった: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったのに</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Trailing off</span>The result is left unsaid, which leaves a “sigh” feeling. It's very common for complaints and regret.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>せっかく作ったのに…。</span>(And I went to the trouble of making it…)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・な-adjectives and nouns must have な: say <span class='gd-jp'>静かなのに</span>, never 「静かのに」 or 「静かだのに」.<br>・After のに, you <b>can't put your own intention, an invitation, a command, or a request</b>. Things like “let's ~”, “I'll ~”, and “please ~” don't work.<br>　× 高いのに、買おう。<br>　○ <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど、買おう。</span> (It's expensive, but let's buy it.)<br>・のに carries emotion. If you say 「～のに」 directly to a superior or a customer, it can sound like you're complaining or blaming them, so watch the situation.<br>・There's another 「のに」 that looks identical and means “for / in order to do ~ (use, take)”: <span class='gd-jp'>駅へ行くのに30分かかる。</span> (It takes 30 minutes to get to the station.) It has no “even though” meaning. You can tell it apart if what follows is about time taken, something needed, or something used.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>のに</b> = even though ~, … (emotional): <span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだのに、熱が下がらない。</span> (Even though I took medicine, my fever won't go down. How can that be?)<br><b>けど</b> = although ~, but … (a plain contrast, calmer): <span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだけど、熱が下がらない。</span> (I took medicine, but my fever hasn't gone down.)<br><b>ても</b> = even if ~, …, and it can talk about hypotheticals that haven't happened yet: <span class='gd-jp'>明日雨が降っても、行きます。</span> (Even if it rains tomorrow, I'll go.)<br>You can't swap のに into this last sentence, because it's not certain yet whether it will rain tomorrow, and what follows is something you intend to do.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ので</b> goes in exactly the opposite direction: “because ~, so …”, following the result naturally:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨なので、出かけなかった。</span> (Because it was raining, I didn't go out.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨なのに、彼は出かけた。</span> (Even though it was raining, he went out.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if the first part is an established fact (not a hypothetical) and you're thinking “how can that be?”, use のに. For a plain contrast, use けど. For “even if ~”, use ても. When the result follows naturally, use ので.</div>","n4-22":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ても」 means <b>“even if ～” or “even though ～, still …”</b>. The first half steps back and grants a situation. The second half says the result stays the same anyway. For “Even if it rains, I’m going,” Japanese says <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、行きます。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the て form, then add 「も」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いても</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでも</span></span> (if the て form ends in 「で」, it becomes 「でも」)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ても</span>（きても）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: change the final 「い」 to 「く」, then add 「ても」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高くても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悪い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悪くても</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: just add 「でも」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かでも</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨でも</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Even if not ～</b>: change the 「ない」 of the ない form to 「なくても」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなくても</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Even if it happens</span>Suppose a situation, then say your decision won’t change.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>体調が悪くても、試合に出ます。</span>(Even if I don’t feel well, I’ll play in the match.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>高くても、買います。</span>(Even if it’s expensive, I’ll buy it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Tried it, still no luck</span>You really did it, and the result still fell short of what you expected.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度読んでも、意味がわかりません。</span>(Even after reading it many times, I still can’t understand it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電話しても、出ません。</span>(Even when I call him, he doesn’t pick up.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No matter what</span>Put a word like <span class='gd-jp'>いくら</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>どんなに</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何を</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>誰が</span> in front, and it means “no matter how much ～, …”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いくら食べても、太りません。</span>(No matter how much I eat, I don’t gain weight.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんなに忙しくても、朝ごはんは食べます。</span>(No matter how busy I am, I eat breakfast.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰が来ても、ドアを開けないでください。</span>(No matter who comes, please don’t open the door.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>読む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> have a て form ending in 「で」, so say <span class='gd-jp'>読んでも</span>, not 読んても.<br>・Nouns and な-adjectives take 「でも」: <span class='gd-jp'>雨でも</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>元気でも</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よくても</span>, not いくても.<br>・The second half should say that the result doesn’t change, or goes against expectation. For a natural, expected result, use たら (or ば) for the condition. Use から or ので only when the situation is already a fact and you’re giving a reason.<br>　<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったら、行きません。</span>(If it rains, I won’t go.)<br>　<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、行きます。</span>(Even if it rains, I’ll go.)<br>・The “even” of “even if” is already built into ても, so you don’t need to add 「も」 in the second half. If you say <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っても、私も行きます</span>, it means “I’m going too (like everyone else).”<br>・Don’t confuse it with 「〜てもいいです」. Adding いいです changes the meaning to “it’s okay to ～.” <span class='gd-jp'>食べてもいいです。</span>(You may eat it.) / <span class='gd-jp'>食べても、太りません。</span>(Even if I eat it, I won’t gain weight.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>のに</b> also has a “but still / even though” feel, but it’s used differently. Same rainy situation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨でも、行きます。</span>(Even if it rains, I’ll go: you state your own decision, and whether it rains right now doesn’t matter.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨なのに、彼は行きました。</span>(Even though it was raining, he went: it already happened, with a note of surprise or disapproval.)<br>The point of <b>ても</b> is “nothing in the first half can change the second half.” It can be hypothetical, or something you’ve already tried.<br><b>のに</b> always follows something that is already true, and the tone is “even though ～, (surprisingly) …”, often with complaint or surprise. Also, after のに you can’t use phrases like “I will ～” or “please ～”.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say “even if ～, I will / please do ～,” use ても. To complain “even though ～, how come …,” use のに.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>たとえ</span> in front for a stronger tone: <span class='gd-jp'>たとえ反対されても、留学します。</span>(Even if people object, I’ll study abroad.)</div>","n4-24":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てしまう」 covers English ideas like <b>“finish ～ off,” “end up ～ing,” and “accidentally ～.”</b><br>It has two meanings. One is <b>doing something completely, finishing it off</b>, like “I ate up the whole cake.” The other is <b>doing something by accident and feeling bad about it</b>, like “Oh no, I forgot my wallet.” What the two share is this: <b>the thing is done, and you can’t undo it</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Done completely</span>It stresses that the whole thing is thoroughly finished, often with a feel of “in one go” or “let’s get it out of the way.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題はもう全部やってしまいました。</span>(I’ve already finished all my homework.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ご飯の前に、宿題をやってしまおう。</span>(Let’s get the homework done before dinner.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>By accident / regret</span>You did something you didn’t mean to, or something turned out badly, and you feel “oh no” or “what a shame.” This is the meaning you’ll hear most in daily life.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>財布を忘れてしまいました。</span>(I accidentally forgot my wallet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大事なお皿を割ってしまった。</span>(I broke an important plate.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今朝、寝坊してしまいました。</span>(I accidentally overslept this morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Sometimes both meanings show up at once, so let the context decide: <span class='gd-jp'>ケーキを全部食べてしまった。</span> can simply mean “I ate up the whole cake,” or “I ended up eating the whole thing (oops).”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to the て form, then add <b>しまう</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れてしまう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>割る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>割って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>割ってしまう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んでしまう</span></span><br>しまう itself conjugates as a group 1 (godan) verb: polite is <span class='gd-jp'>しまいます</span>, past is <span class='gd-jp'>しまいました</span>, and among friends it’s <span class='gd-jp'>しまった</span>. Since you’re mostly talking about things that already happened, <b>the past form is the one you’ll hear most</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you’ll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Spoken contraction</span>In speech, 「てしまう」 often shrinks to <b>ちゃう</b>, and 「でしまう」 to <b>じゃう</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べてしまった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃった</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行ってしまう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行っちゃう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んでしまった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んじゃった</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、ケーキ全部食べちゃった。</span>(Ah, I ate the whole cake.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As a standalone phrase</span>The <span class='gd-jp'>しまった！</span> you blurt out when you realize a mistake means “Oh no!” or “Damn!”</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs whose て form ends in 「んで」 (<span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>読む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>死ぬ</span>) take <b>でしまう</b>, which contracts to <b>じゃう</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>飲んじゃった</span>, not 飲んちゃった.<br>・ちゃう and じゃう are casual. They sound natural with friends, but with a boss, a customer, or in a letter, <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまいました</span> is safer.<br>・The verb <span class='gd-jp'>しまう</span> on its own means “to put away”: <span class='gd-jp'>本をかばんにしまう。</span>(I put the book away in my bag.) With no て form in front, it’s not this grammar point.<br>・In English, “I forgot” is enough by itself, so many learners skip しまう. <span class='gd-jp'>忘れた</span> is grammatically fine, but it sounds like a plain statement of fact. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>忘れてしまった</span> is what brings out the “oh no, I feel bad” mood.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>For the same event, the plain past 「た」 and 「てしまった」 feel different:<br><b>た</b> = simply says what happened: <span class='gd-jp'>財布を忘れた。</span>(I forgot my wallet.)<br><b>てしまった</b> = adds the feeling of “oh no, what a shame”: <span class='gd-jp'>財布を忘れてしまった。</span>(Oh no, I forgot my wallet.)<br><br><b>た</b> = ate: <span class='gd-jp'>ケーキを食べた。</span>(I ate the cake.)<br><b>てしまった</b> = ate it all up, or ended up eating it by accident: <span class='gd-jp'>ケーキを食べてしまった。</span>(I ate up the cake.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: when in English you’d want to say “ended up ～ing,” “went and ～ed,” “～ed it all up,” or “oh no, I ～ed,” use てしまう in Japanese. If you’re just stating what happened, plain た is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another “て form + a verb” pattern is <span class='gd-jp'>〜ておく</span> (doing something in advance as preparation), and it’s worth comparing the two. Spoken contractions like ちゃう and じゃう will be covered all together in the lesson on contracted forms.</div>","n4-28":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ところ」originally means \"place,\" but here it becomes <b>a point in time</b>. It lines up neatly with English <b>\"about to…\"</b>, <b>\"in the middle of…\"</b> and <b>\"just…\"</b>: change the form of the verb before it, and you show how far along the action is.</div><div class='gd-h'>One situation, three answers</div><div class='gd-sec'>A friend calls and asks 「<span class='gd-jp'>今、大丈夫？</span>」(Is now a good time?). You can answer like this:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ごめん、今から出かけるところ。</span>(Sorry, I'm about to head out.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、ご飯を食べているところ。</span>(I'm in the middle of eating right now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょうど今、家に着いたところ。</span>(I just got home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>This is where 「ところ」shows up most: <b>telling someone what you're doing right now</b>. With friends you can just say 「ところ」; to be more polite, add 「です」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>About to do</span>Dictionary form (the base form you find in the dictionary) + ところ. The action <b>hasn't started yet</b> but is about to.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べるところです</span></span> (about to eat)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これから晩ご飯を作るところです。</span>(I'm about to start making dinner.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In the middle of doing</span>ている + ところ. The action is <b>halfway through</b> and not finished.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べているところです</span></span> (in the middle of eating)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、宿題をしているところです。</span>(I'm doing my homework right now.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just finished</span>た-form (the past-tense form) + ところ. The action has <b>only just ended</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたところです</span></span> (just finished eating)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、起きたところです。</span>(I just woke up.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Words that often appear with it: <span class='gd-jp'>これから</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>今から</span> (with \"about to\"), <span class='gd-jp'>今</span> (with \"in the middle of\"), <span class='gd-jp'>ちょうど</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>今</span> (with \"just finished\").</div><div class='gd-h'>You can use it in the past too</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change 「です」to 「でした」and you can say what stage you were at <b>at a certain moment in the past</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電話が来たとき、お風呂に入っているところでした。</span>(When the phone rang, I was in the middle of taking a bath.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達が来たとき、ちょうど出かけるところでした。</span>(When my friend came, I was just about to head out.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・These are three different points in time, and if you pick the wrong form the meaning changes: <b>dictionary form = not started yet, ている = in progress, た-form = just ended</b>. 「<span class='gd-jp'>食べるところです</span>」means \"about to eat,\" not \"eating right now.\"<br>・Before ところ, use the plain form, not ます: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行くところです</span>, not 行きますところです.<br>・「ているところ」describes an action in progress at <b>a specific point in time</b> (now, or a certain moment in the past). Daily habits can only use plain ている: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日日本語を勉強しています。</span>(I study Japanese every day.) You wouldn't say 「毎日勉強しているところです」.<br>・States that simply continue, like 「住んでいる」or 「結婚している」, can't take ところ either.<br>・「たところ」means <b>really just now</b>, usually within a few minutes, or a few hours at most. If you've already been in Japan for three months, don't say 「来たところです」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with its look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たところ</b> and <b>たばかり</b> (n4-29) both translate as \"just…\".<br>At the moment you've just walked into the station, both work: <span class='gd-jp'>今、駅に着いたところです。</span>/ <span class='gd-jp'>今、駅に着いたばかりです。</span> (I just arrived at the station.)<br>But if some time has passed and it only <b>feels recent to you</b>, you can only use たばかり: <span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たばかりです。</span> (I've only just come to Japan, e.g. three months ago.)<br>Also, たばかり can go directly before a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかりの靴</span> (shoes I just bought). たところ can't be used that way.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if it really happened just now and the timing is precise, use たところ; if a while has passed but it still feels new, use たばかり.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put 「<span class='gd-jp'>もう少しで</span>」before 「dictionary form + ところでした」and it becomes \"almost…\": <span class='gd-jp'>もう少しで電車に遅れるところでした。</span> (I almost missed the train.)</div>","n4-31":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そう」means <b>\"looks…\"</b> or <b>\"seems about to…\"</b>. You use it when you see how something looks and a feeling pops up right away. For example, you see a cake and say \"That looks delicious,\" or you see a dark sky and say \"It looks like it's going to rain.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs: about to happen</span>You see a sign and feel that something is about to happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>空が暗いですね。雨が降りそうです。</span>(The sky is so dark. It looks like it's going to rain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、ボタンが取れそう。</span>(Oh, that button is about to come off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Adjectives: looks…</span>An impression you get from the outward appearance.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、おいしそう！</span>(This cake looks delicious!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは今日も元気そうですね。</span>(Mr. Tanaka looks full of energy today too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the ます-form, drop ます, and add そう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降りそう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣きそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できそう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」and add そう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>熱い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>熱そう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: add そう directly, with no だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気そう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇そう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Two exceptions to memorize</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よさそう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なさそう</span></span><br>To say \"looks not…\", attach to the negative form of the adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしくない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしくなさそう</span></span><br>To say a verb \"doesn't look like it will happen,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>降りそうにない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>降りそうもない</span> (it doesn't look like it will rain).</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>そう behaves like a な-adjective: before a noun it becomes <b>そうな</b>, and before a verb it becomes <b>そうに</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>おいしそうなパンですね。</span>(That's some delicious-looking bread.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもたちが楽しそうに遊んでいる。</span>(The children are playing and seem to be having fun.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「looks…」form of <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> is <b>よさそう</b>; you can't say いさそう. And <span class='gd-jp'>いいそう</span> would mean \"I hear it's good.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>かわいそう</span> means \"poor thing,\" not \"looks cute.\"<br>・Don't use そう for things that are obvious at a glance. If a flower is beautiful, just say <span class='gd-jp'>きれいですね</span>, not きれいそう (\"looks beautiful\").<br>・You can't add this そう directly after a noun. You can't say 学生そう for \"looks like a student.\"<br>・Verb + そう often means \"<b>about to</b>…,\" not just \"seems to\": <span class='gd-jp'>泣きそう</span> means \"about to cry.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with its look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>The 「〜そうです (hearsay)」in the next lesson means <b>\"I hear that…\"</b>. It looks almost identical; only the form before it differs. Compare using the same words:<br><b>Verb</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうです。</span> (It looks like it's going to rain.)<br>　　　<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るそうです。</span> (I hear it's going to rain.)<br><b>い-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそうです。</span> (It looks delicious.)<br>　　　　<span class='gd-jp'>おいしいそうです。</span> (I hear it's delicious.)<br><b>な-adjective</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>元気そうです。</span> (He looks full of energy.)<br>　　　　<span class='gd-jp'>元気だそうです。</span> (I hear he's full of energy.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what comes before そう is the dictionary or plain form (<span class='gd-jp'>降る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>元気だ</span>) = hearsay. If the verb has ます dropped, the い-adjective has い dropped, or the な-adjective is attached directly with no だ (<span class='gd-jp'>降り</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おいし</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>元気</span>) = appearance.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn 「〜そうです (hearsay)」and practice the two side by side. After that comes 「〜ようです・〜みたいです」, which is \"it seems that…\" based on clues you've picked up.</div>","n4-32":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「〜そうです」 means <b>\"I heard that...\"</b> or <b>\"they say that...\"</b>, and in Japanese it goes at the very end of the sentence. When you pick something up from the news, a weather forecast, or a friend and want to pass it on, you add 「そうです」 after that sentence. It tells the listener, \"This isn't my own claim. I heard it from someone else.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新しい社長は厳しいそうです。</span>(I heard the new president is strict.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんはもう帰ったそうです。</span>(I heard Tanaka has already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form</b> before it, meaning the casual form without ます or です. Think of it this way: say the whole sentence you heard in casual speech, then stick そうです on the end.<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降るそうです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないそうです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>結婚した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>結婚したそうです</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いそうです</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: you must add <b>だ</b> first. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだそうです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だそうです</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Name the source first</span>It often follows 「〜によると」 (according to ~) or 「〜の話では」 (from what ~ says), so you say where you heard it before giving the information.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです。</span>(According to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>林さんの話では、あの店はおいしいそうです。</span>(According to Mr. Lin, that restaurant is good.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>With friends</span>Swap です for だ to get the casual version: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は休みだそうだ。</span> In casual chat, though, 「〜んだって」 and 「〜って」 are even more common: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、休みなんだって。</span> (I heard tomorrow's a day off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The most common slip is <b>forgetting だ</b> after nouns and な-adjectives. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨だそうです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>元気だそうです</span>. Without だ, the meaning shifts or the sentence doesn't work at all (see below).<br>・You can't put the ます form before it: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行くそうです</span>, not 「行きますそうです」.<br>・For \"I heard it <b>was</b>...\" or \"I heard it <b>isn't</b>...\", put the past or negative in the clause before it. そうです itself never changes: <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったそうです</span> (I heard it rained). You can't say 「雨だそうでした」.<br>・In English, \"I heard that...\" can open the sentence, but in Japanese そうです always comes at the end.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from its look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <b>様態そうです</b> from the previous lesson (\"looks like...\") is spelled exactly the same. The difference is <b>what comes before it</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このかばんは高いそうです。</span> (I heard this bag is expensive: someone told me.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>このかばんは高そうです。</span> (This bag looks expensive: I'm judging with my own eyes.)<br><br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るそうです。</span> (I heard it's going to rain: I saw the forecast.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうです。</span> (It looks like it's about to rain: the sky is dark.)<br><br><span class='gd-jp'>先生は元気だそうです。</span> (I heard the teacher is doing well.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生は元気そうです。</span> (The teacher looks energetic.)<br><br>「いい」 is a special case: \"I heard it's good\" is <span class='gd-jp'>いいそうです</span>, while \"it looks good\" is <span class='gd-jp'>よさそうです</span>.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before そうです is a <b>complete sentence</b> (高い, 降る, 元気だ), it means \"I heard\". If the word looks <b>chopped short</b> (高, 降り, 元気), it means \"looks like\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Compare this lesson with 「〜らしいです」, which can also mean \"I hear / it seems\", and with 「〜と言っていました」, which quotes someone's exact words.</div>","n4-33":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようです」 and 「〜みたいです」 mean <b>\"it seems...\"</b> or <b>\"it looks like...\"</b>. You've seen, heard, or sensed some clues, and you reach a conclusion on your own. Both mean the same thing, but <b>ようです is more formal</b> and <b>みたいです is more casual</b>. In everyday conversation, みたい sounds the most natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing from clues</span>This is the most common use. You aren't 100% sure, but you have some evidence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電気が消えていますね。田中さんはもう帰ったようです。</span>(The lights are off. It seems Mr. Tanaka has already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外がうるさいですね。何かあったみたいです。</span>(It's noisy outside. It seems something happened.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>風邪をひいたみたいです。</span>(I think I've caught a cold.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The last sentence is based on how your own body feels, such as a sore throat or chills. That counts as a clue too, so this is very common.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Comparison: \"like...\"</span>You know it isn't the real thing, you're just saying it's very similar. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>まるで</span> (just like).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まるで夢のようです。</span>(It's just like a dream.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは日本語が上手で、日本人みたいです。</span>(Mr. Tanaka's Japanese is so good that he's like a native Japanese person.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs and い-adjectives: attach directly. Both patterns work the same way.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰ったようです</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>帰ったみたいです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいようです</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいみたいです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns are different</b> (in the present tense):<br>ようです: な-adjectives take 「な」, and nouns take 「の」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなようです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生のようです</span></span><br>みたいです: add nothing, attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かみたいです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生みたいです</span></span><br>In the past tense, both take 「だった」: <span class='gd-jp'>学生だったようです</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>学生だったみたいです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Among friends</span>Drop です and say 「〜みたい」 or 「〜みたいだね」. <span class='gd-jp'>ようだ</span> is more like written language and is rarely used in chat.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店、休みみたい。</span>(That shop looks closed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before a noun</span>It becomes 「ような／みたいな」 + noun, meaning \"a ... like ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんみたいな人になりたいです。</span>(I want to become a person like Mr. Tanaka.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before an action</span>It becomes 「ように／みたいに」, meaning \"(doing something) like ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもみたいに喜んでいます。</span>(He's delighted like a little kid.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives are the easiest to get wrong. It's <span class='gd-jp'>学生みたい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>学生のようです</span>, not 「学生のみたい」. It's <span class='gd-jp'>静かみたい</span>, not 「静かなみたい」.<br>・Don't use it for things you've already confirmed with your own eyes. If you're standing in the rain, just say <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っています</span>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているようです</span> only when you hear the rain or see someone outside holding an umbrella.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>学生みたい</span> can mean \"he seems to be a student\" (a guess) or \"he's like a student\" (but isn't). Context tells you which.<br>・In reports and formal situations, use ようです. みたい is too casual.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from its look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take rain as the example:<br><b>そうです (appearance)</b> = judging by looks, it's <b>about to</b> happen: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうです。</span> (It looks like it's about to rain: the sky is full of dark clouds, but it hasn't started yet.)<br><b>ようです／みたいです</b> = guessing from clues that it's <b>already</b> happening: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているみたいです。</span> (It seems to be raining: you can hear the rain.)<br><b>らしいです</b> = closer to <b>something you heard from others</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>東京は雨が降っているらしいです。</span> (I hear it's raining in Tokyo: from the news or a friend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives work the same way: <span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう</span> means \"looks delicious\", judged by appearance alone. <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいみたい</span> means \"it seems to be delicious\", for example when you see a long line there every day.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"judging by looks alone, about to...\" uses そう. \"I have clues and I'm guessing it seems...\" uses よう／みたい. \"I heard that...\" uses らしい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, study <span class='gd-jp'>〜らしいです</span> side by side with the two kinds of <span class='gd-jp'>〜そうです</span> (appearance and hearsay). That will complete your toolkit for expressing guesses.</div>","n4-34":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「らしい」 has two meanings. The first is <b>\"I hear that ~ / it seems that ~\"</b>, like telling a friend, \"Hey, I heard Nakamura is changing jobs.\" The news came from somewhere else, and you aren't 100% sure yourself. The second is <b>\"typical of ~ / truly ~-like\"</b>, as in English \"very spring-like\" or \"so manly.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I hear / it seems</span>You heard or saw some information, and based on it you think \"it must be like this.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>中村さんは会社をやめるらしいです。</span>（I hear Nakamura is quitting the company.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店のラーメンはおいしいらしいよ。</span>（I hear the ramen at that place is really good.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは昨日休んだらしいです。</span>（It seems Tanaka took the day off yesterday.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Typical of ~</span>After a noun, it means \"just what you'd expect of this role or season.\" It usually carries a note of praise.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は春らしい天気ですね。</span>（It's such a spring-like day today, isn't it?）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は本当に男らしい人です。</span>（He's really a manly guy.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「らしい」 behaves like an i-adjective (an adjective ending in い, like <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span>), so the negative is <b>らしくない</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はなんだか田中さんらしくないですね。</span>（Tanaka seems a bit unlike himself today.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and i-adjectives</b>: use the plain form (without ます) and attach it directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くらしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないらしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いらしい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Na-adjectives and nouns</b>: drop 「だ」 first, then attach.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>静からしい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨らしい</span></span><br>In the past 「だった」, keep it and don't drop anything:<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったらしい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>With superiors or in formal situations, add 「です」 to make it polite. With friends, 「らしい」 alone is fine.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't leave 「だ」 after na-adjectives and nouns: say <span class='gd-jp'>静からしい</span>, not 静かだらしい.<br>・The ます form can't connect directly to らしい: say <span class='gd-jp'>行くらしいです</span>, not 行きますらしい.<br>・The same word can have two meanings: <span class='gd-jp'>あの人は学生らしい。</span> can mean \"That person seems to be a student.\" <span class='gd-jp'>本当に学生らしい服ですね。</span> means \"Those are really student-like clothes.\" Judge by context. If a degree word like 「本当に」 or 「とても」 comes before it, it's most likely the second meaning.<br>・The second meaning is positive: \"fits what it should be.\" Calling a child <span class='gd-jp'>子供らしい</span> praises their innocence. To say an adult is childish, Japanese uses <span class='gd-jp'>子供っぽい</span>, not 子供らしい.<br>・The first meaning is for things you heard and haven't confirmed yourself. If you saw it with your own eyes and are sure, just say it directly without らしい.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Talking about tomorrow's weather:<br><b>そうです (hearsay)</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです。</span>（According to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow.）There's a clear source, and you're just passing it along as is.<br><b>らしいです</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨らしいです。</span>（It seems it'll rain tomorrow.）It's still something you heard, but the source is vaguer, like \"everyone seems to be saying so,\" and it adds a bit of your own judgment, so it sounds less certain.<br><b>ようです・みたいです</b>: (looking out the window) <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているみたいです。</span>（It looks like it's raining outside.）This is a judgment you make after seeing or feeling something yourself.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here's another example people often mix up. There's a piece of cake on the table:<br><span class='gd-jp'>おいしそう！</span>（It looks so good!）This is an impression from what your eyes see, using 「そうです (appearance)」.<br><span class='gd-jp'>このケーキ、おいしいらしいよ。</span>（I hear this cake is really good.）This is something you heard from someone else.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if the information is \"what someone else said,\" use らしい or そうです (hearsay). Use そうです to relay it exactly as heard, and らしい when the source is vaguer and you add a bit of your own guess. If it's \"what you saw or felt yourself,\" use ようです, みたいです, or ～そう (appearance).</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Review n4-32 「～そうです (hearsay)」 and n4-33 「～ようです・～みたいです」 together. Once you can separate \"heard from others\" from \"seen yourself,\" you'll rarely get these conjecture sentences wrong.</div>","n4-35":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜はずです」 means <b>\"it should be ~ / it must be ~ (as far as logic goes)\"</b>. You have grounds for it, such as having seen the timetable, having heard it from the person, or knowing he's a doctor. From those grounds you draw a conclusion you feel confident about, and that's when you use はず. It's the \"should\" in \"He gets off work at six, so he <b>should</b> be home by now.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Inference with grounds</span>First there's a \"because ~,\" then you conclude \"so it should be ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは6時に会社を出たから、もう家に着いたはずです。</span>（Mr. Tanaka left the company at six, so he should be home by now.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>木村さんは医者だから、薬に詳しいはずです。</span>（Mr. Kimura is a doctor, so he should know a lot about medicine.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No wonder</span>When you see the cause and suddenly understand, 「〜はずだ」 is often used.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓が開いている。寒いはずだ。</span>（The window is open. No wonder it's so cold.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches the same way as when a noun follows:<br><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くはず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないはず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったはず</span></span><br><b>I-adjectives</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いはず</span></span><br><b>Na-adjectives</b>: add 「な」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなはず</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休みのはず</span></span><br>For the past, na-adjectives and nouns use 「だった」: <span class='gd-jp'>学生だったはず</span>.<br>With friends say 「はずだ」; with teachers and customers say 「はずです」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>はずがない</span>\"There's no way ~.\" A strong negation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人がうそをつくはずがありません。</span>（There's no way that person would lie.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>はずだった</span>\"It was supposed to ~,\" but it didn't happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車は10時に着くはずだったのに、30分も遅れた。</span>（The train was supposed to arrive at ten, but it was thirty minutes late.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>はずなのに</span>\"It should be ~, so how come…?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>鍵はかばんに入れたはずなのに、ない。</span>（I'm sure I put the key in my bag, but it's not there.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English \"should\" has two meanings: \"He should be there by now\" is a guess, and \"You should go to bed early\" is advice. <b>はず covers only the guess</b>. To give advice, use <span class='gd-jp'>早く寝たほうがいいですよ</span>. If you say <span class='gd-jp'>早く寝るはずです</span>, it means \"by all logic, you'll go to bed early.\"<br>・Nouns need の and na-adjectives need な: say <span class='gd-jp'>休みのはず</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>静かなはず</span>, not 「休みはず」 or 「学生だはず」.<br>・Don't use はず for things you've decided to do yourself. In English you might say \"I should go tomorrow,\" but in Japanese say <span class='gd-jp'>明日は行くつもりです</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>行くと思います</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>行くはずです</span> sounds like \"according to the schedule, I'm going to go.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ないはずです</span> means \"he shouldn't be coming,\" a guess. <span class='gd-jp'>来るはずがありません</span> means \"there's no way he'd come,\" which is much stronger.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Waiting for Mr. Tanaka:<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るはずです。</span>（He said he'd come, so he should come: you have grounds and are confident.）<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るでしょう。</span>（He'll probably come: an ordinary guess.）<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るかもしれません。</span>（He might come, or he might not: about fifty-fifty.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if you can state your grounds (\"because ~, so…\") and feel confident, use はず. If you're just guessing offhand, use でしょう. If you're not very sure, use かもしれません.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>At N3 you'll learn 「べき」 (should do ~), which is exactly the other meaning of English \"should.\" Memorize はず and べき separately and you'll stop mixing them up.</div>","n4-54":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>The ば form means <b>“if ~, then ~”</b>, with the sense of “as long as ~”. <span class='gd-jp'>安ければ買います</span> = As long as it's cheap, I'll buy it. The emphasis is on the <b>condition</b> in the first half: once it's met, the result follows.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As long as ~</span>Once a certain condition is met, a certain result follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>安ければ買います。</span>(If it's cheap, I'll buy it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>急げば間に合いますよ。</span>(If you hurry, you'll make it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Universal truths</span>Nature and common sense, things that happen “every time.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春になれば、桜が咲きます。</span>(When spring comes, the cherry blossoms bloom.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>If not ~</span>The negative version, often used as a warning: “if you don't do it, you're in trouble.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>急がなければ、間に合いません。</span>(If you don't hurry, you won't make it.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「え」-row sound of the same row, then add 「ば」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>行けば</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲めば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買えば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待てば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰れば</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「れば」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べれば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きれば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見れば</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すれば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来れば</span>(くれば)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>“If not ~”</b>: first make the ない form, then drop the 「い」 of 「ない」 and add 「ければ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなければ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べなければ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add 「ければ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安ければ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒ければ</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: no ば here, just add 「なら」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生なら</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来れば</span> is read <b>くれば</b>, not きれば or これば.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>よければ</span>; there is no 「いければ」. Example: <span class='gd-jp'>よければ、どうぞ。</span> (If you don't mind, please go ahead.)<br>・Group 1 doesn't just add れば: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行けば</span>, not 行くれば.<br>・When the first half is an <b>action</b> (go, arrive, eat…) and the second half is a request like “please ~” or a suggestion like “let's ~”, ば sounds odd. Switch to たら:<br>×駅に着けば、電話してください。<br>○<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話してください。</span> (Call me once you get to the station.)<br>If the first half is a <b>state</b> (ある, いる, できる, adjectives), it's fine: <span class='gd-jp'>時間があれば、来てください。</span> (If you have time, please come.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Japanese has four kinds of “if.” Let's compare them in one situation, going to the station:<br><b>ば</b> = as long as ~: <span class='gd-jp'>駅まで歩けば、10分で着きます。</span> (If you walk to the station, you'll be there in 10 minutes.)<br><b>たら</b> = after ~; the second half can be a request or an invitation: <span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話してください。</span> (Call me once you get to the station.)<br><b>と</b> = whenever ~, it always happens; think giving directions or a machine's reaction: <span class='gd-jp'>この道をまっすぐ行くと、駅があります。</span> (If you go straight down this road, you'll find the station.)<br><b>なら</b> = picks up what the other person said and gives advice; the event hasn't happened yet: <span class='gd-jp'>駅に行くなら、この道が近いですよ。</span> (If you're going to the station, this road is shorter.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ば for “as long as ~”; use たら for “after ~” or when the second half is a request or invitation; use と for “whenever ~, it always happens”; use なら for “if you say you're going to ~.” When in doubt, start with たら. It's the least likely to be wrong.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「いい」 afterward to mean “it'd be good if ~.” It's very common for asking someone's advice: <span class='gd-jp'>どうすればいいですか。</span> (What should I do?)<br>Add 「なりません」 after 「なければ」 and you get “must ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は早く起きなければなりません。</span> (I have to get up early tomorrow.)</div>","n4-55":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one sentence</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たら」 means <b>“if ~” or “when ~ / once ~”</b>. You use it for “if I have money, I want to travel” and “I'll call you when I get home.” It's the most common everyday way to express a condition. It also has one special use: <b>“I did ~ and found that ~.”</b></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: if you know the た form, you know たら</div><div class='gd-sec'>Make the た form first, then just add 「ら」. The verb た form is made just like the て form, except that て becomes た and で becomes だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったら</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだら</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたら</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たら</span>(きたら)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives and nouns work too. Same idea: put them in the past form, then add ら:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かったら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暇</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暇だったら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったら</span></span><br>Negatives are the same: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなかったら</span></span> (if I don't go)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>If ~</span>It may or may not happen; it's just a hypothesis.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お金があったら、旅行したい。</span>(If I had money, I'd want to travel.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日雨だったら、行きません。</span>(If it rains tomorrow, I won't go.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Once ~, then</span>Something that will definitely happen; you're saying what you'll do “when the time comes.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ったら、電話します。</span>(I'll call you when I get home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、連絡してください。</span>(Please contact me when you get to the station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Found that ~</span>After doing something, you discover something unexpected. Here the second half must be in the <b>past tense</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けたら、猫がいた。</span>(When I opened the window, there was a cat.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ったら、母が来ていました。</span>(When I got home, my mom had come over.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With たら, the first half always happens <b>first and the second half after it</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行ったら、ガイドブックを買います。</span> means “I'll buy a guidebook once I'm in Japan.” If you mean “I'll buy one before I leave,” you need なら (see below).<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来たら</span> is read <b>きたら</b>, not こたら.<br>・Don't use たら to tell a series of things you did in the past. “I went home and ate” is <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰って、ご飯を食べました。</span> 「たら + past tense」 is specifically for “and then I ran into / discovered something,” so putting a deliberate action of your own in the second half sounds strange.<br>・To make the “if” clearer, you can put <span class='gd-jp'>もし</span> in front: <span class='gd-jp'>もし雨だったら</span>. But you can't add もし to the “once ~” kind of thing that will surely happen: <span class='gd-jp'>もし家に帰ったら</span> sounds like you're not sure you'll make it home.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>At N4 you learn four conditionals: たら, ば, と, なら. Let's look at them in the same situation:<br><b>たら</b> = the all-purpose one; the second half can be a request, a wish, or a plan: <span class='gd-jp'>春になったら、日本へ行きたい。</span> (When spring comes, I want to go to Japan.)<br><b>と</b> = it always happens this way, like a natural phenomenon: <span class='gd-jp'>春になると、桜が咲きます。</span> (When spring comes, the cherry blossoms bloom.)<br><b>ば</b> = stresses “as long as this condition is met, ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>春になれば、暖かくなります。</span> (Once spring comes, it gets warm.)<br><b>なら</b> = follows what the other person said and gives advice, and <b>you can talk about the later event first</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くなら、春がいいですよ。</span> (If you're going to Japan, spring is a good time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The difference is clearest when you ask someone to do something: <span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話してください。</span> sounds natural. If you switch to と or ば, as in 「駅に着くと」 or 「駅に着けば」, and then add 「電話してください」, Japanese speakers will find it wrong.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: when you can't decide, use たら. It works in most cases. The exceptions: for “it's like that every time,” と is more common; for “I heard the other person is going to do something, so here's some advice,” use なら.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「いいです」 afterward and it becomes advice: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に聞いたらいいですよ。</span> (You should just ask your teacher.)</div>","n4-56":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「なら」 is like English <b>“if it's about X…” or “in that case…”</b>. Say a friend tells you, “I want to go to a hot spring.” You pick up on that and reply, “If it's hot springs you want, Hakone is nice.” You first take up what the other person brought up, then give your suggestion or thought. That's the most common use of なら.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Replying with advice</span>The other person mentions something, and you give an opinion about it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：週末、温泉に行きたいな。</span> (I really want to go to a hot spring this weekend.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>B：温泉なら、箱根がいいよ。</span> (If it's hot springs, Hakone is nice.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Assume first, then state your plan</span>“If that's the case, then I'll…”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あなたが行くなら、私も行きます。</span> (If you're going, I'll go too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家賃が予算内なら、契約したいです。</span> (If the rent is within my budget, I'd like to sign the contract.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないなら</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いなら</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: drop 「だ」 first, then attach. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなら</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本語</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本語なら</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + なら</span>It means “if we're talking about X,” narrowing the topic down to that one thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーなら、駅前の店がおいしいですよ。</span> (If it's coffee, the shop in front of the station is really good.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それなら</span>It goes at the start of a sentence and means “in that case.” In casual speech people often say just 「なら」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：明日は忙しいです。</span> (I'm busy tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>B：それなら、あさってにしましょう。</span> (In that case, let's make it the day after tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With nouns and な-adjectives, <b>don't add だ</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>学生なら</span>, not 学生だなら.<br>・The thing after なら <b>can happen before the thing in front of it</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>京都へ行くなら、ホテルを早く予約したほうがいいですよ。</span> (If you're going to Kyoto, you'd better book a hotel early.) Booking the hotel happens before the trip. This is exactly the opposite of たら.<br>・What follows なら is mostly advice, a request, your own plan or a judgment. For things that always happen, like “when spring comes, flowers bloom,” don't use なら; use と: <span class='gd-jp'>春になると、桜が咲きます。</span> (When spring comes, the cherry blossoms bloom.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>なら and たら can both be translated as “if,” so they're the easiest to mix up. The difference is <b>when the second action happens</b>. Take the same trip to Japan:<br><b>なら</b> = you do it before going:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くなら、日本語を勉強しておいたほうがいいよ。</span> (If you're going to Japan, you'd better study some Japanese first.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たら</b> = you do it after arriving:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行ったら、ラーメンを食べたい。</span> (When I get to Japan, I want to eat ramen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you changed the first sentence to 「行ったら」, it would mean “start studying Japanese once you've arrived in Japan,” which isn't what you meant. Conversely, “call me when you get to the station” can only happen after you arrive, so use たら: <span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話してください。</span> (Please call me when you get to the station.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use なら for “not done yet, here's advice,” and たら for “it happens after you've done it or arrived.” When the other person has just mentioned something and you reply with an opinion, it's almost always なら.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Japanese has four main ways to say “if”: と, ば, たら and なら. Once you've learned なら, try comparing it with <span class='gd-jp'>〜ば</span> (n4-54) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜と</span> (n4-57) to see when each one fits.</div>","n4-59":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The passive form (受身形) is like English <b>“be + past participle”</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に褒められた</span> means “I was praised by the teacher.” But Japanese uses the passive much more than English does, and it often carries a feeling of <b>“this happened to me and it was a nuisance or bad luck”</b>, like English “I got rained on” or “I had my cake eaten.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (五段 verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the same row's 「あ」-column sound, then add 「れる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かれる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>踏む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>踏まれる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>叱る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>叱られる</span></span><br>Watch out for verbs ending in 「う」: the う doesn't become あ, it becomes <b>わ</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → change う to わ → <span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>誘う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>誘われる</span></span><br>You can also think from the ます form: change the 「き」 of <span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> to 「か」, then add 「れます」 → <span class='gd-jp'>書かれます</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (一段 verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「られる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>される</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span> (こられる)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once you've formed 「〜れる／〜られる」, keep conjugating it like a Group 2 verb: <span class='gd-jp'>叱られます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>叱られた</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>叱られて</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The three most common passives</div><div class='gd-sec'>In every type, <b>the person who does the action takes 「に」</b>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something was done to me</span>(私は) person に + passive. This is the closest to the English passive, and it works for both good and bad things.<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生が私を褒めた。</span> (The teacher praised me.) → turned into the passive:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は先生に褒められた。</span> (I was praised by the teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母に叱られました。</span> (I was scolded by my mother.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something of mine was affected</span>(私は) person に + thing を + passive. Japanese likes to make the affected person the subject, and the thing takes 「を」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車で足を踏まれた。</span> (I had my foot stepped on on the train.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟にケーキを食べられた。</span> (My little brother ate my cake on me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Being put out by something</span>What someone else did wasn't really about you, but you ended up suffering from it. Even actions like “rain” and “cry” can become passive. English wouldn't normally use a passive for these.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨に降られて、びしょびしょになった。</span> (I got rained on and ended up soaked.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜中に子供に泣かれて、寝られなかった。</span> (The child cried in the middle of the night, and I couldn't sleep.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The passive of <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span>, not 「言あれる」 or 「言られる」. <span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>使う</span> work the same way, with わ.<br>・The person doing the action takes 「に」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>先生に褒められた</span>, not 「先生が褒められた」, which would mean “the teacher was praised.”<br>・In English you can say “my foot got stepped on,” but don't translate it word for word as 私の足が踏まれた; the natural way is <span class='gd-jp'>足を踏まれた</span>.<br>・English “got + past participle” often suggests something bad, but the Japanese passive works for good things too: <span class='gd-jp'>褒められた</span> (was praised) and <span class='gd-jp'>誘われた</span> (was invited) are both very common.<br>・When you see a passive sentence, ask yourself, “Is the subject the one who is affected, or even put out?” That will help you catch the nuance.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Passive (受身形) vs potential (可能形)</b>: for Group 2 verbs and 来る, the two forms look exactly the same.<br><span class='gd-jp'>犬にパンを食べられた。</span> (The dog ate my bread: passive)<br><span class='gd-jp'>このパンはまだ食べられる。</span> (This bread is still edible: potential)<br>If there's a 「person に」 and the sentence is about who had something done to them, it's the passive. If it's about “can or can't,” it's the potential.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜られる vs 〜てもらう</b>: both can describe someone coming to see you.<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に来られて困った。</span> (A friend showed up, and it put me in a bind.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に来てもらって助かった。</span> (A friend came to help, and it was a real lifesaver.)<br>The passive often carries a feeling of “I was bothered.” 〜てもらう means “I received the other person's help” and carries gratitude.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: when you're thinking “this happened to me, what bad luck,” use 〜れる／〜られる. When you're thinking “someone helped me, how nice,” use 〜てもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to study next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next is the causative form (making someone do something): <span class='gd-jp'>書かせる</span>. Beyond that is the causative-passive (being made to do something): <span class='gd-jp'>母に野菜を食べさせられた。</span> (I was made to eat vegetables by my mother.) Also, the same 〜れる／〜られる can be used to show respect to someone of higher status, as in <span class='gd-jp'>先生が書かれた本</span> (the book the teacher wrote). You'll meet that again when you learn honorific forms.</div>","n4-62":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Honorific language (尊敬語) is what you use when talking about <b>what the other person, or someone senior to you, is doing</b>. You swap the action for a more respectful expression. It's like turning “Are you coming?” into “Will you be joining us?”, or “What do you want?” into “What would you like?”. The action is the same; only the wording gets more respectful.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use it when the person doing the action is someone you should respect, such as a teacher, boss, customer, or elder. You can use it when speaking to them directly, or when mentioning them in front of other people.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生はもうお帰りになりました。</span>(The teacher has already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は何をなさいますか。</span>(What will you be doing this weekend?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: think in two steps</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Step 1: Check whether the verb has its own special honorific form</b><br>Very common verbs have their own honorific forms. If one exists, you usually use it first:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いる・行く・来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いらっしゃる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おっしゃる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる・飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>召し上がる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご覧になる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なさる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>くれる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>くださる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご存じだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長は明日大阪へいらっしゃいます。</span>(The president will go to Osaka tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本、もうご覧になりましたか。</span>(Have you already looked at this book?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Step 2: If there's no special form, use “お + ます-stem + になる”</b><br>Change the verb to its ます form, drop ます, then add お in front and になる at the end.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待ちます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>お待ちになる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お帰りになる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お書きになる</span></span><br>For “Chinese-character noun + する” verbs, ご is usually used: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>利用する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご利用になる</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長は何時にお戻りになりますか。</span>(What time will the manager be back?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs like <b>見る・言う・する・いる・来る</b> must use their special forms; don't force the formula on them. A few verbs, such as <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, accept both: <span class='gd-jp'>何を召し上がりますか。</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>何をお飲みになりますか。</span> (What would you like to drink?) both sound natural.</div><div class='gd-sec'>There is also a lighter honorific form with 「れる・られる」 (for example <span class='gd-jp'>社長が帰られました</span>), which is covered separately in n4-74.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>In shops</span><span class='gd-jp'>いらっしゃいませ。</span> (Welcome!) This actually comes from いらっしゃる.<br><span class='gd-tag'>Please ~</span>お + ます-stem + ください: <span class='gd-jp'>少々お待ちください。</span> (Please wait a moment.)<br><span class='gd-tag'>Asking if they know something</span><span class='gd-jp'>このお店、ご存じですか。</span> (Do you know this shop?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Honorific language <b>must never be used about yourself</b>. To say “I'm going tomorrow,” you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>私がいらっしゃいます</span>. Use plain polite speech or humble language: <span class='gd-jp'>私が参ります</span>.<br>・Don't force the formula on these verbs: you can't say お見になる、お言いになる、おしになる. Say <span class='gd-jp'>ご覧になる</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>おっしゃる</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>なさる</span>.<br>・The ます forms are easy to get wrong: they are <span class='gd-jp'>いらっしゃいます</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>おっしゃいます</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>なさいます</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>くださいます</span>, not いらっしゃります or おっしゃります.<br>・When you talk to outside customers about your own boss at your company, don't use honorifics; lower your own company's people. For example, if a customer calls asking for the manager, reply <span class='gd-jp'>田中は今おりません。</span> and don't say “部長はいらっしゃいません”.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Humble language (n4-63) is also keigo, but it goes the opposite way: honorific language <b>raises the other person</b>, while humble language <b>lowers yourself</b>. Look at the same conversation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>A：この資料、ご覧になりましたか。</span> (Have you looked at this document? The other person looks, so honorific.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>B：はい、拝見しました。</span> (Yes, I've looked at it. I look, so humble.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>A：何を召し上がりますか。</span> (What would you like to eat?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>B：じゃ、ケーキをいただきます。</span> (Then I'll have the cake.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: first ask <b>who is doing the action</b>. If it's the other person or a senior, use honorific language. If it's you (or someone on your side), use humble language.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn humble language (n4-63). Memorizing the two together is the best way to avoid mixing them up. Then look at the honorific use of 「れる・られる」 (n4-74).</div>","n4-63":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Humble language (謙譲語) means <b>talking about your own actions in a lowered way, so the other person naturally comes across as higher</b>. English does this a little too: “Allow me to carry that for you,” “I'd be happy to assist.” Japanese humble language is the same idea, but it's used far more often and far more systematically.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use it with customers, teachers, bosses, and others you should respect when talking about <b>what you yourself are going to do</b>. In part-time jobs, at work, or when writing to a teacher, you'll run into it almost every day.</div><div class='gd-h'>Method 1: お + verb + します</div><div class='gd-sec'>Drop ます from the ます form, add 「お」 in front and 「します」 at the end.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>持ちます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>お持ちします</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待ちます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お待ちします</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>送ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お送りします</span></span><br>For Chinese-character nouns like 「案内」 and 「連絡」, you usually add 「ご」 in front:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>案内します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご案内します</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>連絡します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ご連絡します</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>荷物、お持ちします。</span>(Let me carry your luggage.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅までお送りします。</span>(I'll see you to the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あとでご連絡します。</span>(I'll contact you later.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>This pattern only works for actions <b>done for the other person, or involving them</b>. Eating or sleeping by yourself has nothing to do with them, so you can't apply it there.</div><div class='gd-h'>Method 2: Swap in a special verb</div><div class='gd-sec'>Some common verbs have their own humble versions. You <b>replace the verb entirely</b> and don't use お～します. For N4, memorize these:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く・来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>参ります</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おります</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>申します</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いたします</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる・飲む・もらう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いただきます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>拝見します</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'>to ask, to visit the other person's place → <span class='gd-jp'>伺います</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>存じております</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>お目にかかります</span></span><br>The 「いただきます」 you say before a meal is actually this humble いただく.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はじめまして、林と申します。</span>(Nice to meet you, my name is Lin.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>資料、拝見しました。</span>(I've looked at the materials.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>では、お先に失礼いたします。</span>(Well then, if you'll excuse me, I'll leave first.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>When talking to outsiders about the actions of <b>your own family</b>, you also use humble language, because family counts as “your side.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は今、出かけております。</span>(My father is out right now.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Humble language is only for <b>yourself (and your own side)</b>. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>いただいてください</span> to invite your teacher to eat is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>召し上がってください</span>. And to ask whether your teacher is going, you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>先生も参りますか</span>; say <span class='gd-jp'>先生もいらっしゃいますか</span>.<br>・If a verb has its own special version, use that: don't say お見します, say <span class='gd-jp'>拝見します</span>; don't say お行きします, say <span class='gd-jp'>参ります</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>伺います</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>お待ちします</span> means “I'll wait for you,” while <span class='gd-jp'>お待ちください</span> means “please wait.” The subject is different. When a shop clerk says <span class='gd-jp'>少々お待ちください</span>, they're asking you to wait; when they say <span class='gd-jp'>お待ちしております</span>, they're the ones waiting for you.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>参ります</span> just makes “go, come” polite; <span class='gd-jp'>伺います</span> means “go to the other person's place.” <span class='gd-jp'>来週、大阪へ参ります。</span> (I'm going to Osaka next week.) / <span class='gd-jp'>明日、先生のお宅に伺います。</span> (Tomorrow I'll visit the teacher's home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>The form most easily confused with this is <b>honorific language</b>. For the same event, if the other person is the subject, use honorific language (raising them); if you are the subject, use humble language (lowering yourself).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生も召し上がりますか。</span>(Will you have some too, teacher? The other person eats, so honorific.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、私もいただきます。</span>(Yes, I'll have some too. I eat, so humble.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生、傘をお持ちになりましたか。</span>(Teacher, did you bring an umbrella? The other person brings it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>傘、お持ちします。</span>(Let me carry your umbrella. I carry it.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: first ask “Who is doing this action?” If the other person: honorific (お～になる、いらっしゃる、召し上がる). If you: humble (お～します、参ります、いただきます).</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn 「〜させていただきます」, a very humble way of saying “I'm going to do ~” (literally “I receive your permission to do ~”), for example <span class='gd-jp'>本日は休ませていただきます。</span> (Please allow me to take today off.) You'll hear it every day at part-time jobs and at work.</div>","n4-60":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The causative form is how Japanese says <b>“make someone do…”</b> or <b>“let someone do…”</b>. Think “Mom makes her kid eat vegetables” or “The manager lets the part-timer leave early.” In both cases Japanese turns the verb into 「〜せる／〜さ せる」. It can mean <b>forcing someone to do it</b>, or <b>letting someone do what they want to do (permission)</b>. Context tells you which.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Force</span>You tell someone to do it, whether they want to or not.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母は子供に野菜を食べさせます。</span>(Mom makes her kid eat vegetables.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Permission</span>They want to do it, and you let them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供が遊びたがっていたので、公園で遊ばせました。</span>(My kid really wanted to play, so I let him play at the park.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Causing a feeling</span>Making someone worry or making someone happy also uses the causative.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親を心配させたくない。</span>(I don’t want to make my parents worry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to its 「あ」-row equivalent, then add 「せる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → く becomes か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かせる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲ませる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らせる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たせる</span></span><br>Verbs ending in 「う」 change to <b>わ</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買わせる</span></span><br>Tip: it uses the same sound as the ない form. Take <span class='gd-jp'>書かない</span>, drop ない, add せる, and you get <span class='gd-jp'>書かせる</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「させる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見させる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きさせる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>させる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来させる</span> (こさせる)</span><br>Verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>残業する</span> work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強させる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once formed, it is just an ordinary Group 2 verb and conjugates as usual: <span class='gd-jp'>書かせます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書かせた</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書かせない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The person being told: に or を?</div><div class='gd-sec'>The pattern looks like this: <b>the person giving the order</b>は　<b>the person doing it</b>に／を　〜させる<br>How to choose: first check whether the sentence already has <b>another 「を」</b> (what is eaten, what is written).</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>There is another を</span>The person doing the action can only take <b>に</b>. Japanese doesn’t like two 「を」 in one sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は生徒に作文を書かせます。</span>(The teacher makes the students write an essay.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供に日本語を勉強させています。</span>(I’m having my child study Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No other を</span>Either <b>を</b> or <b>に</b> works. With を, the sense of force is stronger.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は学生を立たせました。</span>(The teacher made the student stand up.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長は毎日私を残業させます。</span>(My department head makes me work overtime every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長は毎日私に残業させます。</span>(My department head makes me work overtime every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>させてください</span>“Please let me…”: you want to do it and you’re asking for permission.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>少し休ませてください。</span>(Please let me rest for a bit.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私にやらせてください。</span>(Let me do it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>待たせる</span>“Make someone wait”: extremely common in apologies.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お待たせしました。</span>(Sorry to keep you waiting.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>待たせてごめんね。</span>(Sorry to keep you waiting.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs ending in 「う」 change to <b>わ</b>: it’s <span class='gd-jp'>買わせる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>言わせる</span>, not 「買あせる」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> are Group 1: it’s <span class='gd-jp'>帰らせる</span>, not 「帰させる」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来させる</span> is read <b>こさせる</b>, not きさせる.<br>・The causative is a top-down form, so <b>don’t use it to direct someone senior to you</b>. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>先生に説明させる</span> for “I asked the teacher to explain” sounds very rude. Say <span class='gd-jp'>先生に説明してもらう</span> instead (or, more politely, <span class='gd-jp'>説明していただく</span>).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>させる vs てもらう</b>: in English, “have a friend take my photo” could mean either, but Japanese treats them very differently.<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に写真を撮らせた。</span> (I made my friend take a photo: it sounds like you’re ordering them around.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に写真を撮ってもらった。</span> (I asked my friend to take a photo for me: they did you a favor, and it carries thanks.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>させる vs される (passive)</b>: one syllable apart, completely opposite directions.<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達を1時間待たせた。</span> (I kept my friend waiting for an hour: it’s my doing.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>友達に1時間待たされた。</span> (My friend kept me waiting for an hour: I’m the one who lost out.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: 「<b>せ</b>」 means I make someone else do something; 「<b>れ</b>」 means something is done to me by someone else; and to ask someone for a favor, use 「てもらう」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Causative + passive = being forced to do something (the causative-passive): <span class='gd-jp'>母に野菜を食べさせられた。</span> (I was made to eat vegetables by my mom.)<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>〜させていただきます</span>, which you hear all the time in customer service, comes from the causative too.</div>","n4-61":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The causative-passive means <b>“be made to do…”</b> or <b>“be sent off to do…”</b>. The key point: <b>you are the one doing the action, but you don’t want to</b>, because someone else is forcing you. Think “As a kid, I was made to eat green peppers by my mom”: you’re the one eating, Mom is the one forcing, and there’s a hint of complaint in the tone.</div><div class='gd-h'>Where it comes from</div><div class='gd-sec'>The name is long, but it is really two things stacked together: the <b>causative</b> (make someone do something) + the <b>passive</b> (be ~ed).<br>First, from Mom’s point of view:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母は私にピーマンを食べさせた。</span>(Mom made me eat green peppers.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Tell it from “my” point of view and it becomes the causative-passive:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は母にピーマンを食べさせられた。</span>(I was made to eat green peppers by my mom.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The person being forced (me) takes <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>が</span>, and the person forcing you takes <b><span class='gd-jp'>に</span></b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: check the verb group first</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add 「させられる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見させられる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to its 「あ」-row equivalent, then add 「される」. This is the version Japanese people use most.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → く becomes か → <span class='gd-jp'>行かされる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まされる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たされる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → う becomes わ → <span class='gd-jp'>買わされる</span></span><br>Adding 「せられる」 is also correct (<span class='gd-jp'>行かせられる</span>), just longer and less common.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>させられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来させられる</span> (こさせられる)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>For the polite form, replace the final る with ます: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待たされる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たされます</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅で30分も待たされた。</span>(I was kept waiting at the station for a whole 30 minutes.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長にお酒を飲まされました。</span>(My department head made me drink alcohol.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供の頃、毎日ピアノを練習させられた。</span>(As a kid, I was made to practice piano every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Group 1 verbs ending in 「す」 <b>can’t</b> use される: <span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>話させられる</span>, not 話さされる.<br>・Verbs ending in 「う」 change to 「わ」, not 「あ」: it’s <span class='gd-jp'>買わされる</span>, not 買あされる.<br>・The causative-passive of <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> is read <b>こさせられる</b>, not きさせられる.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べさせる</span> means “make someone else eat,” while <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせられる</span> means “be made to eat.” Without 「られる」 the meaning flips completely.<br>・It carries a feeling of reluctance, so don’t use it for happy things. To say a senior colleague treated you to a meal, say <span class='gd-jp'>先輩におごってもらった</span>. If you said <span class='gd-jp'>先輩におごらされた</span>, it would mean “I was forced to pay for my senior.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Passive</b> = the action is done by <b>the other person</b>, and you’re on the receiving end: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に叱られた。</span> (I was scolded by the teacher: the teacher does the scolding.)<br><b>Causative-passive</b> = the action is done by <b>you</b>, but you’re forced into it: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に立たされた。</span> (I was made to stand by the teacher: I’m the one standing.)<br><b>てもらう</b> = the other person does something for you, and you’re grateful: <span class='gd-jp'>先生に作文を直してもらった。</span> (The teacher corrected my essay for me.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: ask yourself, “Who is doing this action?” If the other person does it to you, it’s the passive. If you do it unwillingly, it’s the causative-passive. If the other person helps you and you’re grateful, use てもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To say “please allow me to…” politely, use the te-form of the causative plus <span class='gd-jp'>いただきます</span> (<span class='gd-jp'>〜させていただきます</span>): <span class='gd-jp'>発表する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>発表させる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>発表させていただきます。</span> (Allow me to give the presentation.)</div>","n4-7":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「ので」 means <b>“because ~, so ~”</b>. Like 「から」, it gives a reason. The difference is that 「ので」 sounds more <b>polite, as if you're explaining the situation</b>. English “because” is closer to から, while “since” or “as” is closer to ので. When you give a reason to a teacher, a boss, or a customer, ので is the safest choice.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving a reason politely</span>Most common at work, in class, and when talking with people you don't know well.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>用事があるので、お先に失礼します。</span>(I have something to do, so I'll head out first.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道が混んでいたので、遅れました。</span>(The roads were crowded, so I was late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Making a request</span>Give the reason first, then ask for help. It sounds very polite.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒いので、窓を閉めてください。</span>(It's cold, so please close the window.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Declining indirectly</span>Japanese speakers often give only the reason and leave the sentence unfinished. The other person gets the message.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日はちょっと用事があるので…。</span>(I have something on tomorrow, so…)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くので</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったので</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>痛い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>痛いので</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: add 「な」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなので</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨なので</span></span><br>For something in the past, use 「だった」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったので</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>For a more formal tone, you can also attach it after ます or です. You hear this a lot in shops and workplaces:<br><span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っていますので、お気をつけください。</span>(It's raining, so please be careful.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives can't take だ directly: <span class='gd-jp'>雨だので</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨なので</span>.<br>・い-adjectives are the opposite: <b>no な</b>. It's <span class='gd-jp'>痛いので</span>, not 痛いなので.<br>・When you answer 「Why?」, you can end the sentence with <span class='gd-jp'>電車が遅れたからです</span>, but you <b>can't</b> say 遅れたのでです.<br>・ので doesn't suit harsh commands. 「うるさいので、黙れ」 sounds odd. Use から for that.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>から vs ので</b>: the same situation, taking a day off with a fever.<br><span class='gd-jp'>熱があるから、休む。</span>(I have a fever, so I'm taking a day off. Said to a friend, more direct.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>熱があるので、休ませてください。</span>(Because I have a fever, please let me rest. Said to a boss, more polite.)<br>For scolding or warning, only から works: <span class='gd-jp'>危ないから、触るな！</span>(It's dangerous, don't touch it!)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ので vs のに</b>: only one syllable apart, but the meaning is very different.<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだので、治った。</span>(I took medicine, so I got better.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだのに、治らない。</span>(Even though I took medicine, I'm not getting better.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: to give a reason politely, use ので. To stress your point, sound forceful, or give an order, use から. For “even though ~”, it's のに.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you have more than one reason, you can list them one by one with 「し」: <span class='gd-jp'>雨も降っているし、寒いし、今日は出かけない。</span>(It's raining, and it's cold too, so I'm not going out today.)</div>","n4-5":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜やすい」 means <b>“easy to ~”</b>, and 「〜にくい」 means <b>“hard to ~”</b>. Japanese uses them for things like “this pen is easy to write with” or “this name is hard to remember.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into the ます form, drop 「ます」, then add やすい or にくい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>書きやすい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べにくい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話しやすい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>覚える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>覚えにくい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しやすい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来やすい</span>（きやすい）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once attached, the whole word <b>works as an い-adjective</b> and conjugates just like 「おいしい」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書きやすい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書きやすかった</span></span> (it was easy to write)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>わかりやすい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>わかりやすくて</span></span> (easy to understand, and…)<br>You can also put it right before a noun: <span class='gd-jp'>使いやすいアプリ</span> (an easy-to-use app)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Easy / hard to do</span>How smoothly things go when a person does the action.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この靴は軽くて歩きやすいです。</span>(These shoes are light and easy to walk in.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生の説明はわかりやすいです。</span>(The teacher's explanations are easy to understand.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この魚は骨が多くて食べにくいです。</span>(This fish has a lot of bones, so it's a hassle to eat.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Likely / unlikely to happen</span>This isn't about a person doing something on purpose. It's about things or people that <b>tend to end up that way</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>白い服は汚れやすいです。</span>(White clothes get dirty easily.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冬は風邪をひきやすいです。</span>(You catch colds easily in winter.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このコップは割れにくいです。</span>(This cup doesn't break easily.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach it to the <b>ます form minus ます</b>, not the dictionary form. It's <span class='gd-jp'>書きやすい</span>, not 書くやすい.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べやすい</span> does not mean “tasty”! It means “easy to eat, convenient to eat.” For “delicious,” use <span class='gd-jp'>おいしい</span>. In the same way, <span class='gd-jp'>食べにくい</span> means “awkward to eat, a hassle to eat,” not “tastes bad” (that's <span class='gd-jp'>まずい</span>).<br>・To say “hard to write,” Japanese speakers usually just say <span class='gd-jp'>書きにくい</span>. It's more natural than <span class='gd-jp'>書きやすくない</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>割れやすい</span> means “breaks easily.” It describes the nature of the thing, not “easy to break on purpose.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜そうです」 (looks like it will ~) also attaches to the ます form minus ます, so it's easy to mix up with やすい:<br><b>割れそう</b> = judging by its appearance, you <b>guess in the moment</b> that it will break: <span class='gd-jp'>このお皿、薄くて割れそう。</span>(This plate is so thin, it looks like it's going to break.)<br><b>割れやすい</b> = you know it's <b>fragile</b> by nature, as a characteristic: <span class='gd-jp'>このお皿は割れやすいから、気をつけて。</span>(This plate breaks easily, so be careful.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: for “looks like it's about to ~,” use そう. For “inherently prone to ~” or “easy to do,” use やすい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other patterns also take the ます form minus ます and add something, like <span class='gd-jp'>食べすぎる</span> (eat too much) and <span class='gd-jp'>書き方</span> (way of writing). Learn the pattern once and you get all of these together.</div>","n4-10":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たことがあります」 means <b>“have (ever) done ~”</b>. It says whether you <b>have had this experience</b> at some point in your life. “I've been to Japan” is <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行ったことがあります。</span> and “I've never eaten cilantro” is <span class='gd-jp'>パクチーを食べたことがありません。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into its <b>た-form</b> (the plain past tense, the form behind “ate” and “went”), then add <span class='gd-jp'>ことがあります</span>.<br>The た-form is made just like the て-form, except て becomes た and で becomes だ:<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>乗る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>乗った</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add た. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見た</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>した</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span>（きた）</span><br>Then attach the whole phrase: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたことがあります</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Have done</span>Talking about your own experience. You can add a number of times in front.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>着物を着たことがあります。</span>(I've worn a kimono.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>大阪には二回行ったことがあります。</span>(I've been to Osaka twice.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Never done</span>Change あります to <b>ありません</b>. Add 「一度も」 to mean “not even once.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一度も雪を見たことがありません。</span>(I've never seen snow, not even once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone</span>Add か at the end. You don't need to repeat the whole sentence in your answer. あります／ありません is enough.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>馬に乗ったことがありますか。</span>(Have you ever ridden a horse?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、あります。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、ありません。</span>(Yes, I have. / No, I haven't.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual version</span>With friends, use <span class='gd-jp'>ことがある</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>ことがない</span>. In speech, が is often dropped.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>富士山、登ったことある？</span>(Have you climbed Mt. Fuji?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It's about whether you've had the experience at any point in your life, so <b>don't use it for recent things like just now or yesterday</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>さっき食べたことがある</span> is wrong. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>さっき食べた</span>. A long-ago, broad time frame like “as a kid” is fine: <span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ、日本に住んだことがあります。</span> (I lived in Japan when I was a kid.)<br>・<b>Don't use it for things everyone has obviously done</b>. If you say <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べたことがあります</span> for “I've eaten rice,” it sounds very odd to Japanese people, as if eating a meal were some rare experience.<br>・“Have never done” is <span class='gd-jp'>食べたことがありません</span>, not <span class='gd-jp'>食べなかったことがあります</span>. The latter means “there was a time when I didn't eat.”<br>・The verb before it must be in the <b>た-form</b>. With the dictionary form the meaning changes completely. See below.<br>・In <span class='gd-jp'>来たことがあります</span>, 来 is read <b>き</b>, not く or こ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>た-form + ことがあります</b> = have done ~ (experience)<br><span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べたことがあります。</span>(I've eaten natto.)<br><b>Dictionary form + ことがあります</b> = sometimes does ~ (happens occasionally)<br><span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べることがあります。</span>(I sometimes eat natto.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, English “have you eaten?” can mean “have you eaten yet?” In that case Japanese does <b>not</b> use ことがある:<br><span class='gd-jp'>もう食べましたか。</span>(Have you eaten yet?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>食べたことがありますか。</span>(Have you ever eaten it? = Do you have that experience?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say “Have you ever ~?”, use たことがある. If it means “Have you ~ yet?”, use <span class='gd-jp'>もう〜ました</span>. If the verb before it is in the dictionary form, it means “sometimes.”</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Pair it with 「〜てみたい」 (want to try ~). It's great for self-introductions and small talk:<br><span class='gd-jp'>温泉に入ったことがないので、一度入ってみたいです。</span>(I've never been in a hot spring, so I'd like to try one once.)</div>","n4-45":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜んです」 is like saying <b>“the thing is…”, “it's just that…”, or “is something wrong?”</b> in English. The meaning of the sentence doesn't change, but it adds a layer of feeling: <b>I'm giving you the background</b>, or <b>I've noticed a situation and want to know what's going on</b>. Japanese people use it all the time. In speech it's almost always 「んです」, while 「のです」 is the version for writing and speeches.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Explaining a reason</span>When someone asks you why, or when you feel you should explain the reason.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どうして昨日休んだんですか。</span>(Why were you off yesterday?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>熱があったんです。</span>(I had a fever.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking about something you see</span>You see a situation in front of you and want to know what's behind it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どうしたんですか。</span>(What happened?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降っているんですか。</span>(Is it raining outside? Said when you see the other person come in with a wet umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Setting the scene before you ask</span>Use 「〜んですが」 or 「〜んですけど」 to lay out the situation first, then make your request or ask your question. It's a very natural way to open.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、駅に行きたいんですが…。</span>(Excuse me, I'd like to get to the station…)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Sharing news or feelings</span>Telling someone something on your own initiative, or stressing how you feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>実は来月結婚するんです。</span>(Actually, I'm getting married next month.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本当に困っているんです。</span>(I'm really in trouble.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: take the plain form (the form without ます or です) and add んです directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くんです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないんです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったんです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いんです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: in the present affirmative, you need to add <b>な</b> in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きなんです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生なんです</span></span><br>For past or negative, just attach the plain form with no な: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だったんです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好きじゃない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きじゃないんです</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>When talking with friends, use 「〜んだ」 or 「〜の」 to explain, and 「〜の？」 to ask: <span class='gd-jp'>どうしたの？</span> (What's wrong?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Forgetting な with な-adjectives and nouns: it's not 学生んです or 学生だんです, it's <span class='gd-jp'>学生なんです</span>.<br>・It can't follow ます or です: not 行きますんです or 高いですんです, but <span class='gd-jp'>行くんです</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>高いんです</span>.<br>・Don't put it in every sentence. To state a plain fact, ます is enough. If every sentence ends in んです, you sound like you're constantly explaining yourself or making excuses.<br>・When you ask about someone's actions with 「〜んですか」, the wrong tone or situation can sound like you're pressing or interrogating them. For example, <span class='gd-jp'>何をしているんですか</span> in a harsh voice becomes “What do you think you're doing!”, and <span class='gd-jp'>どうして遅れたんですか</span> can also sound like you're demanding an explanation. When asking someone older or senior, soften your tone, and you can start with <span class='gd-jp'>あのう</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: a coworker's plans for tomorrow.<br><b>〜ますか</b> = a plain question, with no clues to go on:<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日、どこかへ行きますか。</span>(Are you going anywhere tomorrow?)<br><b>〜んですか</b> = you see your coworker dragging a suitcase and want to know what's going on:<br><span class='gd-jp'>どこかへ行くんですか。</span>(Are you going somewhere?)<br>The answer works the same way. To give the reason, use んです: <span class='gd-jp'>大阪に出張なんです。</span>(I'm going to Osaka on a business trip.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you've noticed something and want to know the reason behind it, use んですか. If you're just asking about something plainly, use ますか.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Follow 「〜んですが」 with a request, and you get a very polite way of asking: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語がよくわからないんですが、ゆっくり話していただけませんか。</span>(I don't understand Japanese very well. Could you please speak more slowly?)</div>","n4-47":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both are about \"deciding,\" but the difference is <b>who made the call</b>.<br><b>ことにする</b> = \"I've decided to ~,\" so you make the decision yourself.<br><b>ことになる</b> = \"It's turned out that ~,\" so it may have been arranged by your company or school, or things may simply have worked out that way. It's like the difference between \"I decided to move\" and \"I've been transferred to Osaka.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Something you will do: dictionary form + ことにする／ことになる<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くことにする</span></span><br>Something you won't do: ない form + ことにする／ことになる<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないことにする</span></span><br>A decision is usually already made by the time you talk about it, so the past tense is the most common: <b>ことにした</b>, <b>ことになった</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The four forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことにした</span>A decision you've just made yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本に留学することにしました。</span>(I've decided to study abroad in Japan next year.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日はどこにも行かないことにした。</span>(I've decided not to go anywhere today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことになった</span>Someone else decided, or things simply worked out that way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来月から大阪に転勤することになりました。</span>(I'm being transferred to Osaka from next month.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>6月に結婚することになりました。</span>(I'm getting married in June.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Getting married is clearly your own decision, so why use なる? Because なる sounds like \"things have come to this point,\" which is more <b>low-key and polite</b>. Japanese people often say it this way when telling coworkers or their boss.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことにしている</span>A rule you set for yourself and keep following.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝る前はスマホを見ないことにしている。</span>(I make it a rule not to look at my phone before bed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ことになっている</span>A rule or custom set by a group, not by you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この寮では、10時までに帰ることになっている。</span>(In this dorm, you're supposed to be back by 10.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After a noun, attach にする directly with no こと: when ordering, say <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーにします</span> (I'll have coffee). Only a verb needs こと before にする: <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーをやめることにします</span> (I've decided to give up coffee).<br>・The particle is <b>に</b>, not を: <span class='gd-jp'>行くことにした</span>, not 「行くことをした」.<br>・When telling someone you're leaving your job, <span class='gd-jp'>辞めることにしました</span> sounds like \"I decided this myself,\" so your stance is very clear. <span class='gd-jp'>辞めることになりました</span> is softer and less direct.<br>・ことにしている means \"a rule I set myself,\" while ことになっている means \"that's just the rule.\" Don't describe a dorm rule with ことにしている.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same idea, \"walking every day\":<br><b>ことにする</b> = the moment you make the decision: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日歩くことにした。</span> (I've decided to walk every day.)<br><b>ようにする</b> = making an effort to do it: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日歩くようにしている。</span> (I try to walk every day.)<br><b>つもり</b> = a plan in your head that isn't settled yet: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日歩くつもりだ。</span> (I plan to walk every day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <b>ことになる</b> and <b>ようになる</b> are often confused:<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本で働くことになった。</span> (I'm going to work in Japan: it's been arranged, the matter is settled.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せるようになった。</span> (I've become able to speak Japanese: an ability or habit has gradually changed.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use ことにする for \"I decided,\" ことになる for \"it's been settled,\" ようにする for \"I try to,\" and つもり for \"I plan to.\"</div>","n4-48":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜まま」means <b>\"just leaving it as it is\" or \"with ~ still on/open/going\"</b>: a state <b>doesn't change</b>, and you go straight on to the next action. Think of leaving the house with the AC still on, or walking into a room with your shoes still on. It often carries the feeling of \"you should have changed that first, but didn't.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verb た form + まま</b>: the most common. You did an action, it left a state behind, and that state never changed.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>開けた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>開けたまま</span> (left open)</span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>つけた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>つけたまま</span> (left the light or TV on)</span><br><b>Verb ない form + まま</b>: the state of continuing not to do something.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わないまま</span> (without ever saying it)</span><br><b>Noun + の + まま</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>昔</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>昔のまま</span> (the same as before)</span><br><b>この・その・あの + まま</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>このまま</span> (like this), <span class='gd-jp'>そのまま</span> (like that)<br>Adjectives work too: い-adjectives attach directly, as in <span class='gd-jp'>若いまま</span> (staying young); な-adjectives need な, as in <span class='gd-jp'>きれいなまま</span> (staying clean).</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ended up that way by accident</span>Used with 「〜てしまう」 to show regret or \"oops.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>眼鏡をかけたままシャワーを浴びてしまった。</span>(I took a shower with my glasses still on.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テレビをつけたまま寝てしまった。</span>(I fell asleep with the TV still on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules and reminders</span>Telling someone not to do something while in a certain state.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>靴を履いたまま部屋に入らないでください。</span>(Please don't enter the room with your shoes on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Never did it</span>With the ない form, for something that was never done from start to finish.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何も言わないまま帰ってしまった。</span>(He went home without saying anything.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>このまま・そのまま</span>You'll hear these constantly in daily life, especially from shop staff and customer service.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのままお待ちください。</span>(Please wait just as you are.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>袋はいりません。このままで大丈夫です。</span>(I don't need a bag. I'm fine carrying it as it is.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the end of a sentence</span>Use 「〜のままだ／のままです」 to say \"it's still the same.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町は昔のままだ。</span>(This town is exactly the same as it used to be.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The た here is <b>not \"past tense\"</b>; it means \"it has already become that state.\" You use the た form even when talking about the present or future: <span class='gd-jp'>靴を履いたまま入ってもいいですよ。</span> (It's fine to come in with your shoes on.)<br>・To say \"staying in a state,\" don't use the dictionary form: ×<span class='gd-jp'>眼鏡をかけるまま</span>, but ○<span class='gd-jp'>眼鏡をかけたまま</span>.<br>・A noun must always take の: ×<span class='gd-jp'>昔まま</span>, but ○<span class='gd-jp'>昔のまま</span>.<br>・English \"while ~ing\" or \"with ~ on\" isn't always まま. If you're just doing two things at the same time (eating while watching something), Japanese uses ながら; see below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ながら</b> = two actions <b>happening at the same time</b>, and you're actively doing both:<br><span class='gd-jp'>テレビを見ながら勉強した。</span> (I studied while watching TV.)<br><b>〜たまま</b> = a state <b>left untouched</b> while you go on to the next action:<br><span class='gd-jp'>テレビをつけたまま勉強した。</span> (I studied with the TV left on.)<br>In the first sentence you're really watching TV; in the second the TV is just on, and you may not be watching at all.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜て</b> = a plain \"doing something with ~,\" with no sense of \"should have changed it\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>帽子をかぶって出かけた。</span> (I put on a hat and went out.)<br><b>〜たまま</b> = the listener feels \"shouldn't you have changed that first?\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>帽子をかぶったまま教室に入った。</span> (He walked into the classroom with his hat still on, as if he should have taken it off first.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: two things done together is ながら; leaving a state alone and going on to the next action is たまま.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「にしておく」 to get <b>deliberately keeping it that way</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けたままにしておいてください。</span> (Please leave the window open; don't close it.)</div>","n4-15":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なくてもいいです」means <b>\"you don't have to ~\"</b> or <b>\"it's fine not to ~\"</b>. Think of a shop assistant telling you \"You don't need to take your shoes off.\" It means you <b>can skip it, and it's also fine if you do it</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Telling someone they needn't do something</span>Use it to put the other person at ease or save them effort.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は来なくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to come tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>全部食べなくてもいいですよ。</span>(You don't have to eat it all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking whether something is necessary</span>Add 「か」 at the end and it becomes \"Is it okay not to ~?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>靴を脱がなくてもいいですか。</span>(Do I not need to take my shoes off?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はい、脱がなくてもいいですよ。</span>(Right, you don't need to.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into its ない form (the \"not ~\" form). Then replace the final 「い」 with 「くて」 and add 「もいい」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → replace い with くて → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなくて</span></span> → add もいい → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かなくてもいい</span></span><br>To make it polite, add 「です」 at the end.</div><div class='gd-h'>Conjugation: first, which type of verb is it?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the matching 「あ」-row sound, add ない, then change it to なくてもいい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>書かなくてもいい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たなくてもいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らなくてもいい</span></span><br>Verbs ending in 「う」 change to <b>わ</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買わなくてもいい</span></span><br>Exception: the ない form of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> is just 「ない」, so it becomes <span class='gd-jp'>なくてもいい</span>, not あらなくてもいい.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>予約がなくてもいいですか。</span>(Is it okay without a reservation?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add なくてもいい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べなくてもいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>慌てる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>慌てなくてもいい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (just two verbs, so memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しなくてもいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来なくてもいい</span>(こなくても)</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は残業しなくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to work overtime today.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Between friends</span>Swap 「です」 for 「よ」, and the 「も」 often gets dropped too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>無理しなくていいよ。</span>(No need to push yourself.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Another way to say it</span>Replace 「いい」 with 「大丈夫」; the meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>返事しなくても大丈夫です。</span>(You don't need to reply.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>More polite</span>At work, in shops and in formal notices you'll hear 「なくてもかまいません」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>印鑑は持ってこなくてもかまいません。</span>(You don't need to bring your seal.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't attach ても straight onto ない: 「行かないてもいい」 is wrong. Change い to くて to get <span class='gd-jp'>行かなくてもいい</span>.<br>・The ない form of <span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, not 買あない; the ない form of <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>ない</span>, not あらない.<br>・In English, \"you don't have to come\" and \"you must not come\" can sound close, but they are very different. <span class='gd-jp'>来なくてもいい</span> means \"you can skip it, but you're welcome to come.\" It does not mean \"please don't come.\"<br>・When someone asks 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜なくてもいいですか</span>」 and the answer is \"you must do it,\" answer with <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、〜なければなりません</span>. Don't just say いいえ and stop there.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are four ways to talk about \"going to the office tomorrow\":<br><b>てもいい</b> = you may do it: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、会社に行ってもいいです。</span>(You may go to the office tomorrow.)<br><b>なくてもいい</b> = you don't have to: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、会社に行かなくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to go to the office tomorrow.)<br><b>てはいけない</b> = you must not: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、会社に行ってはいけません。</span>(You must not go to the office tomorrow.)<br><b>なければならない</b> = you must: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、会社に行かなければなりません。</span>(You must go to the office tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another one that's often confused with it is 「ないでください」:<br><span class='gd-jp'>来ないでください。</span>(Please don't come: you're telling them not to.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>来なくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to come: either way is fine.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say \"no need, skip it,\" use なくてもいい. To say \"don't do it, not allowed,\" use ないでください or てはいけない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adjectives and nouns can express the same idea: <span class='gd-jp'>高くなくてもいいです。</span>(It doesn't have to be expensive.), <span class='gd-jp'>上手じゃなくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to be good at it.) Next, learn 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜なければなりません</span>」, and you'll be able to say both \"you don't have to\" and \"you must\" clearly.</div>","n4-16":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なければなりません」means <b>\"must ~\"</b> or <b>\"have to ~\"</b>. The Japanese logic is interesting: it literally says \"if you don't do it, it won't do.\" If not doing it is unacceptable, then you have to do it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules and obligations</span>Use it for rules and duties: things your school or company requires, or things the situation forces you to do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日までにレポートを出さなければなりません。</span>(You must hand in the report by tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>学校では制服を着なければなりません。</span>(At school, you have to wear a uniform.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Feeling you have to do it yourself</span>Nobody has set a rule; you just feel \"I really should do this now.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう11時だ。帰らなければならない。</span>(It's already 11 o'clock. I have to go home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬を毎日飲まなければなりません。</span>(I have to take my medicine every day.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Three steps: turn the verb into its ない form (the \"not ~\" form), drop the final 「ない」, then add <b>なければなりません</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span> → drop ない → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かなければなりません</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書かなければなりません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待たなければなりません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買わなければなりません</span></span> (verbs ending in う become わ, not あ)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べなければなりません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きなければなりません</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (just two verbs, so memorize them)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しなければなりません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来なければなりません</span>(こなければ)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>なければなりません</span>The polite form, for teachers, bosses and customers.<br><span class='gd-tag'>なければならない</span>The plain form, for talking to yourself and for writing.<br><span class='gd-tag'>なきゃ／ないと</span>Casual speech between friends: 「なければ」 shrinks to 「なきゃ」, or you just stop at 「ないと」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、もう行かなきゃ。</span>(Oh, I've got to go now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日早いから、早く寝ないと。</span>(I have an early start tomorrow, so I'd better get to bed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To say \"had to ~\" in the past, change the ending to <b>なりませんでした</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日は遅くまで働かなければなりませんでした。</span>(Yesterday I had to work until late.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You'll also hear 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜なければいけません</span>」. It means almost the same thing and is very common in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the ない form, not the dictionary form (the base form you'd find in a dictionary): it's not 行くなければ but <span class='gd-jp'>行かなければ</span>.<br>・It's long and easy to misread. Break it into 「な・け・れ・ば」 + 「なりません」 to remember it; it's not なくれば or なけば.<br>・Don't see the 「ません」 at the end and think it means \"don't do it.\" The whole phrase means <b>you must do it</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> is read <b>こなければ</b>, not きなければ.<br>・「なきゃ」 and 「ないと」 are for friends. With a boss or teacher, use the full なければなりません.<br>・When asking someone to do something, saying \"you must ~\" outright sounds harsh. 「〜てください」 is the usual, politer choice.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>These four often show up together on tests. Here they are with the same situation, \"going to school tomorrow\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校に行かなければなりません。</span>(You <b>have to</b> go to school tomorrow.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校に行かなくてもいいです。</span>(You <b>don't have to</b> go to school tomorrow.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校に行ってもいいです。</span>(You <b>may</b> go to school tomorrow.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日、学校に行ってはいけません。</span>(You <b>must not</b> go to school tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Watch out: if someone asks 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜なければなりませんか</span>」 (\"Do I have to ~?\") and you want to answer \"no need,\" say <b>なくてもいいです</b>. It isn't a negative of なければなりません.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>名前を書かなければなりませんか。</span>(Do I have to write my name?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、書かなくてもいいです。</span>(No, you don't have to. It's fine not to write it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another frequent mix-up is <b>ほうがいい</b>, which is only a \"suggestion\" and not mandatory:<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだほうがいいです。</span>(You'd better take some medicine.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲まなければなりません。</span>(You have to take your medicine.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: \"must\" is なければなりません, \"don't have to\" is なくてもいい, and \"had better\" is ほうがいい.</div>","n4-13":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てもいいです」means <b>“you may ~” or “it's okay to ~”</b>. Add「か」at the end and it becomes “May I ~?” or “Is it okay to ~?”, the most common way to <b>ask permission</b> in Japan. In a shop, when you want to take a photo, sit in an empty seat or try something on, this is how you start the question.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its te-form, then add「もいいです」。<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>使う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使ってもいいです</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to conjugate: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: look at the last kana.<br>う、つ、る → って：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>座る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>座って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待って</span></span><br>む、ぶ、ぬ → んで：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休んで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>呼ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>呼んで</span></span><br>く → いて：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span></span>(the only exception is <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span></span>)<br>ぐ → いで：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳いで</span></span><br>す → して：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話して</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop「る」and add て.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見て</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来て</span>（きて）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking permission</span>Add「か」at the end.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この椅子を使ってもいいですか。</span>(May I use this chair?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここに座ってもいいですか。</span>(May I sit here?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>How to answer</span>Yes: <span class='gd-jp'>はい、どうぞ。</span>(Yes, go ahead), <span class='gd-jp'>いいですよ。</span>(Sure)<br>No: <span class='gd-jp'>すみません、ちょっと…。</span>(Sorry, that's a bit difficult…)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Telling someone it's okay</span>It's common to add「よ」at the end, which sounds friendlier.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は休んでもいいですよ。</span>(You can take tomorrow off)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>鉛筆で書いてもいいです。</span>(You may write in pencil)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Between friends</span>Drop です, and in casual speech even「も」is often dropped.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これ、食べていい？</span>(Can I eat this?)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To ask more politely of a boss or a customer, swap いい for <span class='gd-jp'>よろしい</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>写真を撮ってもよろしいですか。</span>(Would it be all right to take a photo?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When the te-form ends in「で」, what follows is「でもいい」: <span class='gd-jp'>休んでもいい</span>. Don't write 休んてもいい.<br>・When someone asks「〜てもいいですか」and you want to say no, textbooks teach <span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、いけません</span>, but in real conversation it sounds harsh, like scolding. In daily life people mostly say <span class='gd-jp'>すみません、ちょっと…</span>.<br>・「〜てもいいですよ」<b>grants permission</b>, and it sounds a bit like talking down to someone. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>帰ってもいいですよ</span> to a teacher or boss would be very rude.<br>・English “can” also covers ability, as in “I can speak Japanese”. That is really “I'm able to speak it”, so Japanese says <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せます</span>. If you say <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話してもいいです</span>, it means “you're allowed to speak Japanese”.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling apart the look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The four patterns “may, must not, don't have to, have to” look alike. Here they are with one and the same situation:<br><span class='gd-tag'>Do it, OK</span><span class='gd-jp'>明日は来てもいいです。</span>(You may come tomorrow)<br><span class='gd-tag'>Do it, NG</span><span class='gd-jp'>明日は来てはいけません。</span>(You must not come tomorrow)<br><span class='gd-tag'>Don't do it, OK</span><span class='gd-jp'>明日は来なくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to come tomorrow)<br><span class='gd-tag'>Don't do it, NG</span><span class='gd-jp'>明日は来なくてはいけません。</span>(You have to come tomorrow)<br>The last one is also often said as <span class='gd-jp'>来なければなりません</span>, with the same meaning.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: first check whether the front part is「て」or「なくて」, then whether the ending is「いい」or「いけない」.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also don't mix it up with <b>ことができます</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ここで泳いでもいいですか。</span>(May I swim here?: asking if it's allowed)<br><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐことができますか。</span>(Can you swim?: asking if you're able)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: for “is it allowed?” use てもいい; for “can you / are you able to?” use ことができる.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Learn the other three cells of the table above: 「〜てはいけません」(prohibition), 「〜なくてもいいです」(not necessary), and 「〜なければなりません」(obligation).</div>","n4-14":"<div class='gd-h'>The gist in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てはいけません」means <b>“must not ~” or “not allowed to ~”</b>. You'll hear it when a teacher announces rules, when someone explains the rules of a place, or when parents tell a child “don't lie”. (Real signs mostly say「<span class='gd-jp'>撮影禁止</span>」or「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ないでください</span>」instead of writing out the whole sentence.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Explaining rules</span>Rules of a school, company or place, spoken aloud to someone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮ってはいけません。</span>(You must not take photos here)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>授業中に寝てはいけません。</span>(You must not sleep during class)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Teaching kids, stating general rules</span>When adults speak to children, or talk about things everyone should follow, the plain form「〜てはいけない」(without ます) is common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うそをついてはいけないよ。</span>(You mustn't lie, okay?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First change the verb to its <b>te-form</b> (the same form that goes before「〜てください」), then add「はいけません」directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>撮る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>撮って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>撮ってはいけません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べてはいけません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してはいけません</span></span><br>Some Group 1 (godan) verbs have a te-form ending in「で」, so it becomes「ではいけません」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んではいけません</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んではいけません</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜てはいけません</span>The polite form, used when explaining rules.<br><span class='gd-tag'>〜てはいけない</span>The plain form, for family, friends and children.<br><span class='gd-tag'>〜ちゃいけない</span>The shortened spoken form: 「ては」becomes「ちゃ」and「では」becomes「じゃ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>危ないから、ここで遊んじゃいけないよ。</span>(It's dangerous, so you mustn't play here, okay?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>〜ちゃだめ</span>Swap「いけない」for「だめ」and it gets even more casual. Parents scolding kids use it all the time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まだ食べちゃだめ。</span>(You can't eat yet)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The「は」in「ては」is pronounced <b>wa</b>, not ha.<br>・After「飲んで」「遊んで」you need <b>では</b>. Don't write 飲んてはいけません. When shortened it also becomes「じゃ」: <span class='gd-jp'>飲んじゃだめ</span>.<br>・This phrase is <b>very strong</b>, like laying down a rule. Don't use it directly with bosses, customers or elders. Say <span class='gd-jp'>〜ないでください</span> (please don't ~) instead, or the phrase shops often use, <span class='gd-jp'>〜はご遠慮ください</span>.<br>・Textbooks often teach「<span class='gd-jp'>〜てもいいですか</span>」→「<span class='gd-jp'>いいえ、〜てはいけません</span>」, but in real life that reply comes across as harsh. Japanese people usually say <span class='gd-jp'>すみません、ここはちょっと…</span>, using「ちょっと」to decline gently.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling apart the look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>“May / must not / don't have to / have to” are easy to tangle. Here they are in one situation (a doctor's instructions about medicine):<br><b>てもいい</b> = may do: <span class='gd-jp'>この薬は飲んでもいいです。</span>(You may take this medicine)<br><b>てはいけない</b> = must not do: <span class='gd-jp'>この薬は飲んではいけません。</span>(You must not take this medicine)<br><b>なくてもいい</b> = don't have to do: <span class='gd-jp'>この薬は飲まなくてもいいです。</span>(You don't have to take this medicine)<br><b>なければならない</b> = have to do: <span class='gd-jp'>この薬は飲まなければなりません。</span>(You have to take this medicine)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The one to be most careful with is「<b>なくてはいけません</b>」. It also contains「いけません」, but it means <b>must</b>, the same as「なければなりません」:<br><span class='gd-jp'>飲んではいけません</span> (must not take it)<br><span class='gd-jp'>飲まなくてはいけません</span> (have to take it)<br>Only a few syllables differ, yet the meaning is completely opposite.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick trick: look at what comes before「はいけません」. A regular te-form (<span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span>) → must not; 「なくて」(<span class='gd-jp'>飲まなくて</span>) → have to.</div>","n4-21":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「し」works like English <b>\"and what's more…\"</b> or <b>\"not only X, but also Y\"</b>. When you explain <i>why</i>, you stack up reasons one by one and then lead to your conclusion. For example, \"This place is cheap, and it's tasty, so I come here often.\" In Japanese, し links \"cheap\" and \"tasty\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いし、おいしいし、よく来ます。</span>(This place is cheap and tasty, so I come often.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form</b> (the form without ます or です) in front, then add し directly.<br><b>Verbs</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降るし</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ないし</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>疲れた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>疲れたし</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いし</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遠くない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遠くないし</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「だ」 first, then し. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだし</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「だ」 here too. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だし</span></span><br>When speaking to superiors or customers, you can use the polite form too: <span class='gd-jp'>便利ですし</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>あまり時間もありませんし</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Stacking reasons to reach a conclusion</span>This is the most common use. 「も」 often goes after each reason, giving a \"this too, and that too…\" feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お金もないし、時間もないし、旅行は無理です。</span>(I have no money and no time either, so a trip is out of the question.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨も降っているし、疲れているし、タクシーで帰りましょう。</span>(It's raining, and we're tired, so let's take a taxi home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing the good or bad points of a person or thing</span>Use it to list the features of a person or a place.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは優しいし、おもしろいし、みんなに人気がある。</span>(Tanaka is kind and funny, and popular with everyone.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving just one reason</span>You can also give only one. It sounds like \"(there are other reasons too, but) this alone is enough…\", and the tone is softer.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう遅いし、そろそろ帰ります。</span>(It's already late, so I'll head home soon.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can even leave the conclusion unsaid and let the other person figure it out. This is very handy for turning someone down:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日はちょっと疲れてるし…</span>(I'm a bit tired today, so…)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・な-adjectives and nouns <b>must take だ</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静かだし</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>雨だし</span>, never 「静かし」 or 「雨し」.<br>・Stacked reasons should <b>point in the same direction</b>, all supporting the same conclusion. To say \"I like this place,\" stack \"cheap\" and \"tasty\"; don't mix a good point with a bad one.<br>・し carries a sense of \"and that's not all.\" If you have just one clear reason and want to state it plainly and firmly, 「から」 or 「ので」 is more direct.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>し</b> = stacks several reasons, with the tone \"X, and Y, so…\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いし、おいしいし、よく来ます。</span>(It's cheap and tasty, so I come often.)<br><b>から・ので</b> = states plainly \"it's because of this\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いから、よく来ます。</span>(Because it's cheap, I come often.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たり</b> also lists several things, but it gives examples of <b>actions you do</b>, not reasons:<br><span class='gd-jp'>週末は映画を見たり、買い物をしたりします。</span>(On weekends I do things like watch movies and go shopping.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>週末は暇だし、天気もいいし、出かけよう。</span>(I'm free this weekend and the weather's nice, so let's go out.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: explaining \"why\" with several stacked reasons → し; one clear reason → から・ので; listing \"what you do\" → たり.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you want to add one more reason after you've finished, use 「それに」 (besides, on top of that) to start the next sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は広いし、駅にも近い。それに家賃も安い。</span>(This room is spacious and close to the station. On top of that, the rent is cheap.)</div>","n4-36":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜でしょう」 is like English <b>\"probably…\"</b> or <b>\"I guess…\"</b>. With a falling ending, you're <b>guessing</b>; with a rising ending, it becomes \"…, right?\" and you're <b>checking with the other person</b>. 「だろう」 is the plain form. The meaning is the same, but it isn't polite.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing (falling intonation)</span>You aren't sure, but you feel it's most likely so. Weather forecasts love it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るでしょう。</span>(It will probably rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんはもう家に帰ったでしょう。</span>(Mr. Tanaka has probably already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears with 「<span class='gd-jp'>たぶん</span>」 (probably): <span class='gd-jp'>たぶん大丈夫でしょう。</span>(It's probably fine.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Checking (rising intonation)</span>You think the other person knows too and will agree, so you ask to get a nod. In speech it's often shortened to 「<span class='gd-jp'>でしょ？</span>」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これ、おいしいでしょう？</span>(This is tasty, right?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ほら、言ったでしょ？</span>(See? Didn't I tell you?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form</b> (without です or ます) in front, then add でしょう or だろう.<br>Verbs: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くでしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないでしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったでしょう</span></span><br>い-adjectives: add it directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いでしょう</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives: <b>drop だ</b> first, then add it. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生でしょう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気でしょう</span></span><br>In the past tense, keep 「だった」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だったでしょう</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Choosing between でしょう and だろう</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>でしょう</b>: polite. Use it with people you don't know well, in formal settings, and in news and weather reports.<br><b>だろう</b>: plain. It shows up most in <b>thoughts, talking to yourself, and writing</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は晴れるだろう。</span>(It'll probably clear up tomorrow: thinking to yourself)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Saying 「<span class='gd-jp'>だろう</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>だろ？</span>」 directly to someone sounds masculine and a bit pushy. When chatting with friends, many people (especially women) say 「<span class='gd-jp'>でしょ？</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't keep だ after nouns and な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>学生でしょう</span>, not 「学生だでしょう」.<br>・English \"will\" is not always でしょう. \"I'll go tomorrow\" is your own decision, so just say <span class='gd-jp'>明日行きます</span>. Saying 「行くでしょう」 sounds like you're guessing whether you yourself will go, which is odd.<br>・The confirming 「<span class='gd-jp'>でしょう？</span>」 carries a feel of \"you must know this, right?\", so it can sound a bit rude to a teacher or boss. Switching to 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ですよね</span>」 is safer.<br>・でしょう is pronounced with a long vowel (でしょー); the short 「でしょ」 is the shortened spoken version.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same question: will Mr. Tanaka come today?<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るでしょう。</span>(Mr. Tanaka will probably come.) → I think he <b>most likely will</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るかもしれません。</span>(Mr. Tanaka might come.) → <b>Fifty-fifty</b>; it wouldn't be a surprise if he didn't.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来るはずです。</span>(Mr. Tanaka should come.) → <b>There's a basis for it</b>, e.g. he said himself yesterday that he'd come.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: 「might」 → かもしれない, 「probably」 → でしょう, 「should, as expected」 → はずだ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Adding 「と思います」 afterward makes it softer, and both men and women use it a lot: <span class='gd-jp'>明日は寒くなるだろうと思います。</span>(I think it'll probably get cold tomorrow.)<br>Turning でしょう into the question 「でしょうか」 is more polite than 「ですか」: <span class='gd-jp'>すみません、駅はどこでしょうか。</span>(Excuse me, where is the station?)</div>","n4-11":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ほうがいい」 means <b>“you'd better ～”</b> or <b>“you'd better not ～”</b>. If your friend has a fever and you say “You should go see a doctor,” that's <span class='gd-jp'>病院に行ったほうがいい</span>. It's advice, with a hint of “if you don't, things might not go so well.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Better to do ～</span>verb <b>た-form</b> + ほうがいい<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休んだほうがいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったほうがいい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたほうがいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>したほうがいい</span></span><br>If the た-form still feels shaky, work from the て-form: just swap 「て」 for 「た」 and 「で」 for 「だ」.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休んだ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Better not to ～</span>verb <b>ない-form</b> + ほうがいい<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないほうがいい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べないほうがいい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないほうがいい</span>（こない）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The ending depends on who you're talking to: with friends, say 「〜ほうがいいよ」; to be more polite, say 「〜ほうがいいです」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだから、傘を持っていったほうがいいよ。</span>（It looks like rain, so you'd better take an umbrella.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寝る前にコーヒーを飲まないほうがいいです。</span>（You'd better not drink coffee before bed.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It uses the た-form, but it's <b>not past tense</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>行ったほうがいい</span> tells you to go now or later, not that you “have gone.”<br>・Your instinct may be to use the dictionary form: 「行くほうがいい」. When giving someone specific advice, Japanese speakers normally say <span class='gd-jp'>行ったほうがいい</span>, so memorize it that way to be safe.<br>・For the negative, use just the ない-form, <b>with no further change to the た-form</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行かないほうがいい</span>, not 「行かなかったほうがいい」.<br>・The tone is actually not that light. Saying 「〜たほうがいいですよ」 straight to your boss or teacher can sound like you're telling them what to do. To soften it, add 「と思います」: <span class='gd-jp'>少し休んだほうがいいと思います。</span>（I think you'd better rest a little.）<br>・In kanji, 「ほう」 is written 「方」, and here it's read <b>ほう</b>, not the かた of 「〜方」 (“way of doing ~”).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation, a sore throat:<br><b>たほうがいい</b> = advice. You could skip it, it's just not ideal: <span class='gd-jp'>のどが痛いなら、薬を飲んだほうがいいですよ。</span>（If your throat hurts, you'd better take some medicine.）<br><b>なければなりません</b> = a rule. You must do it: <span class='gd-jp'>この薬は一日三回飲まなければなりません。</span>（You must take this medicine three times a day.）<br><b>たらいい</b> = suggesting one way to do it, in a lighter tone: <span class='gd-jp'>のどが痛いときは、温かいお茶を飲んだらいいですよ。</span>（When your throat hurts, it's good to drink some hot tea.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: “you'd better ～ (or it won't be good)” takes ほうがいい; “you must ～” takes なければなりません; “just do ～ and you'll be fine” takes たらいい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change いい to よかった and you get regret, “I should have ～”: <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持ってきたほうがよかった。</span>（I should have brought an umbrella.）</div>","n4-12":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜すぎる」 means <b>“too ～”</b> or <b>“～ too much”</b>. Eating too much, drinking too much, too expensive, too hard: whenever something goes <b>past the right amount</b>, add 「すぎる」 after it. For a polite tone, say 「すぎます」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日、ゲームをやりすぎました。</span>（I played too many video games yesterday.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このかばんは高すぎます。</span>（This bag is too expensive.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span>Change to the ます-form, drop 「ます」, and add すぎる. All three verb groups work the same way.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みすぎる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べすぎる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しすぎる</span></span>（e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>心配しすぎる</span> = to worry too much）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>い-adjectives</span>Drop the final 「い」 and add すぎる.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高すぎる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>難しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>難しすぎる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjectives</span>Attach it directly, <b>without adding な</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かすぎる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>派手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>派手すぎる</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>すぎる itself conjugates too</div><div class='gd-sec'>すぎる conjugates like a <b>Group 2 (ichidan) verb</b>: drop 「る」 and add the ending.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>すぎる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すぎます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>すぎる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すぎた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>すぎる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すぎて</span></span><br>The most common form is 「すぎて」, which gives the reason for the result that follows:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べすぎて、お腹が痛いです。</span>（I ate too much, and my stomach hurts.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は静かすぎて、ちょっとこわいです。</span>（This room is too quiet, and it's a little scary.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear a lot</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Becomes a noun</span>Verb ます-stem + すぎ, without the る, becomes a noun meaning “too much ～” or “overdoing ～.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>飲みすぎに気をつけてください。</span>（Please be careful not to drink too much.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Apology</span>You hear this a lot after an argument.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さっきは言いすぎました。ごめんなさい。</span>（I went too far with what I said earlier. I'm sorry.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You must drop the 「い」 of い-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>高すぎる</span>, not 高いすぎる.<br>・Don't add な to な-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>静かすぎる</span>, not 静かなすぎる.<br>・<b>「いい」 has to become 「よ」</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>よすぎる</span> (too good). If you write <span class='gd-jp'>いいすぎる</span>, Japanese people will hear it as <span class='gd-jp'>言いすぎる</span> (to say too much, to go too far).<br>・「ない」 (not having) becomes <span class='gd-jp'>なさすぎる</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>時間がなさすぎる</span> (there's far too little time). But <span class='gd-jp'>少ない</span> follows the normal rule: <span class='gd-jp'>少なすぎる</span>.<br>・すぎる usually carries a feeling of “past the limit, not great.” Young people do use <span class='gd-jp'>かわいすぎる</span> to praise someone, but for ordinary “really tasty” or “really pretty,” <span class='gd-jp'>とても</span> is the safer choice.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>English has the same split between “very” and “too,” and Japanese keeps them apart too. Same situation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このかばんはとても高いです。</span>（This bag is very expensive.）: it just describes the price as high.<br><span class='gd-jp'>このかばんは高すぎます。</span>（This bag is too expensive.）: it's beyond what you can accept, and the implication is “I won't buy it.”</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you can follow it with “so it's no good / so there's a problem,” use すぎる. If you're just saying the degree is high, use とても.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Combine it with 「〜ないようにする」 and you get a favorite line of doctors: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みすぎないようにしてください。</span>（Please try not to drink too much.）</div>","n4-6":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜方」 works like English <b>\"how to ~\" or \"the way of ~ing\"</b>: how to write, how to read, how to use, how to eat. It turns an action into \"the way to do it.\" You'll use it all the time when you ask \"How do I use this?\" or \"How do I get there?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in ます form, <b>drop ます</b>, then add <b>方（かた）</b>.<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs) and Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b> work the same way:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>書き方</span></span> (how to write)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読み方</span></span> (how to read)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>使います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使い方</span></span> (how to use)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べ方</span></span> (how to eat)<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見方</span></span> (how to look at something, a point of view)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (する・来る) needs to be memorized separately</b>:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>する</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>仕方</span>（しかた）.<br>・For 「noun ＋ する」 verbs, say <b>noun ＋ の ＋ 仕方</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強の仕方</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>掃除する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>掃除の仕方</span></span><br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>来方</span>（きかた）, but you'll hear <span class='gd-jp'>行き方</span> much more often.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking for a method</span>You don't know how to do something, so you ask someone to teach you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この漢字の読み方を教えてください。</span> (Please tell me how to read this kanji.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅への行き方がわかりません。</span> (I don't know how to get to the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語の勉強の仕方を教えてください。</span> (Please teach me how to study Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing how someone does something</span>It isn't always a \"method.\" It can also mean the <b>manner or habit</b> someone has when doing it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は話し方がやさしいです。</span> (He has a gentle way of speaking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>箸の持ち方がきれいですね。</span> (You hold your chopsticks so properly, don't you!)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Change を to の.</b> 「〜方」 is already a noun, so を can't come before it: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本を読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本の読み方</span></span>, not 「本を読み方」.<br>・「に／へ」 usually becomes 「への」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>駅に行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>駅への行き方</span></span>.<br>・Since it's a noun, it takes particles like <span class='gd-jp'>が</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>を</span> after it: <span class='gd-jp'>使い方がわかりません</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書き方を教えてください</span>.<br>・Here 方 is read <b>かた</b>, and it doesn't turn into がた: <span class='gd-jp'>書き方</span> is read かきかた.<br>・For verbs like 勉強する and 掃除する, don't follow the formula and make 「勉強し方」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>勉強の仕方</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with a look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same kanji 「方」 is read <b>ほう</b> in <span class='gd-jp'>〜ほうがいいです</span>, and the meaning is completely different.<br><span class='gd-jp'>書き方を教えてください。</span> (Please teach me how to write it: method)<br><span class='gd-jp'>名前を書いたほうがいいです。</span> (You should write your name: advice)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what comes before is 「ます form minus ます」 (書き, 読み), it's read かた = \"how to ~.\" If it's the た form, ない form, or dictionary form (書いた, 書かない), it's read ほう = comparison or advice.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜やすい・〜にくい」 also attach to the ます form minus ます, so it's handy to learn them together: <span class='gd-jp'>このペンは書きやすいです。</span> (This pen is easy to write with.)</div>","n4-9":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことができます」 means <b>\"can ~\", \"be able to ~\", or \"know how to ~\"</b>. It's simple: add 「こと」 to an action to turn it into \"the act of doing it,\" then say it \"can be done (できます).\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Ability</span>You've studied or practiced, so you can do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は日本語を話すことができます。</span> (I can speak Japanese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ピアノを弾くことができます。</span> (I can play the piano.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Circumstances allow it</span>It's not about whether you have the skill. The place or the rules let you do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この図書館ではWi-Fiを使うことができます。</span> (You can use Wi-Fi at this library.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このカードはコンビニでも使うことができます。</span> (This card can be used at convenience stores too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use the <b>dictionary form</b> of the verb (the form you find in a dictionary) and add 「ことができます」. All three verb groups work the same way, with no extra changes.<br>If you only remember the ます form, change it back to the dictionary form first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書くことができます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べることができます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>することができます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ることができます</span>（くる）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>For \"can't,\" change ます to ません. For \"was able to (at that time),\" change it to ました:<br><span class='gd-jp'>漢字を書くことができません。</span> (I can't write kanji.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日はゆっくり休むことができました。</span> (Yesterday I was able to get a good rest.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Nouns for <b>skills, sports, and languages</b> can also take 「ができます」 directly: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語ができます。</span> (I can do Japanese / I speak Japanese.), <span class='gd-jp'>テニスができます。</span> (I can play tennis.)<br>Ordinary nouns can't be used this way. For example, you don't say 「電車ができます」. You say <span class='gd-jp'>電車に乗ることができます</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before must be the dictionary form, never the ます form. You don't say 「行きますことができます」. It's <span class='gd-jp'>行くことができます</span>.<br>・The original 「を」 stays: <span class='gd-jp'>ピアノを弾くことができます</span>. You don't need to change it to が.<br>・English \"will\" (future) can't be expressed with ことができます. \"It will rain tomorrow\" is <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るでしょう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降るかもしれません</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span> already means \"to understand,\" so don't add ことができます. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>日本語がわかります</span>.<br>・To ask permission on the spot (\"May I ~?\"), <span class='gd-jp'>〜てもいいですか</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <b>potential form</b> (話せる, 書ける) means almost the same as ことができます:<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すことができます。</span> (I can speak Japanese: more formal, stiffer)<br><span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せます。</span> (I can speak Japanese: more common in everyday speech)<br>You'll often see ことができます on notices, in manuals, and in formal self-introductions. With friends, people mostly use the potential form. If you haven't learned the potential form yet, ことができます is always safe.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜てもいいです</b> means \"I allow you to do it\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮ることができます。</span> (You can take photos here: explaining the rules or the situation)<br><span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真を撮ってもいいですよ。</span> (You may take photos here: the speaker is giving permission)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to talk about ability or possibility (\"can or can't\"), use ことができます or the potential form. To talk about permission (\"allowed or not\"), use てもいいです.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A more casual way to say the same thing is the potential form, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span></span>.</div>","n4-1":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜までに」means <b>\"by…\" or \"no later than…\" (it has to be done before then)</b>. In other words, it sets a deadline. Think of a bill that says \"Please pay by May 10\". That \"by\" is までに. The key point: <b>you finish one thing before that point in time</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving a deadline</span>A teacher, boss, or shop tells you \"this has to be done by such-and-such a time\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>レポートは金曜日までに出してください。</span>(Please hand in the report by Friday.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日までにお願いします。</span>(Could you get it to me by tomorrow, please?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Your own goals</span>Setting a deadline for yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>5時までに駅に着きたい。</span>(I want to get to the station by 5:00.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏までに5キロやせたい。</span>(I want to lose 5 kg by summer.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Times, days, and events</b> take までに directly:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>3時</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>3時までに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>試験</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>試験までに</span></span><br><b>Verbs</b> use the plain dictionary form (辭書形) plus までに, meaning \"before ~ happens\":<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰るまでに</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母が帰るまでに部屋を片付けます。</span>(I'll tidy my room before my mom gets back.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from its look-alike</div><div class='gd-sec'>It differs by just one 「に」, but the meaning changes a lot.<br><b>まで</b> = an action <b>keeps going</b> until that time, then stops.<br><b>までに</b> = you <b>get something done before</b> that time, and then it's finished.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>5時まで待ちます。</span>(I'll wait until 5:00. I wait continuously from now until 5:00.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>5時までに来てください。</span>(Please come by 5:00. Coming at 4:00 or 4:30 is fine.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10時まで勉強します。</span>(I'll study until 10:00.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10時までにお風呂に入ります。</span>(I'll take a bath before 10:00.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you'd say \"until\" in English, use <b>まで</b>. If you'd say \"by\" or \"no later than\", use <b>までに</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When you mean \"keep doing something up to that time\" (waiting until 5:00, studying until 10:00), use まで, not までに. For example, <span class='gd-jp'>5時までに待ちます</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>5時まで待ちます</span>. But if you mean \"get something done before a certain time\", までに works, as in <span class='gd-jp'>試験までに勉強しておきます</span> (I'll get my studying done before the exam). Japanese people say this all the time.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>金曜日までに</span> normally includes Friday itself. Handing it in on Friday still counts as on time, so it doesn't have to be Thursday.<br>・A verb before it must be in dictionary form: you say <span class='gd-jp'>日本に来るまでに</span>, never 「来たまでに」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「は」 to get <span class='gd-jp'>までには</span>, which adds the nuance \"at the very least, before ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>夜までには帰ります。</span> (I'll definitely be back by evening at the latest.)</div>","n4-23":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てみる」means <b>\"try ...ing\"</b> or <b>\"give it a try\"</b>: try eating it, try it on, try asking. You don't know the result yet, so you do it once to see what happens. Add「みる」after the verb's て-form. For the polite form, say「みます」.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Trying something</span>It's your first time and you don't know how it'll turn out, so you give it a go.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べてみた。</span>(I tried natto.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>わからないから、駅員さんに聞いてみます。</span>(I don't know, so I'll try asking the station staff.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Trying on / trying out</span>Very common in shops. Follow it with「〜てもいいですか」to ask permission.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この服、着てみてもいいですか。</span>(May I try this on?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking someone to try</span>「〜てみてください」= please try ...ing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この靴、はいてみてください。</span>(Please try these shoes on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Wanting to try</span>「〜てみたい」= I'd like to try ...ing. Use it for things you've never done and are curious about.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一度、富士山に登ってみたい。</span>(I'd like to try climbing Mt. Fuji once.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its て-form, then add「みる」。<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ってみる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べてみる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してみる</span></span><br>「みる」itself is a <b>Group 2 (ichidan) verb</b>, so it follows the Group 2 rules afterward: drop the る and attach the ending.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やってみる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やってみます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やってみた</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やってみたい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やってみて</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This「みる」is normally <b>written in hiragana</b>, not as 見る. It no longer means \"look with your eyes.\" So <span class='gd-jp'>聞いてみる</span> means \"try asking,\" and has nothing to do with eyes.<br>・If you really do mean \"try looking,\" that's <span class='gd-jp'>見てみる</span>. The first 見る is \"look\" and the second みる is \"try.\" It isn't a typo or a repeat.<br>・Use it only for things <b>you decide to do yourself</b>. You can't add てみる to things outside your control, like rain or illness. You can't say「雨が降ってみる」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>試してみる</span> sounds redundant, since both parts mean \"try,\" but Japanese people really do say it and it sounds natural.<br>・In a shop, <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと考えてみます</span> (I'll think about it) is often a polite \"no for now.\" Don't expect the person to come back and buy.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>てみた</b> = the focus is on <b>having tried</b>. You're usually talking about that one occasion and then giving your impression:<br><span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べてみた。けっこうおいしかった。</span> (I tried natto, and it was actually pretty good.)<br><b>たことがある</b> = the focus is on <b>whether you have the experience</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>納豆を食べたことがある。</span> (I've eaten natto.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たい</b> = simply \"want to do\": <span class='gd-jp'>沖縄に行きたい。</span> (I want to go to Okinawa.)<br><b>てみたい</b> = you've never done it, you're curious, and you want to \"try it once\": <span class='gd-jp'>沖縄に行ってみたい。</span> (I'd like to go and see Okinawa.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say \"try ...ing\" or \"give it a try\" in English, use てみる. If you mean \"have done ...\", use たことがある. If it's just \"want to ...\", use たい. If you're curious and want to experience it once, use てみたい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>These are also \"て-form + a verb\" patterns. Next, try <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまう</span> (to do something by accident / to finish doing) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜ておく</span> (to do something in advance). They're formed the same way, only the meanings differ.</div>","n4-25":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ておきます」means <b>\"do ... in advance\"</b> or <b>\"get ... ready beforehand\"</b>. Buy your tickets before a trip, chill the beer before friends come over. Japanese uses「〜ておく」for these actions you do now for the sake of what comes later. It can also mean <b>\"leave it as it is, don't touch it.\"</b></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Preparing ahead</span>You do something now for the sake of something later. This is the most common use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出張の前に切符を買っておきます。</span>(I'll buy the ticket before the business trip.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議の前に資料を読んでおきます。</span>(I'll read through the materials before the meeting.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ホテルはもう予約しておきました。</span>(I've already booked the hotel.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Leaving as is</span>You deliberately keep something in its current state and don't touch it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けておいてください。</span>(Please leave the window open.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そのままにしておいてください。</span>(Please leave it just as it is.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its て-form, then add「おく」(polite: 「おきます」).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買っておく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでおく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べておく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しておく</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The「おく」at the end conjugates too, just like the Group 1 (godan) verb <span class='gd-jp'>置く</span>:<br>Past: <span class='gd-jp'>買っておきました</span> (I bought it in advance.)<br>Making a request: <span class='gd-jp'>買っておいてください</span> (Please buy it ahead of time for me.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual contraction</span>When talking with friends,「ておく」is often shortened to「とく」, and「でおく」to「どく」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買っておく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買っとく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>置いておく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>置いとく</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んでおく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んどく</span></span><br>When asking a friend for something,「ておいて」also shortens to「といて」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言っておいて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言っといて</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ビール、冷やしとくね。</span>(I'll get the beer chilled ahead of time, okay?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>置いておいてください</span> isn't a typo with a repeated word. The first <span class='gd-jp'>置いて</span> is the action \"put,\" and the later <span class='gd-jp'>おいて</span> is ておく meaning \"for now.\" Together it means \"please just leave it there.\"<br>・The て-form after「ん」is「で」, so it's <span class='gd-jp'>読んでおく</span>, not 読んておく.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>切符を買います</span> on its own is just \"I buy a ticket.\" It has no sense of getting it done in advance. Add ておく to get that feeling.<br>・The contracted <span class='gd-jp'>買っとく</span> (buy in advance) looks like <span class='gd-jp'>買っちゃう</span> (end up buying / buy it, oops), but the meanings are completely different. Don't mix them up when listening.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>The \"leaving as is\" ておく and「〜まま」can both be translated as \"left open,\" but they feel different. Take a window that's open:<br><b>ておく</b> = you leave it open <b>on purpose</b>, with a reason: <span class='gd-jp'>暑いから、窓を開けておきました。</span> (It was hot, so I left the window open.)<br><b>まま</b> = the state didn't change, and you went on to do something else, often because you forgot or shouldn't have: <span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けたまま出かけてしまった。</span> (I went out and left the window open.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if there's a purpose and you chose to keep it that way, use ておく. If the state simply stayed unchanged and things carried on (often without you noticing), use まま.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are more \"て-form + a verb\" patterns, such as <span class='gd-jp'>〜てみる</span> (try ...ing) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜てしまう</span> (finish doing / oops). Learn them together and you'll quickly get used to this way of attaching verbs.</div>","n4-29":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜たばかりです」means <b>“have just ~”</b> or <b>“only just ~”</b>. The key is that the speaker <b>feels</b> “not much time has passed.” Say you tell a coworker “I just joined the company.” It may have been three months, but it still feels new to you, so you can say it this way.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the <b>た-form</b> of the verb and add「ばかりです」.<br>The た-form is made the same way as the て-form: change 「て」to「た」and「で」to「だ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>入って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>入った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>入ったばかりです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだばかりです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたばかりです</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来て</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来た</span>（きた）→ <span class='gd-jp'>来たばかりです</span></span><br>With friends, drop「です」or swap it for「だ」: <span class='gd-jp'>来たばかりだよ。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Really just happened</span>Something from a few minutes or a few hours ago.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さっきご飯を食べたばかりです。</span>(I just ate a little while ago.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Still feels new</span>It has actually been months or even a year, but to you it hasn’t been long.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>去年大学を卒業したばかりです。</span>(I only graduated from university last year.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ばかりの＋noun</span>“A thing that was just ~ed.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかりの傘をなくしました。</span>(I lost the umbrella I had just bought.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ばかりで</span>“Because I’ve only just ~, so…” Often used to explain that you’re still not used to things.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たばかりで、まだよくわかりません。</span>(I’ve only just come to Japan, so I don’t really understand yet.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ばかりなのに</span>“Even though it was only just ~,” with a note of frustration or complaint.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日洗ったばかりなのに、もう汚れた。</span>(I only washed it yesterday, and it’s already dirty.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Before a noun, add <b>の</b>: it’s <span class='gd-jp'>買ったばかりの靴</span>, not「買ったばかり靴」.<br>・You must use the <b>た-form</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>食べたばかり</span> means “just finished eating,” while <span class='gd-jp'>食べてばかり</span> means “eating all the time, doing nothing but eating.” The meanings are very different.<br>・You can only use it for things that have already happened. It doesn’t work for “about to go” or “will do in a bit.”<br>・Among friends it’s often shortened to <b>ばっか</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>来たばっかだよ。</span>(I only just got here.) Don’t say it this way to someone senior to you.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a similar pattern</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たところです</b> (n4-28) also means “just ~,” but it talks about <b>the objective moment right now</b>, usually within the last few minutes. <b>たばかりです</b> is about <b>how you feel</b>, and the time span can be short or long.<br>For very recent events, both work:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、家に着いたところです。</span>(I’ve just got home.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>今、家に着いたばかりです。</span>(I’ve only just got home.)<br>When the time span is longer, only ばかり works:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>先月、日本に来たばかりです。</span>(I only came to Japan last month.) ○<br>・「先月、日本に来たところです」×</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if there’s a longer time word like「先月」or「去年」, use ばかり. If you’ve literally just finished something this moment, ところ works too.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same「ばかり」with a different meaning, “nothing but ~, always ~” (n4-49): <span class='gd-jp'>弟はゲームばかりしている。</span>(My younger brother does nothing but play video games.)</div>","n4-37":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「BよりAのほうが～」means <b>“A is more ~ than B.”</b> You put two things side by side and pick the one that is “more ~.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>バスより電車のほうが速いです。</span>(Trains are faster than buses.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun</span>noun＋より、noun＋<b>の</b>ほうが</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏より冬のほうが好きです。</span>(I like winter better than summer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verb</span>dictionary form＋より、dictionary form＋ほうが (<b>no の</b> in between)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外で食べるより、家で作るほうが安いです。</span>(Cooking at home is cheaper than eating out.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>After ほうが you can use an adjective, or a feeling expression like <span class='gd-jp'>好きです</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>落ち着きます</span>.<br>You can also swap the two parts without changing the meaning. The focus is still on the ほうが side: <span class='gd-jp'>電車のほうがバスより速いです。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Answering “which one?”</span>When someone asks「AとBと、どちらが～ですか」(Which is more ~, A or B?), you answer「Aのほうが～です」. Both things have just been mentioned, so より is usually left out.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車とバスと、どちらが速いですか。</span>(Which is faster, the train or the bus?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車のほうが速いです。</span>(The train is faster.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーと紅茶と、どちらがいいですか。</span>(Coffee or tea, which would you like?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>紅茶のほうがいいです。</span>(Tea is better.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・より goes after the <b>“baseline”</b>, the side that is less ~. ほうが goes after the side that is more ~. Don’t think of it as “who wins or loses”: <span class='gd-jp'>兄より弟のほうが下手です。</span>(The younger brother is worse at it than the older brother.) In this sentence the older brother, right before より, is actually the better one.<br>・In English “than” comes <b>before</b> the thing you compare to (“faster than the bus”). In Japanese, より goes <b>after</b> バス: <span class='gd-jp'>バスより</span>.<br>・Attach ほうが directly to a verb, with no の: ×<span class='gd-jp'>歩くのほうが</span>　○<span class='gd-jp'>歩くほうが</span>. Only nouns take の: <span class='gd-jp'>電車のほうが</span>.<br>・The kanji for ほう is <span class='gd-jp'>方</span>, read ほう here, meaning “that side.” It’s different from the <span class='gd-jp'>方</span> in <span class='gd-jp'>読み方</span> (かた, “way of doing”).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>AはBより～</b>: simply describes A.<br><span class='gd-jp'>電車はバスより速いです。</span>(Trains are faster than buses.)<br><b>BよりAのほうが～</b>: puts the two side by side and stresses “it’s the A side.”<br><span class='gd-jp'>バスより電車のほうが速いです。</span>(Trains are faster than buses.)<br>The two sentences mean almost the same, but when you’re answering「どちら？」, use <b>ほうが</b>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>verb た-form＋ほうがいい</b>: gives advice, “you’d better ~.”<br><span class='gd-jp'>疲れているなら、休んだほうがいいですよ。</span>(If you’re tired, you’d better take a rest.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>タクシーより歩いたほうがいいですよ。</span>(Walking is better than taking a taxi.) ← It has より, but with the た-form it’s still advice.<br><b>AよりBのほうがいい</b>: a comparison, stating a preference.<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーより紅茶のほうがいいです。</span>(Tea is better than coffee.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: look at the verb form. 「verb た-form＋ほうがいい」(<span class='gd-jp'>休んだほうがいい</span>) is advice. 「dictionary form＋ほうが＋adjective」(<span class='gd-jp'>歩くほうが早い</span>) and「noun のほうが～」are comparisons. Note that「AよりBのほうがいいです」is also a comparison (B is better).</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To say it the other way, “B is not as ~ as A,” use「ほど…ない」: <span class='gd-jp'>バスは電車ほど速くないです。</span>(Buses are not as fast as trains.)<br>To compare three or more things and say “the most ~,” use「でいちばん」: <span class='gd-jp'>クラスで田中さんがいちばん背が高いです。</span>(Tanaka is the tallest in the class.)</div>","n4-26":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てくる」 and 「〜ていく」 work a lot like the English direction words <b>\"come\" and \"go\"</b> added to a verb. \"Come running\" is <span class='gd-jp'>走ってくる</span>, and \"take it along\" is <span class='gd-jp'>持っていく</span>. They work for time too: \"it's getting cold\" uses てくる, and \"keep doing it from now on\" uses ていく.<br>Remember this: <b>くる = toward me, building up to now; いく = away from me, from now onward</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in te-form, then add くる or いく.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降ってきた</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>持つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>持って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>持っていきます</span></span><br>The くる at the end conjugates like the normal verb「来る」: <span class='gd-jp'>てきます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>てきました</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>てこない</span>. In this pattern it's usually written in hiragana.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Toward / away from here</span>Use てくる when the action moves toward the speaker, and ていく when it moves away from the speaker.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟が走ってきた。</span>(My little brother came running.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りそうだから、傘を持っていきます。</span>(It looks like rain, so I'll take an umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Go and come back</span>てくる can also mean \"go do something, then return here.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと飲み物を買ってきます。</span>(I'll go buy a drink and be right back.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Changing up to now</span>For a gradual change from the past up to now, you usually use the past form <span class='gd-jp'>てきました</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、日本語が面白くなってきました。</span>(Lately, Japanese has been getting more and more interesting.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>From now on</span>Something that continues from now on, or keeps getting more and more ～.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからも日本語を勉強していきます。</span>(I'll keep studying Japanese from now on, too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something appears, and you notice it</span>A change starts and you become aware of it, like rain, hunger, or sleepiness.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、雨が降ってきた。</span>(Oh, it's started raining.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お腹がすいてきた。</span>(I'm starting to get hungry.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The phrase you say when leaving home, <span class='gd-jp'>行ってきます</span>, means \"I'll go and come back,\" so it means \"I'm heading out,\" not literally \"I go and come.\"<br>・Whether you use come or go depends on <b>where the speaker is</b>. If you're taking something to someone's home, English often says \"I'll bring it over to you,\" but Japanese usually says <span class='gd-jp'>持っていきます</span>, because the thing is leaving your side.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ってきた</span> means \"it has started to rain,\" not \"the rain is drifting toward me.\"<br>・The 「きた」 in <span class='gd-jp'>てきた</span> is read きた, and the negative <span class='gd-jp'>てこない</span> is read こない.<br>・In casual speech, <span class='gd-jp'>ていく</span> is often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>てく</span>, for example <span class='gd-jp'>持ってく</span>. This is spoken style.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Three ways to say it's getting cold:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒くなった。</span>(It got cold: states only the result.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒くなってきた。</span>(It's gradually getting cold: you feel the change building up.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>これから寒くなっていく。</span>(It's going to keep getting colder: looks ahead.)<br><br>Two ways to say it started raining:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降り始めた。</span>(It began to rain: simply states the start.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ってきた。</span>(It's started raining: carries a sense of \"I just noticed.\" This is the most natural thing to say when you look up and see rain.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use 「なった」 to state only the result; use 「てきた」 when you feel the change coming up to now; use 「ていく」 when your eyes are on what comes next.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Combined with 「ようになる」, it can express an ability that develops gradually: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が少し話せるようになってきた。</span>(I've gradually become able to speak a little Japanese.)</div>","n4-27":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>These three are like the English <b>\"start ~ing,\" \"keep ~ing,\" and \"finish ~ing.\"</b> They split one action into three stages: just starting, in progress, and done.<br><span class='gd-jp'>読みはじめる</span> = start reading<br><span class='gd-jp'>読み続ける</span> = keep reading, continue reading<br><span class='gd-jp'>読み終わる</span> = finish reading</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the verb in ます form, <b>drop ます</b>, and attach <span class='gd-jp'>はじめる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>続ける</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span> directly to what's left.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読みます</span></span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読みはじめる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span></span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べ続ける</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強します</span></span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強し</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強し終わる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>Once attached, the last word conjugates by its own rules:<br><span class='gd-jp'>はじめる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>続ける</span> are Group 2 (ichidan) verbs: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読みはじめる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読みはじめました</span></span><br><span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span> is a Group 1 (godan) verb: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読み終わる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読み終わりました</span></span><br>「はじめる」 can also be written with kanji as <span class='gd-jp'>始める</span>, but after a verb it's often written in hiragana.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>はじめる: start</span>An action begins at this moment. It works for things people do and for natural changes like weather.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>去年から日本語を勉強しはじめました。</span>(I started studying Japanese last year.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、雨が降りはじめた。</span>(Oh, it's started to rain.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>続ける: keep going, continue</span>The action doesn't stop and keeps going. It often appears with 「ずっと」 or a length of time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は1時間も話し続けた。</span>(He talked for a whole hour straight.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからも日本語を勉強し続けたいです。</span>(I want to keep studying Japanese from now on, too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>終わる: finish</span>Something with an endpoint gets done, like finishing a book or a meal.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本、やっと読み終わった。</span>(I finally finished this book.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べ終わったら、お皿を洗ってね。</span>(When you're done eating, wash the dishes, okay?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Always attach it to the <b>ます form with ます dropped</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>読むはじめる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>読んではじめる</span> are both wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>読みはじめる</span>.<br>・Even when the subject is a natural phenomenon like rain or wind, you still use <span class='gd-jp'>はじめる</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降りはじめた</span>. You wouldn't say 「降りはじまった」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span> is for actions that have a \"finished\" endpoint, like reading, eating, writing, or watching. You don't say 「降り終わった」 when rain stops; use <span class='gd-jp'>雨がやんだ</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span> is Group 1, so its ます form is <span class='gd-jp'>終わります</span>; <span class='gd-jp'>はじめる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>続ける</span> are Group 2, giving <span class='gd-jp'>はじめます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>続けます</span>. Don't mix up how they conjugate.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ている vs 〜続けている</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもが泣いている。</span>(The child is crying.): simply describes the crying happening now.<br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもがずっと泣き続けている。</span>(The child keeps crying and crying.): stresses that it's gone on a long time and hasn't stopped.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜終わる vs 〜てしまう</b><br>Both might come out as \"finished eating\" in English, but the focus is different.<br><span class='gd-jp'>ケーキを食べ終わった。</span>(I finished the cake.): just says the eating action is over, in a neutral tone.<br><span class='gd-jp'>弟のケーキを食べてしまった。</span>(I ate my little brother's cake.): carries a feeling of \"ate it all up\" or \"oops, I didn't mean to.\"</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ている to say something is in progress; use 続ける to stress it hasn't stopped; use 終わる to simply say it's done; use てしまう when you feel \"ate it all up\" or \"oops.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>If you want to pinpoint timing more finely, like \"about to start,\" \"in the middle of,\" or \"just finished,\" learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜ところです</span> next: <span class='gd-jp'>今から食べるところです。</span>(I'm just about to start eating.)</div>","n4-42":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようと思います」means <b>\"I'm thinking of ~ing\" or \"I plan to ~\"</b>. Use it to tell someone about a plan you already have in mind, like \"I'm thinking of cleaning my room this weekend.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は部屋を掃除しようと思います。</span>（I'm thinking of cleaning my room this weekend）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into the <b>volitional form</b> (the 「〜よう」「〜こう」 form, the \"let's ~\" form), then add <span class='gd-jp'>と思います</span>. See n4-51 for how to make the volitional form. Here's a quick review:<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to こ, add う → <span class='gd-jp'>行こうと思います</span></span><br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> → drop る, add よう → <span class='gd-jp'>寝ようと思います</span></span><br><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>：<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しようと思います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ようと思います</span>（こよう）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようと思います</span>States your plan right now. It's often a thought you've only just had.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く寝ようと思います。</span>（I'm thinking of going to bed early today）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようと思っています</span>This plan has <b>been in your mind for a while</b>. It's very common for longer-term plans.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ留学しようと思っています。</span>（I'm planning to study in Japan next year）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようかと思っています</span>With an extra 「か」 in the middle, it means <b>\"I'm wondering whether to ~\"</b>. You're still undecided.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事を辞めようかと思っています。</span>（I'm wondering whether to quit my job）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くと思います</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>行こうと思います</span> differ by only one sound, but the meanings are far apart. The first is \"I think (he) will go,\" a guess. Only the second means \"I'm planning to go.\"<br>・\"I'm <b>not</b> planning to go\" can't be said as <span class='gd-jp'>行かないと思います</span>. That means \"I think (he) won't go.\" To say you don't plan to, use <span class='gd-jp'>行かないつもりです</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>〜ようと思います</span> only works for <b>your own</b> plans. To talk about someone else's plan, switch to <span class='gd-jp'>〜ようと思っています</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>兄は車を買おうと思っています。</span>（My older brother is thinking of buying a car）<br>・When asking what the other person plans to do, <span class='gd-jp'>思っています</span> sounds more natural: <span class='gd-jp'>夏休みは何をしようと思っていますか。</span>（What are you planning to do over summer break?）</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even for the same \"going to Japan,\" these three patterns feel different:<br><b>たい</b> = simply \"want to,\" a wish in your heart, with no plan necessarily: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行きたいです。</span>（I want to go to Japan）<br><b>ようと思う</b> = \"thinking of / planning to.\" The idea is already there, but it's softer and leaves some flexibility: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行こうと思います。</span>（I'm thinking of going to Japan）<br><b>つもり</b> = the plan is firmer, almost settled: <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くつもりです。</span>（I intend to go to Japan）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if it's just \"I'd love to ~,\" use たい. If it's \"I'm thinking of ~,\" use ようと思う. If it's \"I've already planned to ~,\" use つもり. When you say \"I want to go\" in English, stop and ask yourself: is it a wish or a plan?</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Once the plan is really settled, you can use 「〜ことにします」: <span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ行くことにしました。</span>（I've decided to go to Japan next year）</div>","n4-43":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜か（どうか）」<b>tucks a question inside a sentence</b>. In English you say \"I don't know <b>whether he'll come</b>.\" The part in the middle is really a question, just not asked on its own. In Japanese you add 「か」 or 「かどうか」 after that part to signal: this is a question.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が来るかどうか、分かりません。</span>（I don't know whether he'll come）</div><div class='gd-h'>Two types: with or without a word like \"who, where, what\"</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No question word</span>When the answer to the question is just yes or no, use <b>かどうか</b>. It's the same as English \"whether ~ or not\" or \"if ~.\" Just 「か」 also works, and it's common in speech.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>鍵をかけたかどうか、覚えていない。</span>（I don't remember whether I locked the door）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末に時間があるか、確認してください。</span>（Please check whether you're free this weekend）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>With a question word</span>If the sentence already has a word like <span class='gd-jp'>誰</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>どこ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>いつ</span>, <b>add only 「か」</b>, not どうか.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議が何時に始まるか、知っていますか。</span>（Do you know what time the meeting starts?）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>トイレがどこか、教えてください。</span>（Please tell me where the restroom is）</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs that most often follow: <span class='gd-jp'>分からない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>決まる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>確認する</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>聞く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>考える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>覚えている</span>. They all mean things like knowing, checking or deciding.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Always use the <b>plain form</b> before it (the form without ます or です).<br>Verbs: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くかどうか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったかどうか</span></span><br>い-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>おいしいです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいかどうか</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives: <b>drop だ</b> and attach か directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本当です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本当かどうか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かかどうか</span></span><br>You can also say 「<span class='gd-jp'>来るか来ないか</span>」, which means the same as <span class='gd-jp'>来るかどうか</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・In English, \"whether\" or \"if\" is a separate word you can't skip, and Japanese works the same way: <b>か is required</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>彼が来る分かりません</span> is wrong.<br>・Even when the whole sentence is polite, the part in the middle stays in plain form. Put the politeness at the end: say <span class='gd-jp'>行くかどうか分かりません</span>, not 「行きますかどうか」.<br>・Don't add だ after nouns or な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>学生かどうか</span>, not 「学生だかどうか」.<br>・Don't add どうか when there's a question word: say <span class='gd-jp'>どこがいいか</span>, not 「どこがいいかどうか」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both have 「か」 and both carry some uncertainty, so beginners often mix this up with <b>かもしれない</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は来るかどうか分からない。</span>（I don't know whether he'll come: you have no idea, you're just saying \"I'm not sure about this\"）<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は来るかもしれない。</span>（He might come: you're guessing, and you think it's possible he'll come）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if a verb like 「分からない、確認する、教えて」 follows, it's かどうか. If the sentence ends right there and means \"maybe ~,\" it's かもしれない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It pairs nicely with 「〜てみます」: <span class='gd-jp'>行けるかどうか、先生に聞いてみます。</span>（I'll ask my teacher whether I can go）</div>","n4-40":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜と言います」 means <b>\"so-and-so says ~\"</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> is \"to say,\" and the <span class='gd-jp'>と</span> before it is a marker telling the listener \"the quoted content ends here.\" It works much like quotation marks in English.</div><div class='gd-h'>Two ways to say it: quote directly or report in your own words</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Direct quote</span>Put exactly what the person said inside 「」, then add <span class='gd-jp'>と言いました</span>. If the original has ます, keep the ます.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は「明日テストをします」と言いました。</span>（The teacher said, \"We'll have a test tomorrow.\"）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reporting in your own words</span>Drop the 「」, change the content to the <b>plain form</b> (the casual form with no ます or です), then add <span class='gd-jp'>と</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>テストをします</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>テストをする</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生は明日テストをすると言いました。</span>（The teacher said there will be a test tomorrow.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は行かないと言いました。</span>（He said he's not going.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>When reporting, verbs and い-adjectives take the plain form and connect directly to <span class='gd-jp'>と</span>. <b>Nouns and な-adjectives need an extra 「だ」</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いと言いました</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好きです</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きだと言いました</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生です</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だと言いました</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>と言っていました</span>The most natural way to pass on a message for someone. It means \"he did say ~, you know.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは今日は来ないと言っていました。</span>（Mr. Tanaka said he won't be coming today.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Introducing yourself</span>Saying your name.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>はじめまして、林と言います。</span>（Nice to meet you, my name is Lin.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking how to say something</span>You'll definitely use this when learning Japanese.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは日本語で何と言いますか。</span>（How do you say this in Japanese?）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When quoting the <b>content</b> of what someone said (a sentence or a longer passage), use <b>と</b>, not を: × <span class='gd-jp'>明日テストをするを言いました</span> → ○ <span class='gd-jp'>明日テストをすると言いました</span>. But when the object is a plain noun, as in <span class='gd-jp'>名前を言う</span> (say a name) or <span class='gd-jp'>お礼を言う</span> (say thanks), use を. Keep these two cases apart.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> is spelled いう, but it's actually pronounced <b>ゆう</b>.<br>・Forgetting だ after a noun or な-adjective when reporting is the most common mistake. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨だと言っていました</span>.<br>・When reporting, \"I\" has to change to the right person. If he originally said 「<span class='gd-jp'>私は行きません</span>」, your report becomes \"He said <b>he</b> isn't going.\" In Japanese just say <span class='gd-jp'>彼は行かないと言いました</span>, with no 私 needed.<br>・When reporting what a teacher or boss said in a formal setting, you'll switch to the more respectful <span class='gd-jp'>おっしゃいました</span>. You'll meet it when you study honorific language.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are two ways to report \"Mr. Tanaka isn't coming\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来ないと言っていました。</span>（Mr. Tanaka <b>said himself</b> that he isn't coming.）<br><span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは来ないそうです。</span>（<b>I hear</b> Mr. Tanaka isn't coming.）<br>「と言う」 makes clear <b>who said it</b>. 「そうです」 just means \"I hear that.\" The news may have come from Tanaka himself or from someone else, and the source isn't the point.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"so-and-so said ~,\" use と言う. To say just \"I hear ~,\" use そうです.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Swap <span class='gd-jp'>言います</span> for <span class='gd-jp'>思います</span> and you get \"I think ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨だと思います。</span>（I think it will rain tomorrow.） The connection rules are exactly the same, including adding だ.<br>Among friends, <span class='gd-jp'>と言っていた</span> is often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>って</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、来ないって。</span>（Tanaka says he's not coming.）</div>","n4-41":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜と思います」 means <b>\"I think ~\" or \"I feel ~\"</b>. Use it to give your opinion or to guess how something will turn out. Japanese people use it a lot. Even when they're quite sure, they often add と思います so the sentence doesn't sound too blunt.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving an opinion</span>Your impression or judgment of something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このラーメンはおいしいと思います。</span>（I think this ramen is delicious.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing</span>You're not 100% sure, but you think it's probably so.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降ると思います。</span>（I think it will rain tomorrow.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんはもう帰ったと思います。</span>（I think Mr. Tanaka has probably already gone home.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking for someone's opinion</span>Use 「どう思いますか」. You don't need to memorize a separate pattern.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この服、どう思いますか。</span>（What do you think of this outfit?）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form</b> before it, meaning the form without です or ます (the dictionary form, ない, た, and so on).<br>Verbs and い-adjectives connect directly:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くと思います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないと思います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いと思います</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives need <b>だ first</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生だと思います</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かだと思います</span></span><br>With friends, change 思います to <b>思う</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいと思う。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Forgetting だ after a noun or な-adjective: say <span class='gd-jp'>学生だと思います</span>, not 「学生と思います」.<br>・Don't put です or ます before と: say <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいと思います</span>, not 「おいしいですと思います」. The final 「思います」 already makes it polite enough.<br>・To say \"I don't think he'll come,\" Japanese normally puts the negative inside the quote: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は来ないと思います。</span> (literally \"I think he won't come\"). If you say <span class='gd-jp'>来るとは思いません</span>, it means \"I really don't think he'll come,\" which is much stronger and sounds like pushing back.<br>・「と思います」 is only for what <b>you</b> think. To say what someone else thinks, use 「と思っています」: <span class='gd-jp'>母は私がまだ子どもだと思っています。</span>（My mother thinks I'm still a child.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't mix it up with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>行くと思います vs 行こうと思います</b>: only one syllable apart, but the meaning is very different.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日のパーティー、田中さんは行くと思います。</span>（About tomorrow's party, I think Mr. Tanaka will go.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日のパーティー、私も行こうと思います。</span>（About tomorrow's party, I'm thinking of going too.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>Plain form before it = you're <b>guessing</b> what will happen. Volitional form before it (行こう, 食べよう) = <b>you plan</b> to do it.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>と思います vs かもしれません</b>: they differ in how sure you are.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降ると思います。</span>（I think it will rain tomorrow: fairly sure.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るかもしれません。</span>（It might rain tomorrow: fifty-fifty.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for \"I think / I guess,\" use plain form + と思います. For \"I'm planning to,\" use volitional form + と思います. For just \"maybe,\" use かもしれません.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the volitional form before it and you get \"I'm thinking of ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ行こうと思います。</span>（I'm thinking of going to Japan next year.）</div>","n4-49":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ばかり」means <b>\"all the time,\" \"nothing but,\" \"keeps on ~ing\"</b>. When a mom scolds her kid with \"All you do is play video games,\" Japanese uses ばかり. The key point: the speaker feels there's <b>too much of it, and that it's not good</b>, so it often carries a complaining or scolding tone.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Too much of the action itself</span>Verb て-form + ばかりいる: stresses \"keeps doing this and nothing else.\"<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んで</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んでばかりいる</span></span> (does nothing but play)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝て</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝てばかりいる</span></span> (does nothing but sleep)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの犬は一日中寝てばかりいる。</span> (Our dog does nothing but sleep all day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Always the same object</span>Noun + ばかり + verb: stresses \"this is all that gets done.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟はゲームばかりしている。</span> (My little brother does nothing but play video games.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はいつも文句ばかり言っている。</span> (He's always complaining.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can also add 「です／だ」 straight after the noun to describe \"everywhere you look, it's ~\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、雨ばかりですね。</span> (It's been nothing but rain lately, hasn't it?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Scolding</span>「〜てばかりいないで」 = \"stop ~ing all the time.\" It's often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>〜なさい</span>, a classic line from parents and teachers.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遊んでばかりいないで、勉強しなさい。</span> (Stop playing all the time and study.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>泣いてばかりいないで、話してよ。</span> (Stop just crying and talk to me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Self-deprecating</span>You can say it about yourself too, with a feel of \"ugh, why am I always like this?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近、食べてばかりいる。</span> (Lately all I do is eat.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When ばかり follows a noun, the original 「を」 is usually dropped: say <span class='gd-jp'>漫画ばかり読む</span>, not 「漫画をばかり読む」.<br>・It describes a state that <b>keeps repeating over a period of time</b>; it can't be used for something that happened only once.<br>・The tone is critical, so using it about your teacher, boss, or a customer is rude. Be careful.<br>・In casual conversation people often say <span class='gd-jp'>ばっかり</span>, e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>遊んでばっかり</span>. Same meaning, just more colloquial.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たばかり</b> (n4-29) = <b>just</b> finished doing something, nothing to do with frequency:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べたばかりです。</span> (I just finished eating.)<br><b>てばかりいる</b> = <b>keeps</b> doing something:<br><span class='gd-jp'>お菓子を食べてばかりいる。</span> (Keeps eating sweets all the time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>だけ</b> = <b>only</b> this, simply stating a fact with no dissatisfaction:<br><span class='gd-jp'>朝はコーヒーだけ飲みました。</span> (I only had coffee in the morning.)<br><b>ばかり</b> = <b>all the time, too much</b>, with a complaint:<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーばかり飲んでいる。</span> (Does nothing but drink coffee.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if it comes after 「た」, it means \"just did\"; if it comes after 「て」, it means \"keeps doing.\" To simply say \"only this,\" use だけ; to grumble \"why is it always this?\", use ばかり.</div>","n4-50":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜場合」means <b>\"in the case that ~\" or \"if ~ happens\"</b>. The \"In case of fire, please ~\" or \"If you have any problems, please ~\" you see in train stations, hotel rooms, and manuals is said in Japanese with 「場合」. It's more formal than 「〜たら」 and often appears in rules, notices, and explanations from staff or customer service.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Rules and notices</span>You set up a situation that \"might or might not happen\" and tell the other person what to do if it does. It's often followed by 「〜てください」 or a rule.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>地震の場合は、机の下に隠れてください。</span> (In the event of an earthquake, please hide under a desk.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨の場合は、試合は中止です。</span> (If it rains, the match is cancelled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>分からない場合は、先生に聞いてください。</span> (If you don't understand, please ask the teacher.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「場合」is almost always followed by 「は」, making 「〜場合は」. Just memorize it as a set.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly to the dictionary form, ない form, or た form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れる場合</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行けない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行けない場合</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れた場合</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>熱が高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>熱が高い場合</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: keep 「な」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>無理</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>無理な場合</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 in between. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨の場合</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>火事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>火事の場合</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dictionary form vs. た form</span>Whether you use the dictionary form or the た form changes the meaning slightly:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅れる場合は、電話してください。</span> (If you're going to be late, please call: you contact them before you're late.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>カードをなくした場合は、すぐ連絡してください。</span> (If you happen to lose your card, please contact us right away: after the thing has happened.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・「場合」is read <b>ばあい</b>, not ばしょ.<br>・Nouns and な-adjectives can't take 「だ」 directly: not 「雨だ場合」 but <span class='gd-jp'>雨の場合</span>; not 「無理だ場合」 but <span class='gd-jp'>無理な場合</span>.<br>・な-adjectives take <b>な</b>; don't confuse them with nouns and write 「の」.<br>・「場合」can only be used for things that <b>may or may not happen</b>. For something certain to arrive, like \"when it hits 10 o'clock,\" you can't say 「10時になった場合」; use <span class='gd-jp'>10時になったら</span>.<br>・You also can't use it for things that already happened: you can't say 「昨日雨が降った場合」 for \"when it rained yesterday.\"<br>・The tone is fairly formal. When chatting with friends and saying \"if it rains, we won't go,\" <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったら</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both mean \"if it rains\":<br><b>場合</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨の場合は、イベントは中止です。</span> (In case of rain, the event is cancelled.) → Sounds like a rule written on a notice.<br><b>たら</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が降ったら、家で映画を見よう。</span> (If it rains, let's watch a movie at home.) → Casual talk between friends.<br>Also, 「たら」 can be used for things that will definitely happen (<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話して。</span> Call me when you get to the station), but 「場合」 can't.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if you can translate it as \"in the event of ~\" or \"should ~ occur,\" and you're talking about rules or instructions, use 「場合」. For everyday chat, or things that will definitely happen, use 「たら」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other ways of saying \"if\" are worth comparing together: <span class='gd-jp'>〜ば</span> (conditional form), <span class='gd-jp'>〜なら</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>〜と</span>. Each has a slightly different feel.</div>","n4-44":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Feelings like “want,” “scared,” and “cold” are known only to the person feeling them. So when Japanese talks about <b>someone else's</b> feelings, it doesn't flatly say “he wants it.” It says “he <b>shows signs of</b> wanting it.” That's 「〜がる」. It's a bit like English “he keeps asking for…” or “he acts scared.”</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>What someone else wants</span>A younger brother, a friend, a child, even a pet: you can see they really want something or really want to do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟が新しいゲーム機を欲しがっています。</span>(My little brother keeps wanting a new game console.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女はずっと日本へ行きたがっています。</span>(She has really wanted to go to Japan for a long time.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Someone else's feelings</span>Fear, cold, pain, dislike: you can tell from their expression and behavior.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもが注射を怖がっています。</span>(The child is scared of shots.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>娘は野菜を嫌がって、食べません。</span>(My daughter hates vegetables and won't eat them.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Only adjectives that express emotions or sensations can take 「がる」 (<span class='gd-jp'>欲しい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>怖い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>痛い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>嫌だ</span>…).<br><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add 「がる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>欲しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>欲しがる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>怖い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>怖がる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>痛い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>痛がる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Wanting to do something</b>: first change the verb to 「〜たい」, then drop the 「い」 and add 「がる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行きたがる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>働く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>働きたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>働きたがる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: drop 「だ」 and add 「がる」, for example <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>嫌だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>嫌がる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>残念だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>残念がる</span></span>. Not many な-adjectives work this way. The common ones are <span class='gd-jp'>嫌がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>残念がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>不思議がる</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>面倒がる</span>.<br>Note: <span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> isn't a feeling, so you can't say ×<span class='gd-jp'>大きがる</span>. And although <span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> is an emotion, you don't say ×<span class='gd-jp'>好きがる</span> either.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Once it becomes 「がる」, it conjugates like <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, as a Group 1 (godan) verb: <span class='gd-jp'>がります</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>がりません</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>がっています</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>がった</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>がっています</span>To say “right now he really wants it / is really scared,” you'll almost always use this form. Just saying <span class='gd-jp'>欲しがります</span> sounds more like a habit or tendency: “he's the type who does this.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犬が散歩に行きたがっている。</span>(The dog wants to go for a walk.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't use がる for yourself</b>: for your own feelings, just say <span class='gd-jp'>私は新しいスマホが欲しいです</span>, not 「私は欲しがっています」. The reverse also holds. In English “he wants it” is perfectly natural, but saying <span class='gd-jp'>弟は欲しいです</span> directly in Japanese sounds odd. You need 欲しがっています.<br>・<b>The particle changes</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>水が欲しい</span> takes 「を」 once it becomes がる: <span class='gd-jp'>弟は水を欲しがっている</span>.<br>・<b>Don't use がる when asking the person you're talking to</b>: with a friend, just ask <span class='gd-jp'>何が食べたい？</span> (What do you want to eat?).<br>・<b>Don't use it for superiors or elders</b>: 「〜がる」 describes how someone appears from the outside, so using it about a teacher or boss sounds like you're commenting on their feelings, which isn't polite enough. Don't say 先生が帰りたがっています. A better way is <span class='gd-jp'>先生は帰りたいとおっしゃっていました。</span> (The teacher said he wanted to go home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>For “my little brother wants a new phone,” there are three ways to say it, and they differ in <b>whose feeling it is and how you know</b>:<br><b>欲しい</b> = your own feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>私は新しいスマホが欲しいです。</span> (I want a new phone.)<br><b>欲しがっている</b> = he has said it or shown it: <span class='gd-jp'>弟は新しいスマホを欲しがっています。</span> (My little brother keeps asking for a new phone.)<br><b>欲しそう</b> = he hasn't said anything; you're guessing from how he looks: <span class='gd-jp'>弟は新しいスマホが欲しそうです。</span> (My little brother looks like he wants a new phone.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use 「たい・欲しい」 for your own feelings, 「がっている」 for what someone else has shown, and 「そう」 when you're only guessing from their face.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you'd rather not comment on someone's feelings, you can report what they said instead: <span class='gd-jp'>姉は留学したいと言っています。</span> (My older sister says she wants to study abroad.)</div>","n4-46":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜なさい」 is like a parent saying <b>“Go to bed, now!” or “Wash your hands properly!”</b> It carries an adult's authority, but it isn't scolding. It's a command from <b>a higher position to a lower one</b>, most often from parents to children and teachers to students.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Parent to child</span>Telling a child to do what they should be doing. Moms in Japanese dramas and anime say this every day.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く寝なさい。</span>(Go to bed, now.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題を先にやりなさい。</span>(Do your homework first.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Teacher to student</span>Instructions in class.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よく聞きなさい。</span>(Listen carefully.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Exam questions</span>The JLPT and Japanese school tests often word questions this way. When you see it, read it as a “please do…” instruction.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>正しいものを選びなさい。</span>(Choose the correct answer.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in its ます form, <b>drop ます</b>, and add なさい. It's the same for all three verb groups, so there's no extra rule to memorize.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>行きなさい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>洗います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>洗いなさい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べなさい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝なさい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しなさい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来なさい</span> (きなさい)</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It doesn't sound harsh, but it is <b>not polite</b>. Saying 「<span class='gd-jp'>座りなさい</span>」 to a teacher, boss, or customer would be very rude. To ask someone to do something politely, use 「<span class='gd-jp'>座ってください</span>」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来なさい</span> is read <b>きなさい</b>, not こなさい.<br>・To tell someone “don't…,” なさい has no negative form, so you can't just attach ない. Use 「〜ないで」 or 「〜てはいけません」 instead, for example <span class='gd-jp'>走らないで</span> (don't run).<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>おやすみなさい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>おかえりなさい</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>ごめんなさい</span> do contain なさい, but they're fixed greetings and have no command meaning. Still, <span class='gd-jp'>ごめんなさい</span> is rather casual. To apologize to a boss or customer, use 「<span class='gd-jp'>すみません</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>申し訳ありません</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even for the same “go to bed early,” changing the form changes the tone and the relationship between speaker and listener:<br><b>〜なさい</b> = from above, with a tone of discipline (mom to child):</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もう11時だよ。早く寝なさい。</span>(It's already 11. Go to bed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Command form</b> = direct and forceful, mostly used by men, or in arguments and emergencies:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く寝ろ。</span>(Go to bed, already!)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜てください</b> = a polite request, used by doctors to patients, shop staff to customers, and so on:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く寝てくださいね。</span>(Please get some rest early today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜てね</b> = a light reminder between friends or family:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く寝てね。</span>(Go to bed early today, okay?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use なさい when an adult is “disciplining” a child; use てください to politely ask for help; use てね for a casual reminder to a friend; ろ is the harshest, so beginners should avoid it.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, learn the “command form and prohibitive form” (<span class='gd-jp'>寝ろ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>寝るな</span>). You'll understand the more direct commands in Japanese dramas, and see more clearly why なさい counts as “mild.”</div>","n4-51":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The volitional form is the <b>casual version</b> of 「〜ましょう」. With a coworker you say <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に行きましょう</span>, and with a friend you say <span class='gd-jp'>一緒に行こう</span>. The meaning is the same, \"let's…\", but the tone is more relaxed.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Inviting someone</span>Ask the other person to do something together with you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一緒に行こう。</span>(Let's go together.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと休もう。</span>(Let's take a short break.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Psyching yourself up</span>Use it to make a decision in your head or talk to yourself. No one else needs to be around.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よし、明日から早く起きよう。</span>(Okay, starting tomorrow I'll get up early.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Offering to help</span>Add 「か」 at the end and it becomes \"Shall I… for you?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>荷物、持とうか。</span>(Shall I carry your bags?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「お」-row sound of the same row, then add 「う」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to こ → <span class='gd-jp'>行こう</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲もう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待とう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話そう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰ろう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会おう</span></span><br>You can also think of it from the ます-form: change the 「き」 of <span class='gd-jp'>行きます</span> to 「こ」, then add 「う」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「よう」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べよう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きよう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見よう</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (just two verbs, memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しよう</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来よう</span>（こよう）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The こう in 「行こう」 is a long vowel, so it sounds like 「いこー」. Saying it short, 「いこ」, is a very cutesy, casual way of talking, with a different tone.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>会おう</span>. Don't mix it up with the ない-form <span class='gd-jp'>会わない</span> and write 「会わう」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1: it's <span class='gd-jp'>帰ろう</span>, not 帰よう.<br>・The volitional form of 来る is read <b>こよう</b>, not きよう.<br>・It can only express what <b>you yourself</b> will do, or <b>inviting the other person to do it with you</b>. You can't use it to tell someone else to go do something. To say \"let's invite our friend too\", change it to <span class='gd-jp'>友達も呼ぼう</span> (let's call our friend too).<br>・This is how friends talk to each other. With a teacher or a customer, go back to 「〜ましょう」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>There are three ways to invite a friend to a movie:<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見に行こう。</span> (Let's go see a movie.): a direct invitation, with a feel of \"it's settled, then!\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見に行かない？</span> (Want to go see a movie?): you ask whether they want to go first, which leaves them more room.<br><span class='gd-jp'>映画を見に行きましょう。</span> (Let's go see a movie.): the polite version of 「行こう」, for people you don't know well or for elders.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Rule of thumb: if the person is a friend and you expect them to say yes, use 「行こう」. If you want to check first, use 「行かない？」. If the person calls for politeness, use 「行きましょう」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「と思います」 after it and it means \"I'm thinking of…\" or \"I plan to…\": <span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ行こうと思います。</span> (I'm thinking of going to Japan next year.)</div>","n4-52":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The potential form is the English <b>\"can…\" / \"be able to…\"</b>, built right into the verb. <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> means \"to write\", and <span class='gd-jp'>書ける</span> means \"can write, be able to write\". When Japanese people talk about whether they can or can't do something, this is the form they use most.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can (a learned skill)</span>You studied or practiced it, so you can do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>韓国語が書けます。</span>(I can write Korean.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ピアノが弾けますか。</span>(Can you play the piano?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can / may (circumstances allow)</span>This isn't about skill, but about whether time, place, or rules allow it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ここで写真が撮れますか。</span>(Can I take photos here?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は来られますか。</span>(Can you come tomorrow?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can eat, can stomach</span>With food, it often means \"can you stomach it?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>納豆が食べられますか。</span>(Can you eat natto?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「え」-row sound of the same row, then add 「る」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>書ける</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲める</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買える</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待てる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰れる</span></span><br>You can also think of it from the ます-form: change the 「き」 of <span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> to 「け」 → <span class='gd-jp'>書けます</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「られる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きられる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (just two verbs, memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span>（こられる）</span><br>Verbs of the 「noun + する」 type work the same way: replace する with できる: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>説明する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>説明できる</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Once formed, keep conjugating</div><div class='gd-sec'>After you form the potential, <b>it is itself a Group 2 verb</b>. To say \"can't\" or \"was able to\", just keep conjugating it by the Group 2 rules:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書けます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書けない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書けた</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝、なかなか起きられない。</span>(I just can't get up in the morning.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Particles: を often becomes が</div><div class='gd-sec'>After a verb becomes potential, the original 「を」 usually changes to 「が」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せる</span></span><br>You'll also hear を used in everyday conversation, but textbooks and exams mainly use が, so remembering <b>が</b> is the safest.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually Group 1: it's <span class='gd-jp'>帰れる</span>, not 帰られる.<br>・The potential form of 来る is read <b>こられる</b>, not きられる.<br>・The potential of する is the whole replacement <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span>. You can't force it through the rules yourself.<br>・In casual speech you often hear <span class='gd-jp'>食べれる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>見れる</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>来れる</span> (without the 「ら」). Japanese people really do say this all the time, but in textbooks, exams, and formal situations you should still use <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span>.<br>・When something is visible or audible <b>naturally, without any effort</b>, Japanese uses <span class='gd-jp'>見える</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>聞こえる</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>ここから富士山が見える。</span> (You can see Mt. Fuji from here.) <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>聞ける</span> mean <b>having the chance or means to go watch or listen</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>このアプリで映画が見られる。</span> (You can watch movies with this app.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>〜ことができる</span>, which you learned earlier, means the same as the potential form:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せます。</span> (I can speak Japanese.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すことができます。</span> (I am able to speak Japanese.)<br>The difference is length and feel. The potential form is shorter and the most common in everyday chat. <span class='gd-jp'>ことができる</span> is longer and more formal, and you often see it in manuals, notices, and speeches. One more point: the verb before <span class='gd-jp'>ことができる</span> doesn't change form, and the particle stays 「を」.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Rule of thumb: use the potential form in everyday speech. When you want to sound more formal and clear, use ことができる. They mean the same thing, so neither is wrong.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「ようになる」 after it and it means \"have become able to…\": <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が少し話せるようになりました。</span> (I've become able to speak a little Japanese.)</div>","n4-53":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>The imperative is the bare shout of \"<b>Do it!</b>\", and the prohibitive is \"<b>Don't!</b>\". Think of English \"Run!\" or \"Don't come in!\", with no politeness at all. Said to a real person in daily life, both sound harsh. You'll hear them most in anime, sports, and emergencies.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Emergencies, danger</span>There's no time to be polite.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>危ない！逃げろ！</span>(Watch out! Run!)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Cheering someone on</span>Shouted from the sidelines. This doesn't sound harsh, and both men and women do it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>頑張れ！負けるな！</span>(Go for it! Don't lose!)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reporting what someone told you</span>Add 「と言われた」 to say \"someone told me to ~\". This is the case you'll use most yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医者にお酒を飲むなと言われた。</span>(The doctor told me not to drink alcohol.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父に早く寝ろと言われた。</span>(My dad told me to go to bed early.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Signs, superior to subordinate</span>The 「<span class='gd-jp'>止まれ</span>」 (Stop) written on roads is an imperative. You'll also hear it in arguments, or when a coach yells at players.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Imperative</b><br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the 「え」-row sound of the same row.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>行け</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>走れ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲め</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待て</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急げ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final 「る」 and add 「ろ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べろ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>起きろ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ろ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しろ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来い</span>（こい）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The <b>prohibitive</b> is much simpler. It doesn't depend on the group: just <b>add 「な」 to the dictionary form</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くな</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べるな</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>するな</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るな</span>（くるな）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Using the imperative with a teacher, boss, or customer is very rude. Don't take 「<span class='gd-jp'>待て！</span>」 and 「<span class='gd-jp'>見ろ！</span>」 from anime and use them on real people.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> are Group 1, so they become <span class='gd-jp'>帰れ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>入れ</span>, not 帰ろ or 入ろ.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来い</span> is read <b>こい</b>, but <span class='gd-jp'>来るな</span> is read <b>くるな</b>. The two readings differ.<br>・The imperative looks a lot like the potential form: <span class='gd-jp'>読め</span> means \"read it!\", while <span class='gd-jp'>読める</span> means \"can read it\". The difference is whether there's a final 「る」.<br>・「な」 must follow the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>行くな</span> means \"don't go\". If you drop ます and add な instead, as in <span class='gd-jp'>行きな</span>, it's a colloquial \"go on, go\", which means the exact opposite.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to tell a kid to go to bed:<br><span class='gd-jp'>早く寝ろ！</span> (Get to bed, now!): the harshest, a straight-up shout.<br><span class='gd-jp'>早く寝なさい。</span> (Go to bed.): the tone parents and teachers use with children. It has a top-down feel but isn't rude.<br><span class='gd-jp'>早く寝てね。</span> (Go to bed early, okay?): an ordinary, gentle request.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to say not to enter a room:<br><span class='gd-jp'>入るな！</span> (Don't come in!): the harshest.<br><span class='gd-jp'>入ってはいけません。</span> (You must not go in.): states a rule or explains a ban, in a more formal tone.<br><span class='gd-jp'>入らないでください。</span> (Please don't go in.): a polite request.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: shouting uses the imperative or prohibitive; an adult managing a child uses なさい; stating rules uses てはいけません; asking someone a favor uses てください or ないでください.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The imperative of <span class='gd-jp'>くれる</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>くれ</span>, not くれろ. Men often say <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと待ってくれ。</span> (Wait a sec for me), which is a bit softer than just 「待て」.</div>","n4-57":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「と」 means <b>\"whenever ~ / as soon as ~, then ~\"</b>. Whenever A happens, B <b>follows every time</b>, with no exceptions, like \"press the switch and the light comes on\". That's why it's used most for natural patterns, how machines work, giving directions, and fixed habits.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Natural patterns</span>Things that just happen that way, and no one can change them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春になると、桜が咲きます。</span>(When spring comes, the cherry blossoms bloom.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜になると、街の灯りがつきます。</span>(When night falls, the street lights come on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Operating machines</span>You do this action, and the machine responds.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このボタンを押すと、ドアが開きます。</span>(When you press this button, the door opens.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この鍵を回すと、金庫が開きます。</span>(When you turn this key, the safe opens.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving directions</span>You'll hear this pattern a lot when asking the way in Japan.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>まっすぐ行くと、右に駅があります。</span>(Go straight and the station is on your right.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Fixed habits</span>Every time you do A, you always go on to do B.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父は家に帰ると、すぐテレビをつけます。</span>(As soon as my dad gets home, he turns on the TV.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the <b>plain form, with no ます and no past tense,</b> before 「と」. Verbs most often use the dictionary form, and the ない form for \"not ~\".<br>Verb dictionary form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>押す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>押すと</span></span><br>Verb ない form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>急がない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>急がないと</span></span><br>い-adjectives attach directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いと</span></span><br>な-adjectives and nouns need だ first: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だと</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>急がないと、遅れるよ。</span>(If you don't hurry, you'll be late.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>寒いと、手が冷たくなります。</span>(When it's cold, my hands get icy.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After 「と」 you <b>can't use</b> requests, commands, invitations, or your own intentions like \"I'll ~\". 「と」 describes a result that happens naturally, not something you want to do.<br>　×<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着くと、電話してください。</span><br>　○<span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたら、電話してください。</span>(When you get to the station, please call me.)<br>・Before 「と」 you always use the <b>non-past form (dictionary form or ない form)</b>, even when talking about the past. The past tense goes only at the end of the sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>子どものころ、冬になると、よく雪が降りました。</span> (When I was a kid, it often snowed when winter came.) You can't say 「押したと」.<br>・「と」 also means \"with\" (<span class='gd-jp'>友達と</span>) and marks a quote (<span class='gd-jp'>と言います</span>). It's only this \"whenever ~, then ~\" pattern when one action or clause is followed by another event.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>と</b> = A, then B, every time. Only things that will happen go after it: <span class='gd-jp'>春になると、桜が咲きます。</span> (When spring comes, the cherry blossoms bloom.)<br><b>たら</b> = after A happens, do B. Requests, invitations, and your own plans can follow: <span class='gd-jp'>春になったら、花見に行こう。</span> (When spring comes, let's go see the blossoms.)<br><b>ば</b> = stresses \"as long as condition A is met\": <span class='gd-jp'>このボタンを押せば、ドアが開きます。</span> (If you just press this button, the door opens.)<br><b>なら</b> = picks up what the other person said and gives advice: <span class='gd-jp'>京都へ行くなら、春がいいですよ。</span> (If you're going to Kyoto, spring is nice.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if what follows is \"please ~\", \"let's ~\", or \"I'll ~\", use たら. If it's a result that \"always happens\", use と.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「と」 can also go before 「いい」 to express a hope: <span class='gd-jp'>明日、晴れるといいですね。</span> (I hope it clears up tomorrow.) This usage is covered in n4-58.</div>","n4-67":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take a question word like 「何、誰、どこ、いつ」 and add 「でも」, and it becomes English <b>\"anything\" / \"anyone\" / \"anywhere\" / \"anytime\"</b>. It means everything is fine, with no exceptions.</div><div class='gd-h'>What you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>何でも</span>＝anything<br><span class='gd-jp'>誰でも</span>＝anyone<br><span class='gd-jp'>どこでも</span>＝anywhere<br><span class='gd-jp'>いつでも</span>＝anytime, whenever<br><span class='gd-jp'>どれでも</span>＝any one (choosing from three or more)<br><span class='gd-jp'>どちらでも</span>＝either one (choosing from two)<br><span class='gd-jp'>どんな</span>＋noun＋<span class='gd-jp'>でも</span>＝any kind of ~, no matter what kind of ~</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は何でも食べます。</span>(I eat anything.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この問題は誰でもできます。</span>(Anyone can do this problem.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いつでも連絡してください。</span>(Contact me anytime.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どんな仕事でも頑張ります。</span>(I'll work hard at any job.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Answering \"anything is fine\"</span>Add 「いいです」 after it and you get the super common \"any is fine\" reply.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>A：いつがいいですか。　B：いつでもいいですよ。</span>(A: When works for you?　B: Anytime is fine!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>赤と青、どちらでもいいです。</span>(Red or blue, either one is fine.)</div><div class='gd-h'>What happens to the original particle?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>が, を</b>: they simply disappear. Don't keep them.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>何を食べますか</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>何でも食べます</span></span> (you never say 何をでも)<br><b>に, と, へ</b> and the like: keep them, and put them before でも.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は誰にでも優しいです。</span>(He's kind to everyone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>妹は誰とでもすぐ友だちになれます。</span>(My younger sister can become friends with anyone right away.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何でも</span> is read <b>なんでも</b>, not なにでも.<br>・To say \"not ~ anything\" you can't use でも. Use 「も」 plus a negative: <span class='gd-jp'>何も食べません</span> (I don't eat anything). <span class='gd-jp'>何でも食べません</span> does not mean \"I don't eat anything\", so don't say it that way.<br>・If a friend sincerely asks \"What do you want to eat?\" and you just flatly answer <span class='gd-jp'>何でもいいです</span>, it can sound a bit like \"whatever.\" Try <span class='gd-jp'>何でも好きです</span> (I like everything), or just suggest something.<br>・When there's no question word before it, でも means something else: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもでもわかります</span> means \"Even a child can understand.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>With the same 「何」, the word that follows changes the meaning a lot:<br><span class='gd-jp'>何でも食べます。</span>(I eat anything: everything is OK)<br><span class='gd-jp'>何も食べません。</span>(I eat nothing: not a single thing)<br><span class='gd-jp'>何か食べます。</span>(I'll eat something: some item, not decided yet)<br><br>「誰」 works the same way:<br><span class='gd-jp'>誰でも知っています。</span>(Anyone knows.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>誰も知りません。</span>(Nobody knows.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>誰か知っていますか。</span>(Does anyone know?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: \"everything is OK\" takes <b>でも</b>; \"not a single one\" takes <b>も</b> with a negative; \"some one, but I'm not sure which\" takes <b>か</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A question word followed by a verb in 「ても」 form means \"no matter what you do ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>何を言っても、彼は聞きません。</span>(No matter what you say, he won't listen.)</div>","n4-68":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「とか」 is like English casual <b>\"~ or something,\" \"like ~ and stuff,\" \"things like ~\"</b>. You toss out a few examples, meaning \"not just these, there's more.\" It's more casual than 「や」, and you'll hear it all the time among friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual examples</span>Give two or three examples to show there are others too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>コンビニでパンとかおにぎりとか買った。</span>(I bought bread, rice balls, and stuff at the convenience store.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just one, to soften your tone</span>When inviting someone or giving advice, even one example is fine. It sounds less pushy, like English \"Want to grab dinner or something?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今度、ご飯とか行かない？</span>(Want to go grab a meal or something sometime?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>疲れたら、少し寝るとかしたほうがいいよ。</span>(If you're tired, you should maybe take a little nap or something.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reporting something you heard</span>「～とか言ってた」＝\"I think (he/she) said ~.\" It shows you remember roughly, but you're not totally sure.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、来週休むとか言ってたよ。</span>(I think Tanaka said he's taking next week off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: just add とか. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>パン</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>パンとかおにぎりとか</span></span><br><b>Verbs</b>: use the plain form without ます (dictionary form), then add とか. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝ます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝るとか</span></span><br>After the last とか you can add 「を」「に」「が」, but in casual talk 「を」 is often just dropped: <span class='gd-jp'>お茶とかお菓子とか買ってきた。</span>(I bought tea, snacks, and stuff and came back.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Where people slip up</div><div class='gd-warn'>・とか is very casual. In reports, or when talking to your boss or customers, use 「や」 for nouns and 「〜たり〜たりする」 for actions to be safe.<br>・The most common pattern puts とか after every item (<span class='gd-jp'>AとかBとか</span>). You'll also hear it only after the first one, but as soon as 「とか」 appears, it means there's more, not that this is everything.<br>・「とか」 and 「と」 differ by just one syllable, but the meaning is very different: <span class='gd-jp'>と</span> means \"just these,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>とか</span> means \"a few examples.\"<br>・Some people stick とか into every sentence, which can sound vague and noncommittal. Don't overuse it in formal situations.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All describing what's in the fridge:<br><b>と</b>＝lists everything, just these: <span class='gd-jp'>冷蔵庫に牛乳と卵があります。</span>(There's milk and eggs in the fridge.)<br><b>や</b>＝examples, and there's more, standard register: <span class='gd-jp'>冷蔵庫に牛乳や卵があります。</span>(There's milk, eggs, and so on in the fridge.)<br><b>とか</b>＝examples, and there's more, casual tone: <span class='gd-jp'>冷蔵庫に牛乳とか卵とかあるよ。</span>(There's milk and eggs and stuff in the fridge.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, 「や」 can only attach to nouns. To list <b>actions</b>, the standard way is <b>「〜たり〜たりする」</b>; in casual talk you can use とか too, but it's more colloquial.<br><span class='gd-jp'>週末は本を読んだり、音楽を聞いたりします。</span>(On weekends I read books, listen to music, and so on: standard.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>週末は本を読むとか、音楽を聞くとかしてる。</span>(On weekends I read or listen to music or something: casual.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"just these,\" use 「と」. For example nouns, use 「や」 in a more formal tone and 「とか」 in casual talk. For example actions, use 「たり」 normally, and 「とか」 is fine in casual talk.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another way to list things one by one: the particle <span class='gd-jp'>し</span> lists <b>reasons</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>安いし、おいしいし、よくこの店に来る。</span>(It's cheap and tasty, so I come to this place often.)</div>","n4-58":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This pattern is the Japanese way of saying <b>\"you could just ~\" or \"I hope ~\"</b>. It has two uses: <b>giving advice</b> (\"Just ask your teacher\") and <b>making a wish</b> (\"I hope it clears up tomorrow\"). Before it you can use any of the three conditional forms, たら, ば or と, and いい follows.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving advice</span>You're telling the other person, \"Do this and it'll be solved.\" The tone is light, and it often comes with 「よ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>医者に相談したらいいですよ。</span>(You could just talk to a doctor.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>分からなかったら、先生に聞けばいいよ。</span>(If you don't understand, just ask the teacher.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>疲れたときは、少し休むといいですよ。</span>(When you're tired, it's a good idea to rest a little.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking what to do</span>Turn it into a question and it means \"What should I do?\" Here it's almost always たら or ば, and it's very common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅までどう行けばいいですか。</span>(How should I get to the station?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何を持っていったらいいですか。</span>(What should I bring?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Making a wish</span>You hope something will happen, usually something you can't control. It often comes with 「ね」 (talking to someone) or 「なあ」 (talking to yourself). 「といい」 is especially common for this use.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日、晴れるといいですね。</span>(I hope it clears up tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨が降らないといいね。</span>(I hope it doesn't rain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試験に合格できたらいいなあ。</span>(It would be great if I could pass the exam.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>たら</b>: Make the た-form first, then add ら.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べたら</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ば</b>: Make the ば-form.<br>Group 1 (godan verbs): change the last sound to the え-row sound of the same row, then add ば.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to け → <span class='gd-jp'>行けば</span></span><br>Group 2 (ichidan verbs): drop る and add れば.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べれば</span></span><br>Group 3 (する・来る): <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>すれば</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来れば</span>（くれば）</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>と</b>: Use the <b>non-past plain form</b>, meaning the dictionary form or the ない form. な-adjectives and nouns need だ.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>晴れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>晴れるといい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>降らない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>降らないといい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>元気</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>元気だといい</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Adding のに: a pity it isn't so</div><div class='gd-sec'>When the sentence ends in 「ばいいのに」 or 「たらいいのに」, it means \"if only ~, but it's not that way,\" with a sense of regret or dissatisfaction.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く春が来ればいいのに。</span>(I wish spring would hurry up and come. It hasn't yet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く言えばいいのに。</span>(You could have just said so sooner!)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't put the た-form before 「と」: <span class='gd-jp'>飲むといい</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>降らないといい</span> are correct, but <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだといい</span> is wrong.<br>・「～たらいいですよ」 has a \"let me tell you what to do\" feel, so it can come across as a bit blunt to a teacher or boss. <span class='gd-jp'>〜たらいかがですか</span> is more polite.<br>・「～ばいいのに」 aimed at another person often carries a complaint. <span class='gd-jp'>早く言えばいいのに</span> is basically scolding the listener, so don't use it with people above you.<br>・Don't translate \"I hope it's sunny tomorrow\" word for word as 「希望します」. In everyday Japanese people just say <span class='gd-jp'>明日晴れるといいですね</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three end in 「いい」, but the meanings differ:<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだほうがいいですよ。</span>(You'd better take medicine.) → <b>ほうがいい</b> is a recommendation that implies \"it's bad if you don't,\" so it's stronger.<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだらいいですよ。</span>(You could just take some medicine.) → <b>たらいい</b> casually offers a way to solve it.<br><span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んでもいいですよ。</span>(You may take medicine.) → <b>てもいい</b> gives permission, not advice.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"you'd better ~\" is ほうがいい, \"you could just ~\" is たらいい, and \"you may ~\" is てもいい.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The past form <span class='gd-jp'>〜ばよかった</span> means \"I should have ~\" and expresses regret: <span class='gd-jp'>もっと勉強すればよかった。</span>(I wish I had studied more.)</div>","n4-64":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜させていただきます」 is like English <b>\"Allow me to ~\" or \"I'll go ahead and ~\"</b>. It talks about something <b>you</b> are going to do, but the tone is very humble, as if you're doing it only because the other person kindly allows it. You'll hear it often in meetings, on stage, and when speaking to superiors or customers.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>On stage, formal occasions</span>Say it first when you're about to explain something, introduce yourself, or give a presentation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>では、説明させていただきます。</span>(Well then, allow me to explain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>自己紹介させていただきます。</span>(Allow me to introduce myself.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Things that affect the other person</span>For things that affect others, like taking a day off, leaving early, or using someone's belongings, this makes you sound more polite.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は休ませていただきます。</span>(I'll be taking tomorrow off, if I may.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Shop notices</span>You'll also see it often on signs posted at shop entrances.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本日は休ませていただきます。</span>(We are closed today.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First turn the verb into the causative form (\"let/make someone do\"), then into the て-form 「〜させて／〜せて」, and add <span class='gd-jp'>いただきます</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to conjugate: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: Change the last sound to the あ-row sound of the same row, add 「せて」, then add いただきます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → change む to ま → <span class='gd-jp'>休ませていただきます</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰らせていただきます</span></span><br>Watch out for verbs ending in 「う」: the 「う」 becomes 「わ」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>使う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使わせていただきます</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: Drop the final 「る」, add 「させて」, then add いただきます.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>考える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>考えさせていただきます</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>させていただきます</span></span> (<span class='gd-jp'>説明する</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>説明させていただきます</span>)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来させていただきます</span>（こさせて）</span>, which is used less often.</div><div class='gd-sec'>To sum up: <b>only Group 2 and Group 3 (する → させて, 来る → 来させて) have 「さ」</b>. Group 1 is simply the あ-row sound + せて.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add an extra 「さ」 to Group 1: it's <span class='gd-jp'>休ませていただきます</span>, not 休まさせていただきます, and <span class='gd-jp'>読ませていただきます</span>, not 読まさせて. Even native speakers often make this mistake, but it counts as wrong on exams.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>使う</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>使わせて</span>, not 使あせて.<br>・It always talks about something <b>you</b> will do. You can't use it for other people.<br>・It isn't really \"asking\"; it's closer to politely <b>announcing</b> \"I'm going to do this.\" If you actually want permission, use 「〜させていただけませんか」 below.<br>・Don't use it for everything. For things that don't involve the other person and need nobody's approval, saying 「させていただきます」 over and over sounds roundabout. Plain 「〜ます」 is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you want to leave work early today. There are three ways to put it to your boss:<br><b>させていただきます</b> = a polite announcement, as if it's already decided:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く帰らせていただきます。</span>(I'll be leaving early today, if I may.)<br><b>させてください</b> = asking the other person to let you, more direct:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く帰らせてください。</span>(Please let me leave early today.)<br><b>させていただけませんか</b> = a real question that the other person can refuse, the most polite:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は早く帰らせていただけませんか。</span>(Could I possibly leave early today?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: an ending in 「ます」 <b>announces</b>, 「ください」 <b>requests</b>, and only 「ませんか」 truly <b>asks</b>. Until you've got approval, 「〜させていただけませんか」 is the safest choice.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at humble language (謙讓語). Special humble forms that lower yourself, like <span class='gd-jp'>参ります</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>申します</span>, often appear together with 「させていただきます」.</div>","n4-65":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English you soften “What’s your name?” into “May I ask your name?” Japanese does something similar: put <b>お</b> or <b>ご</b> before a noun and it sounds more polite and refined.<br>「<span class='gd-jp'>ございます</span>」 is the <b>shop-staff / hotel version</b> of <span class='gd-jp'>あります</span>. It means the same thing, “there is / I have,” just more politely.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use お・ご</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The other person’s things</span>When you talk about the other person’s name, family or address, adding お・ご shows respect.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お名前をお願いします。</span>（May I have your name, please?）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ご家族はお元気ですか。</span>（Is your family doing well?）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Just sounding nice</span>Some words take お・ご simply to sound more refined. You can use these for your own things too, and they’re very common in daily life.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お茶を飲みませんか。</span>（Would you like some tea?）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もうご飯を食べました。</span>（I’ve already eaten.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Which one: お or ご?</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Native Japanese words</b> (kanji with Japanese readings, or words that are already in hiragana) mostly take <b>お</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>お名前</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>お店</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>お水</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>お手紙</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Words borrowed from Chinese</b> (kanji read with the Chinese-derived on’yomi readings, often two-kanji compounds) mostly take <b>ご</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ご住所</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>ご家族</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>ご予約</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>ご連絡</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>But there are <b>quite a few exceptions</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>お電話</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>お時間</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>お食事</span> are all kanji compounds, yet they always take お. Just memorize them one by one when you meet them; don’t force the rule.<br>Katakana loanwords usually don’t take it: just say <span class='gd-jp'>コーヒー</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>ございます: the polite form of あります</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ございます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ありません</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ございません</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ありました</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>ございました</span></span><br>You actually already know it: it’s the part inside <span class='gd-jp'>ありがとうございます</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>おはようございます</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お手洗いはあちらにございます。</span>（The restroom is over there.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>赤いのもございますよ。</span>（We have red ones too.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>申し訳ございません。</span>（I’m very sorry.）</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <b>でございます</b> is the polite version of <span class='gd-jp'>です</span>, and staff use it a lot when introducing things:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こちらがメニューでございます。</span>（This is the menu.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Respectful お・ご <b>can’t be applied to yourself</b>: when introducing yourself, say <span class='gd-jp'>私の名前は</span>, not 「私のお名前は」. Only “make it sound nice” words like <span class='gd-jp'>お茶</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯</span> can be used for yourself too.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ございます</span> is only for <b>things and matters</b>, never for people. To say whether a person is there, use <span class='gd-jp'>おります</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>いらっしゃいます</span> below.<br>・「でございます」 can’t be used to talk about the other person: to ask “Are you Mr. Tanaka?” say <span class='gd-jp'>田中様でいらっしゃいますか</span>, not 「田中様でございますか」.<br>・More お・ご isn’t better. Adding them to words that don’t usually take them sounds odd. Learn the ones in your textbook and the ones staff say all the time, and that’s enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>In the same shop, here are the ways to say “there is / is here”:<br><b>あります</b> (generally polite): <span class='gd-jp'>トイレはあちらにあります。</span>（The toilet is over there.）<br><b>ございます</b> (more polite, for things): <span class='gd-jp'>お手洗いはあちらにございます。</span>（The restroom is over there.）<br><b>おります</b> (for people on your own side; humbles yourself): <span class='gd-jp'>店長は事務所におります。</span>（The manager is in the office.）<br><b>いらっしゃいます</b> (for the other person or customers; raises them up): <span class='gd-jp'>田中様はいらっしゃいますか。</span>（Is Mr. Tanaka in?）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for <b>things</b> use ございます; for <b>people</b>, use おります for your own side and いらっしゃいます for the other person.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>お・ご can also attach to verbs to make honorific or humble expressions, for example <span class='gd-jp'>少々お待ちください。</span>（Please wait a moment.） Next, take a look at honorific language (尊敬語) and humble language (謙譲語).</div>","n4-66":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Contractions are Japanese words that get <b>squashed together in fast speech</b>. Just like English “going to” becomes “gonna,” Japanese people turn <span class='gd-jp'>見ている</span> into <span class='gd-jp'>見てる</span> when they chat. The meaning is exactly the same; it’s just more spoken and casual.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use them</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Everyday chat</span>When talking with friends and family. You’ll hear this almost everywhere in dramas, anime and manga.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Avoid</span>In formal situations such as writing reports, emailing your boss, or interviews, use the full forms.</div><div class='gd-h'>The four you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>1. ている → てる</b>: drop the い in the middle.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見ている</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>見てる</span></span><br>If it was 「でいる」, it becomes 「でる」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んでいる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んでる</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何してるの？</span>（What are you doing?）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>2. ておく → とく</b>: 「て」 and 「お」 merge into a single 「と」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買っておく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買っとく</span></span><br>If it was 「でおく」, it becomes 「どく」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んでおく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んどく</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>飲み物、買っとくね。</span>（I’ll buy the drinks ahead of time, okay?）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>3. てしまう → ちゃう</b>: 「てしま」 shrinks to 「ちゃ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べてしまう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃう</span></span><br>If it was 「でしまう」, it becomes 「じゃう」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲んでしまう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んじゃう</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ケーキ、全部食べちゃった。</span>（I ate the whole cake.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>4. では → じゃ</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生ではない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生じゃない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>They still conjugate after contracting</div><div class='gd-sec'>The contracted forms change into past, negative and て-form as usual:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見てる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見てた</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>見てない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買っとく</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買っといた</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>買っといて</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃう</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃった</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>宿題、まだ終わってない。</span>（I haven’t finished my homework yet.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日までに読んどいて。</span>（Read it before tomorrow, okay?）</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・If the original was 「で」, the contraction becomes voiced too: it’s <span class='gd-jp'>飲んじゃう</span>, not 飲んちゃう, and <span class='gd-jp'>読んどく</span>, not 読んとく.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>見てる</span> (watching right now) and <span class='gd-jp'>見とく</span> (watch in advance) sound similar but mean completely different things, so listen carefully.<br>・A “contraction + ます” like <span class='gd-jp'>見てます</span> is very common in shops and among coworkers, but in documents and letters you should still write <span class='gd-jp'>見ています</span>.<br>・Contraction only makes the speech more relaxed; the meaning doesn’t change. <span class='gd-jp'>ちゃう</span> can still mean “finish doing,” or “accidentally / oops.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>ては</span> also contracts to <span class='gd-jp'>ちゃ</span>. It differs from <span class='gd-jp'>ちゃう</span> by only a hair, but the meaning is very different:<br><span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃった。</span>（I ate it up.）＝<span class='gd-jp'>食べてしまった</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>食べちゃだめ。</span>（You mustn’t eat it.）＝<span class='gd-jp'>食べてはだめ</span></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if 「う」 or 「った」 follows, it’s てしまう; if 「だめ」 or 「いけない」 follows, it’s ては.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>In casual speech, 「なければ」 contracts to 「なきゃ」: <span class='gd-jp'>もう帰らなきゃ。</span>（I’ve got to go home now.）</div>","n4-71":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English you say \"I smell something nice\" or \"I hear a strange noise.\" Japanese doesn't use 「ある」 here. It uses <b>「noun + がする」</b>. The idea is that a sound, taste, or smell shows up on its own and you perceive it. The polite form is 「がします」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The thing you perceive (noun) + が + します (する among friends).<br>You usually put an adjective in front to describe what kind of sound or smell it is:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>匂い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いい匂いがします</span></span> (there's a nice smell)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>音</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>変な音がします</span></span> (there's a strange sound)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Sound</span><span class='gd-jp'>音</span> (sounds of things), <span class='gd-jp'>声</span> (voices of people or animals)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外で変な音がします。</span> (There's a strange noise outside.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>隣の部屋から子どもの声がする。</span> (I can hear a child's voice from the next room.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Smell</span><span class='gd-jp'>匂い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>香り</span> (a fragrance, a more refined word)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台所からいい匂いがする。</span> (A nice smell is coming from the kitchen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Taste</span>The <span class='gd-jp'>味</span> you notice when eating</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このお茶、レモンの味がする。</span> (This tea tastes like lemon.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Feeling</span><span class='gd-jp'>感じがする</span> means \"it gives off a ~ feeling,\" and <span class='gd-jp'>気がする</span> means \"I have a feeling that ~.\" You can put a whole sentence directly before <span class='gd-jp'>気がする</span> (use the plain form of the verb, the one without ます).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人は優しい感じがします。</span> (That person comes across as kind.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>前に会った気がします。</span> (I feel like we've met before.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle is <b>が</b>, not を: say <span class='gd-jp'>音がする</span>, never 「音をする」.<br>・Use がする for what you smell or hear right now. You wouldn't normally say 「いい匂いがあります」.<br>・In English the subject is \"<b>I</b>\" (\"I smell something nice\"), but in Japanese the subject is <b>the smell itself</b>. Don't say 「私はいい匂いがします」. To say where a smell is, say <span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は変な匂いがします</span> (This room smells strange).<br>・<b>Things you see don't take がする</b>: colors and shapes don't work this way, so you can't say 「きれいな色がします」.<br>・A noun must come before it. You can't say 「甘いがします」. Say <span class='gd-jp'>甘い匂いがします</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>甘い味がします</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>音がする vs 聞こえる</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>外で音がする。</span> (There's a sound outside.): the focus is that <b>a sound is occurring</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>外の音が聞こえる。</span> (I can hear the sounds outside.): the focus is <b>whether you can hear it</b>, so it's often used in cases like <span class='gd-jp'>よく聞こえない</span> (I can't hear well).</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>気がする vs と思う</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は来ないと思います。</span> (I think he won't come.): a clearer opinion or judgment.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は来ない気がします。</span> (I have a feeling he won't come.): hard to put into words, more of a hunch, and less certain.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use がする for \"there's a sound/smell,\" and 聞こえる for \"can/can't hear.\" Use と思う for a confident opinion, and 気がする for a mere hunch.</div>","n4-72":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜さ」turns an adjective into a <b>noun</b>, much like English turns \"high\" into \"height\" or \"important\" into \"importance.\" <span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> means \"high,\" and <span class='gd-jp'>高さ</span> means \"height.\" Use it to talk about <b>how ~ something is</b>, or the <b>feeling of being ~</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: drop the final 「い」 and add 「さ」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → drop い → <span class='gd-jp'>高さ</span></span> (height)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>深い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>深さ</span></span> (depth) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>重い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>重さ</span></span> (weight) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒さ</span></span> (the cold) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大きい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大きさ</span></span> (size)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives</b>: just add 「さ」 directly, with no な in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利さ</span></span> (convenience) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>親切</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>親切さ</span></span> (kindness) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大切</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大切さ</span></span> (importance)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Ones to memorize</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よさ</span></span> (good point, merit. Since <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> comes from <span class='gd-jp'>よい</span>, you don't say いいさ)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静けさ</span></span> (quietness, stillness. 静かさ isn't normally used)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Numbers and measurements</span>For things you can measure, like height, length, and weight, it's often followed by a number or 「どのくらい」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>富士山の高さは3776メートルです。</span> (Mt. Fuji's height is 3,776 meters.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋の広さはどのくらいですか。</span> (About how big is this room?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Feelings and qualities</span>For how strongly something comes across, or its good points and characteristics.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>北海道の冬の寒さにびっくりしました。</span> (I was shocked by how cold winter in Hokkaido is.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>病気になって、健康の大切さが分かりました。</span> (After getting sick, I realized how important health is.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店のよさは安いところです。</span> (This shop's good point is that it's cheap.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夜の静けさが好きです。</span> (I like the quietness of the night.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Drop the い: it's <span class='gd-jp'>高さ</span>, not 高いさ.<br>・な-adjectives don't take な: it's <span class='gd-jp'>便利さ</span>, not 便利なさ.<br>・Once you add さ it's a noun, so connect it with 「の」 before it and use は・が・を・に after it: <span class='gd-jp'>冬の寒さ</span>, not 冬は寒さ.<br>・「高さ」「重さ」「大きさ」 are <b>neutral measures</b>, just like \"height\" and \"weight\" in English. Even for a very short mountain, you still ask about its <span class='gd-jp'>高さ</span>, never 低さ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar words apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>You've already learned <span class='gd-jp'>楽しみ</span>. It and <span class='gd-jp'>楽しさ</span> both come from <span class='gd-jp'>楽しい</span>, but the meanings differ.<br><b>楽しさ</b> = how fun something is, that sense of enjoyment: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語の楽しさが分かりました。</span> (I understood the joy of learning Japanese.)<br><b>楽しみ</b> = anticipation, looking forward: <span class='gd-jp'>旅行を楽しみにしています。</span> (I'm looking forward to the trip.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use さ for degree, like \"how fun, how cold, how important.\" Use <span class='gd-jp'>楽しみ</span> for \"looking forward to it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A handy pattern for asking about size: <span class='gd-jp'>〜の長さはどのくらいですか。</span> (About how long is ~?) Swap 長さ for <span class='gd-jp'>重さ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>大きさ</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>深さ</span> to ask about all kinds of things.</div>","n4-69":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ずに」 is the <b>formal version</b> of 「〜ないで」. It means \"without doing ~\" or \"not doing ~, but ...\". In English you might say \"he left without telling anyone\" in conversation, but \"he departed without notice\" in writing. The meaning is the same, and 「〜ずに」 is just more bookish and refined.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Did B without doing A</span>You skipped something you would normally do and went straight to the next thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>朝ご飯を食べずに出かけた。</span>(I left without eating breakfast.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰にも言わずに会社を辞めた。</span>(I quit my job without telling anyone.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Instead of A, B</span>You drop one way of doing something and choose another.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>バスに乗らずに、歩いて帰った。</span>(I didn't take the bus; I walked home.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the verb in its ない form, then remove 「ない」 and replace it with 「ず」 or 「ずに」.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かず</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わず</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>持つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>持たず</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べず</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ず</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せず</span></span> (not しず)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ず</span>（こず）</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>The form is bookish overall, but a few expressions are common in everyday speech too. Memorize these as whole sentences:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遠慮せずに食べてください。</span>(Please don't hold back, go ahead and eat.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>気にせずに休んでね。</span>(Don't worry about it, just get some rest.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>思わず笑ってしまった。</span>(I couldn't help laughing.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>する becomes せず</b>, not しず: <span class='gd-jp'>勉強せずに</span>.<br>・For Group 1 verbs ending in 「う」, the ない form uses 「わ」: <span class='gd-jp'>言わず</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買わず</span>, not 言あず.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ず</span> is read <b>こず</b>, not きず.<br>・You can't use it to ask someone to do something. \"Please don't ~\" can only be <span class='gd-jp'>食べないでください</span>; there is no such thing as 「食べずにください」.<br>・When chatting with friends, ordinary verbs mostly take 「ないで」. Using 「ずに」 everywhere sounds a bit like reading from a script. The fixed expressions above are the exception.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ないで</b> = the casual version: <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持たないで出かけた。</span>(I went out without an umbrella.)<br><b>ずに</b> = the formal, written version, with exactly the same meaning: <span class='gd-jp'>傘を持たずに出かけた。</span>(I went out without an umbrella.)<br>In writing you'll also often see 「ず」 followed directly by a comma. This often means \"because ~ didn't happen\", much like 「なくて」: <span class='gd-jp'>バスが来ず、遅刻してしまった。</span>(The bus didn't come, so I ended up late.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: when you see 「ずに」, swap it for 「ないで」 in your head and you'll understand it. When you're unsure what to say out loud, 「ないで」 is the safest choice.</div>","n4-70":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「こと」 works like <b>\"the act of ~\" or \"~ing\"</b>. In English you can simply say \"Reading is fun\", but in Japanese a verb can't be the subject on its own. You first add 「こと」 after it, which packages \"read\" into \"the act of reading\". Only then can it be the subject or the answer in a sentence.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As the subject</span>Put it before 「は」 or 「が」, then say what it is like.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日運動することは大切です。</span>(Exercising every day is important.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅刻しないことが大切です。</span>(Not being late is important.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>As the answer</span>For \"My hobby is ~\" or \"My dream is ~\", when the \"~\" is an action, use 「～ことです」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私の趣味は写真を撮ることです。</span>(My hobby is taking photos.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夢は日本で働くことです。</span>(My dream is to work in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs connect directly, with no change in form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読むこと</span></span><br>For \"not doing\", use the ない form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読まない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読まないこと</span></span><br>For \"having done\", use the た form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読んだこと</span></span><br>Adjectives can take it too. Then it feels more like \"a ~ thing\": <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>楽しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>楽しいこと</span></span> (fun things). Remember to add な after な-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>好き</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>好きなこと</span></span> (things you like)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You've actually met it already. The 「こと」 inside <span class='gd-jp'>ことができます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>たことがあります</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ことにします</span> is this same \"the act of ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Following English instinct, where \"reading\" can be a subject, and using the bare verb as the subject: <span class='gd-jp'>本を読むが好きです</span> is wrong. You always need <span class='gd-jp'>こと</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>の</span> in between: <span class='gd-jp'>本を読むことが好きです</span>.<br>・Forgetting the な after a な-adjective: say <span class='gd-jp'>好きなこと</span>, not 好きこと or 好きだこと.<br>・「Noun＋のこと」 is a different meaning, \"about ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>彼のことが好きです</span> means \"I like him (as a person)\". It is not the same as turning an action into a noun here, so don't mix them up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>「の」 can also turn an action into \"the act of ~\". In many cases either one works, but <b>some positions allow only one of them</b>. It isn't simply that 「こと」 is more formal.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Either works</span>When it is followed by an evaluation like 「好き」, 「大切」 or 「難しい」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本を読むのが好きです。</span>(I like reading: more common in conversation)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本を読むことが好きです。</span>(I like reading: slightly more formal)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only こと</span>At the end of a sentence in 「～は～ことです」, and in fixed patterns like ことができます.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>趣味は本を読むことです。</span>(My hobby is reading.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Switching to 「のです」 is wrong. The 「の」 would be heard as the explanatory 「のです」, which sounds very unnatural, so the sentence ending must be 「ことです」. In the same way, <span class='gd-jp'>日本語を話すことができます</span> can't be changed to use の.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Only の</span>When you see or hear an action actually happening in front of you.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犬が走るのを見ました。</span>(I saw a dog running.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>隣の部屋で誰かが歌っているのが聞こえます。</span>(I can hear someone singing in the next room.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here, <span class='gd-jp'>走ることを見ました</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>歌っていることが聞こえます</span> are both wrong.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: before 「～です」 at the end of a sentence, and in fixed patterns like ことができる, use <b>こと</b>. For actions you see or hear happening right in front of you, use <b>の</b>. With 「好き」 or 「大切」 either works, but の sounds smoother in conversation.</div>","n4-73":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことがあります」means <b>\"sometimes ~\"</b> or <b>\"there are times when ~\"</b>. It says something <b>doesn't happen every time, but does happen now and then</b>. It's like the \"delays may occur\" notices you see in train announcements or on online shopping pages. They warn you that this situation is possible.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Sometimes you <b>do</b> it: dictionary form + ことがあります<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れることがあります</span></span> (sometimes I'm late / it's sometimes delayed)<br>Sometimes you <b>don't</b> do it: ない form + ことがあります<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べないことがあります</span></span> (sometimes I don't eat)<br>With friends, use the plain form <span class='gd-jp'>ことがある</span>. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing your own habits</span>It's not an everyday thing, but it happens occasionally. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>ときどき</span> (sometimes) and <span class='gd-jp'>たまに</span> (once in a while).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいとき、朝ご飯を食べないことがあります。</span> (When I'm busy, I sometimes skip breakfast.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>たまに電車で寝てしまうことがある。</span> (I occasionally fall asleep on the train.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Warnings and announcements</span>Shops, stations and product descriptions love this pattern. It tells you up front, \"this can happen, so be aware.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨の日は、バスが遅れることがあります。</span> (On rainy days, the bus is sometimes delayed.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>商品の色が写真と違うことがあります。</span> (The color of the product may sometimes differ from the photo.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't use it for things that happen <b>every day, always, or without fail</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>毎日コーヒーを飲むことがあります</span> is wrong. If you drink it every day, just say <span class='gd-jp'>毎日コーヒーを飲みます</span>.<br>・English words like \"will\" and \"can\" cover many meanings, and not all of them map to ことがあります. \"I can speak Japanese\" is <span class='gd-jp'>日本語が話せます</span>, and \"It will rain tomorrow\" is <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るでしょう</span>. Use ことがあります only for <b>\"sometimes\"</b>.<br>・The verb before it must be in dictionary form or ない form. If you use the た form, the meaning changes completely (see below).<br>・You'll often hear <span class='gd-jp'>遅れることもあります</span>. Swapping が for も changes the tone to <b>\"it may also happen that ~\"</b>, which is softer and very common in announcements.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Dictionary form + ことがある</b> = sometimes does (a present tendency)<br><span class='gd-jp'>この電車は遅れることがあります。</span> (This train is sometimes delayed.)<br><b>た form + ことがある</b> = has done ~ before (a past experience)<br><span class='gd-jp'>この電車は遅れたことがあります。</span> (This train has been delayed before.)<br>It's only one word's difference. The first means \"this happens now and then in general,\" and the second means \"this has happened at least once in the past.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>かもしれません</b> = a guess about whether it will happen <b>this one time</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>明日は電車が遅れるかもしれません。</span> (The train might be delayed tomorrow.)<br><b>ことがあります</b> = describes the <b>usual</b> situation, where you run into it once in a while<br><span class='gd-jp'>雪の日は電車が遅れることがあります。</span> (On snowy days, trains are sometimes delayed.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: \"have done ~ before\" takes た form + ことがある. \"Sometimes does ~ in general\" takes dictionary form + ことがある. \"Might ~ this time\" takes かもしれません.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It also works with nouns. Add <span class='gd-jp'>の</span> after the noun: <span class='gd-jp'>この時間は満席のことがあります。</span> (This time slot is sometimes fully booked.)</div>","n4-74":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, you soften your language with seniors: \"Would you like some tea?\" instead of \"Want some tea?\" Japanese has a similar trick: <b>put the actions of seniors, superiors and customers into the「れる・られる」form</b>, and it sounds respectful.<br>For example, \"the company president went home\" is normally <span class='gd-jp'>帰りました</span>, and the more respectful version is <span class='gd-jp'>帰られました</span>. It's the easiest entry point into honorific language, and it's a fairly light level of politeness.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking about a senior's actions</span>When you tell someone what a teacher or boss did.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長はもう帰られました。</span> (The department head has already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長は来週、大阪に出張されます。</span> (The president will go on a business trip to Osaka next week.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking them directly</span>When you ask a teacher or customer what they're going to do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生はいつ日本へ行かれますか。</span> (When will you go to Japan, teacher?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何を飲まれますか。</span> (What would you like to drink?)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>Good news: it looks <b>exactly like the passive form</b>, so if you know the passive, you already know this.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the「あ」-row sound of the same row, then add「れる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>行かれる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読まれる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話される</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰られる</span></span><br>Verbs ending in「う」change to <b>わ</b>, not あ: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会われる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop the final「る」and add「られる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出かける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出かけられる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>始める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>始められる</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (only two verbs, just memorize them)</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>される</span></span> (<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出発する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出発される</span></span>), <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span> (こられる)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In actual speech, you usually follow it with ます: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かれる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かれます</span></span>, and the past is <span class='gd-jp'>行かれました</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use it only for <b>other people</b>. Using the honorific form for your own actions sounds odd. <span class='gd-jp'>私は明日行かれます</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>私は明日行きます</span>.<br>・Don't use it for <b>your own family</b> when talking to outsiders either. You can't say <span class='gd-jp'>父は帰られました</span>. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>父は帰りました</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> becomes <span class='gd-jp'>会われる</span>, not「あれる」.<br>・For Group 2 verbs and <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>, the honorific form happens to look identical to the potential form (\"can ~\"). <span class='gd-jp'>来られます</span> could mean \"you will come\" or \"you can come.\" To avoid confusion, Japanese speakers often switch to dedicated honorific verbs, such as <span class='gd-jp'>いらっしゃる</span> for <span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>召し上がる</span> for <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>ご覧になる</span> for <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span>.<br>・Don't stack two honorific forms together. <span class='gd-jp'>お帰りになられる</span> is often heard, but it's considered over-polite and incorrect. Pick just one.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Honorific form</b> = the senior person does the action <b>themselves</b>, and nobody is being acted upon:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長が帰られました。</span> (The department head went home.)<br><b>Passive form</b> = someone is <b>having something done to them</b>, and the person doing it is often marked with「に」:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長に怒られました。</span> (I was scolded by the department head.)<br><b>Potential form</b> = asks <b>\"can they or can't they?\"</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生は刺身が食べられますか。</span> (Can you eat sashimi, teacher?)<br>They all use the same「られます」, so you have to rely on context to tell which one it is.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the subject is a senior person doing the action themselves, with no sense of \"being done to\" and no \"can or can't,\" it's the honorific form.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>A more polite pattern is「お + ます-stem + になる」: <span class='gd-jp'>社長はお帰りになりました。</span> (The president went home.) It's more courteous than <span class='gd-jp'>帰られました</span>. Check out the lesson on 尊敬語 (honorific language) next.</div>","n4-75":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>In English, you say “drink, don’t drink, can drink, be drunk, make him drink” by adding words around “drink” (or switching to a different form). Japanese <b>changes the tail end of the verb</b>, and how it changes depends on which group the verb belongs to. So whichever form you’re learning, <b>step one is always to sort the verb into its group</b>. Get that right and the rest is just applying rules.</div><div class='gd-h'>Step 1: Work out which group the verb is in</div><div class='gd-sec'>Look at the dictionary form (the unconjugated form you find in a dictionary, e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span>) and ask yourself three questions in order:<br><b>1. Is it する or 来る?</b> If so, it’s <b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>. “Noun + する” verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>掃除する</span> belong here too.<br><b>2. Does it end in something other than る?</b> Verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>書く</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> are always <b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>.<br><b>3. If it ends in る, listen to the sound just before る:</b><br>If it’s an “i” or “e” sound, it’s usually <b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> (べ is be), <span class='gd-jp'>起きる</span> (き is ki), <span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> (み is mi).<br>If it’s an “a, u, o” sound, it’s <b>Group 1</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>作る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>乗る</span>.<br>Step 3 has a few exceptions. <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>入る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>走る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>切る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>要る</span> look like Group 2 but are actually <b>Group 1</b>. Just memorize them.</div><div class='gd-h'>One verb, six of the most common forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1: using <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> as the example.</b> You swap the final “む” for another sound in the same row:<br>む becomes み, add ます: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲みます</span></span> (drink, polite)<br>む becomes ま, add ない: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まない</span></span> (don’t drink)<br>む becomes め, add る: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲める</span></span> (can drink, potential form)<br>む becomes ま, add れる: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲まれる</span></span> (be drunk, passive form)<br>む becomes ま, add せる: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲ませる</span></span> (make someone drink, causative form)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The Group 1 て-form</b> follows its own set of rules, decided by the last kana:<br>う・つ・る → って: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>待つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>待って</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>帰って</span></span><br>む・ぶ・ぬ → んで: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んで</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊ぶ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んで</span></span><br>く → いて: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>書いて</span></span>　ぐ → いで: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泳ぐ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泳いで</span></span><br>す → して: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話して</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2: using <span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> as the example.</b> This is the easiest group. Always drop the る, then add the ending:<br><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>食べて</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span> (can eat), <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span> (be eaten), <span class='gd-jp'>食べさせる</span> (make someone eat)<br>For Group 2, the potential and passive forms <b>look exactly the same</b>, so you can only tell them apart from context.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3: only two verbs, so just memorize them</b><br><span class='gd-jp'>する</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>します</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>しない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>して</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span> (can do), <span class='gd-jp'>される</span> (be done), <span class='gd-jp'>させる</span> (make someone do)<br><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>来ます</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来て</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>来られる</span> (can come; the passive looks the same), <span class='gd-jp'>来させる</span> (make someone come)<br>The kanji in 来る never changes, but its reading does:<br>dictionary form and ば-form use く (くる, くれば);<br>ます-form, て-form and た-form use き (きます, きて, きた);<br>ない-form, potential, passive and causative use こ (こない, こられる, こさせる).</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms don’t change one after another</div><div class='gd-sec'>Every one of the forms above <b>is made directly from the dictionary form</b>. You don’t need to turn a verb into the ます-form before making the て-form, or into the potential before making the passive.<br>One handy pattern: the potential, passive and causative all end in “〜る”, so <b>once you’ve made them, each one is a new Group 2 verb</b> and can be conjugated further with the Group 2 rules:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲めます</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲めない</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語の新聞が読めます。</span> (I can read Japanese newspapers.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>漢字はまだあまり書けない。</span> (I still can’t write kanji very well.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Exceptions like <span class='gd-jp'>帰る</span> are Group 1, so you say <span class='gd-jp'>帰ります</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>帰らない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>帰って</span>, not 帰ます, 帰ない, 帰て.<br>・For verbs ending in う, the う changes to <b>わ</b>, not あ, in the ない-form, passive and causative: <span class='gd-jp'>買わない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買われる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>買わせる</span>.<br>・The て-form of <span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>行って</span>. It doesn’t follow the “く → いて” rule, so you can’t say 行いて.<br>・For “noun + する” verbs, the potential form replaces する with <span class='gd-jp'>できる</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>勉強できる</span> (can study). <span class='gd-jp'>勉強される</span> is the passive and means something different.<br>・In casual speech you’ll often hear <span class='gd-jp'>食べれる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>見れる</span> (with the ら dropped). It’s very common in conversation, but on exams and in formal writing use <span class='gd-jp'>食べられる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>見られる</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar-looking forms apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>With the same verb <span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span>, the potential, passive and causative differ by only a sound or two, yet the meanings are completely different:<br><b>Can</b>: potential form</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>少しならお酒が飲める。</span> (I can drink alcohol if it’s just a little.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Be done to</b>: passive form</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟に私のジュースを飲まれた。</span> (My little brother drank my juice.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Make/let someone</b>: causative form</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもに薬を飲ませる。</span> (I make my child take medicine.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick check: for Group 1, look at the ending. “e-row sound + る” means can, “a-row sound + れる” means passive, and “a-row sound + せる” means causative. For Group 2, “られる” can be either potential or passive, so rely on context. “させる” is always causative.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Once the groups feel familiar, study the forms one at a time: the volitional form (<span class='gd-jp'>飲もう</span>), the potential form, the ば-form (<span class='gd-jp'>飲めば</span>), the passive form and the causative form. Last, learn the causative-passive form, which stacks the causative and the passive (<span class='gd-jp'>飲ませられる</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>飲まされる</span>, to be forced to drink).</div>","n4-30":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>These three all mean \"<b>do something for someone</b>.\" Use <b>てあげる</b> when you do something for someone else, <b>てくれる</b> when someone does something for you, and <b>てもらう</b> when you receive someone's help. The directions are exactly the same as the gift verbs あげる・くれる・もらう. The only difference is that what's passed along isn't a present but \"a favor.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to the て-form, then add あげる／くれる／もらう.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>貸す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>貸して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>貸してあげる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>作る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>作って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>作ってくれる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>撮る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>撮って</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>撮ってもらう</span></span><br>For the polite form, use あげます、くれます、もらいます.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I do it for someone</span><b>てあげる</b>. The pattern is \"I は (person) に ～てあげる.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>妹に本を読んであげました。</span> (I read a book to my little sister.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友だちに傘を貸してあげた。</span> (I lent my umbrella to a friend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Someone does it for me</span><b>てくれる</b>. The pattern is \"(person) が ～てくれる.\" You usually don't say \"for me,\" because くれる itself already means \"toward me.\" Family counts as \"my side\" too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母がお弁当を作ってくれた。</span> (My mom made me a packed lunch.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友だちが駅まで送ってくれました。</span> (My friend took me to the station.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I receive help</span><b>てもらう</b>. The pattern is \"I は (person) に ～てもらう.\" Here「に」means \"<b>from whom</b>\" you receive the help.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友だちに写真を撮ってもらいました。</span> (I had a friend take a photo of me.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>美容院で髪を切ってもらいました。</span> (I got my hair cut at the salon.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The stylist cut your hair, not you, so Japanese naturally uses てもらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking for a favor</span>To ask for help, you can simply ask \"Could you ～ for me?\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと手伝ってくれない？</span> (Can you give me a hand? — to a friend)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このペンを貸してもらえませんか。</span> (Could I borrow this pen? — polite)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・くれる can only point toward \"me (my side).\" When I help someone else, it's always あげる. You can't say「私が友だちに貸してくれた」.<br>・The「に」with もらう doesn't mean \"to\": <span class='gd-jp'>友だちに手伝ってもらった</span> means \"my friend helped me,\" not \"I helped my friend.\" If you read に as \"to,\" it's easy to flip the meaning.<br>・Which particle marks the other person depends on the original verb, not always に. \"I carried my mom's bags for her\" is <span class='gd-jp'>母の荷物を持ってあげた</span>. Saying「母に持ってあげた」sounds unnatural.<br>・In English, \"My friend taught me Japanese\" already shows who benefited through \"me.\" In Japanese, if you only say <span class='gd-jp'>友だちが日本語を教えた</span>, it sounds like you're talking about someone else's business, and the gratitude is missing. When someone does something for you, Japanese people almost always add てくれる: <span class='gd-jp'>友だちが日本語を教えてくれた</span>.<br>・てあげる carries a nuance of \"I'm doing you a favor.\" Saying「持ってあげましょうか」to a teacher or boss is rude. Say <span class='gd-jp'>お持ちしましょうか</span> instead. Be careful when telling others \"I did X for him,\" too, since it can sound like you're taking credit.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>てくれる and てもらう often describe the same event. Only the focus changes:<br><span class='gd-jp'>友だちが手伝ってくれた。</span> (My friend helped me.) The focus is on the friend: \"how kind of them.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>友だちに手伝ってもらった。</span> (I got my friend to help.) The focus is on me: \"I received help.\"<br>In most situations either works. But when the other person offers help <b>on their own initiative</b>, くれる is the most natural: <span class='gd-jp'>頼んでいないのに、友だちが手伝ってくれた。</span> (I hadn't even asked, but my friend came and helped me.)<br>When <b>you asked for it or paid for the service</b>, もらう is more common: <span class='gd-jp'>店の人に包んでもらいました。</span> (I had the shop staff wrap it for me.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if I help someone else, use あげる. If the other person is the subject and is marked with が, use くれる. If I'm the subject and the other person is marked with に, use もらう.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>With teachers and elders, switch to the honorific versions:<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生が教えてくださいました。</span> (The teacher taught me.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>先生に教えていただきました。</span> (I was taught by the teacher.)<br>When asking a favor, 「〜てもらえませんか」becomes even more polite as「〜ていただけませんか」. You can learn these together with honorific (尊敬語) and humble (謙譲語) forms.</div>","n4-2":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜つもりです」means <b>\"I plan to ~\"</b> or <b>\"I'm going to ~\"</b>. You've already made up your mind to do something, and you use it to tell someone your plan.<br>For example, a friend asks what you're doing over summer break, and you say \"I'm planning to go to Japan\". That's <span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行くつもりです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Plan to do</span>Verb <b>dictionary form</b> (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary) + つもりです<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くつもりです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買うつもりです</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勉強する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勉強するつもりです</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ留学するつもりです。</span>(I plan to study abroad in Japan next year.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末は家でゆっくりするつもりです。</span>(I plan to relax at home this weekend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Plan not to do</span>Verb <b>ない form</b> + つもりです<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>吸う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>吸わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>吸わないつもりです</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もうタバコは吸わないつもりです。</span>(I don't plan to smoke anymore.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>When you're talking with friends, swap です for <b>だ</b>, or just stop at つもり: <span class='gd-jp'>夏休み、どうするつもり？</span> (What are you planning to do over summer break?)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>つもりはありません</span>\"I have no intention at all.\" It's more firm than 「〜ないつもりです」 and is often used to refuse.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会社をやめるつもりはありません。</span>(I have no intention of quitting my job.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>つもりでした</span>\"I was planning to ~.\" It's usually followed by \"but it didn't work out.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く寝るつもりでしたが、ゲームをしてしまいました。</span>(I was planning to go to bed early, but I ended up playing video games.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Put the <b>dictionary form</b> before つもり, not the ます form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行くつもりです</span>, not 「行きますつもりです」.<br>・It expresses a <b>person's intention</b>, so you can't use it for things outside your control. Japanese doesn't use つもり for something like \"it's going to clear up tomorrow.\"<br>・It's mainly for talking about <b>your own</b> plans. For someone else's plans, you usually add 「そうです」 or 「と言っていました」: <span class='gd-jp'>田中さんは留学するつもりだそうです。</span> (I hear Tanaka plans to study abroad.)<br>・Asking a teacher or boss directly 「<span class='gd-jp'>どうするつもりですか</span>」 sounds like you're demanding \"What on earth do you intend to do?\" To ask an older or senior person about their plans, <span class='gd-jp'>ご予定は？</span> is more polite.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take \"going to Japan next year\". Different phrasings show how settled the plan is:<br><b>ようと思っています</b> = you have the idea in mind and are thinking it over (N4-42)<br><span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ行こうと思っています。</span> (I'm thinking of going to Japan next year.)<br><b>つもりです</b> = a plan you've firmly decided on, stronger than the sentence above<br><span class='gd-jp'>来年、日本へ行くつもりです。</span> (I plan to go to Japan next year.)<br><b>予定です</b> = already scheduled, with concrete arrangements, like a booked flight and a set date<br><span class='gd-jp'>来年3月に日本へ行く予定です。</span> (I'm scheduled to go to Japan in March next year.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ようと思う for \"still thinking about it\", つもり for \"my mind is made up\", and 予定 for \"it's already on the calendar\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To say \"I've decided to ~\", learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜ことにします</span> (N4-47) next: <span class='gd-jp'>来年留学することにしました。</span> (I've decided to study abroad next year.)</div>","n4-3":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かもしれません」means <b>\"maybe ~\"</b> or <b>\"might ~\"</b>. You're not sure, and you think something is possible, but it may not happen. Like saying \"It might rain tomorrow,\" which in Japanese is <span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るかもしれません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing</span>You're not sure about something, and you state what you think is possible.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は雨が降るかもしれません。</span>(It might rain tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店、今日は休みかもしれない。</span>(That shop might be closed today.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はもう帰ったかもしれない。</span>(He might have already gone home.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Giving a heads-up</span>It's also very common when talking about yourself. It softens what you say and prepares the other person.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>すみません、少し遅れるかもしれません。</span>(Sorry, I might be a little late.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>土曜日はちょっと行けないかもしれない。</span>(I might not be able to make it on Saturday.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: no change needed, just attach it directly. The same goes for negative and past forms.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くかもしれない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないかもしれない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行ったかもしれない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いかもしれない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: drop 「だ」 before attaching.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>静かかもしれない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だ</span> → drop だ → <span class='gd-jp'>雨かもしれない</span></span><br>But keep the past 「だった」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨だったかもしれない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>かもしれません</span>The polite version, for teachers, coworkers and customers.<br><span class='gd-tag'>かもしれない</span>Used among friends and family.<br><span class='gd-tag'>かも</span>An even more casual shortened form, heard all the time when young people and friends chat.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あ、財布忘れたかも。</span>(Oh, I think I forgot my wallet.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't keep 「だ」 after nouns or な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>雨かもしれない</span>, not 「雨だかもしれない」.<br>・The final 「ない」 in 「かもしれない」 is <b>not a negative</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>来るかもしれない</span> means \"might come\". To say \"might not come\", make the verb before it negative: <span class='gd-jp'>来ないかもしれない</span>.<br>・It expresses low certainty, around 50-50 or even lower. Don't use it for things you're sure about, or you'll sound irresponsible. For example, if you've made plans with someone and say <span class='gd-jp'>行くかもしれません</span>, they'll think you might not show up.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say you're guessing whether Suzuki will come. The level of certainty differs:<br><b>かもしれない</b> = might or might not, you have no real idea: <span class='gd-jp'>鈴木さんは来るかもしれません。</span> (Suzuki might come.)<br><b>でしょう</b> = probably, it seems quite likely: <span class='gd-jp'>鈴木さんは来るでしょう。</span> (Suzuki will probably come.)<br><b>はず</b> = should, based on solid grounds, like him saying himself yesterday that he'd come: <span class='gd-jp'>鈴木さんは来るはずです。</span> (Suzuki should be coming.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: from low to high certainty, it's かもしれない ＜ でしょう ＜ はず. If you have no real idea and it's just \"maybe\", use かもしれない. If you have a reason and think \"it should happen\", use はず.</div>","n4-39":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜でいちばん」 means <b>“the most ~ in/of …”</b>. First use 「で」 to mark the range (the class, Japan, your family), then use 「いちばん」 to say “most”. In English you'd say “the tallest in the class is Tanaka”, and in Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>クラスでいちばん背が高いのは田中さんです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>range ＋ <b>で</b> ＋ <b>いちばん</b> ＋ adjective<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い山</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本でいちばん高い山</span></span>(the tallest mountain in Japan)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>富士山は日本でいちばん高い山です。</span>(Mt. Fuji is the tallest mountain in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>クラスで田中さんがいちばん背が高いです。</span>(Tanaka is the tallest in the class.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「いちばん」 isn't limited to adjectives. It can also go before a verb, meaning “do ~ the most / the earliest”:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族で父がいちばん早く起きます。</span>(In my family, my dad gets up the earliest.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If the range is “a category of things” or you've just listed some options, <b>の中で</b> (“among ~”) is common:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>スポーツの中で、サッカーがいちばん好きです。</span>(Among sports, I like soccer the most.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking “which is the most ~?”</span>range ＋ で ＋ question word ＋ が ＋ いちばん～ですか。 Pick the question word to fit what you're asking about: things use <span class='gd-jp'>何</span> (use <span class='gd-jp'>どれ</span> when you've listed options), people use <span class='gd-jp'>だれ</span>, places use <span class='gd-jp'>どこ</span>, and times use <span class='gd-jp'>いつ</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一年でいつがいちばん好きですか。</span>(What time of year do you like the most?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>夏がいちばん好きです。</span>(I like summer the most.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本でどこがいちばんよかったですか。</span>(Where was your favorite place in Japan?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>“The most ~ is X”</span>Put the answer at the very end for more emphasis:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家族でいちばん料理が上手なのは母です。</span>(The best cook in my family is my mom.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use <b>で</b> for the range, not に: it's <span class='gd-jp'>日本でいちばん</span>, not 日本にいちばん.<br>・In questions, put <b>が</b> after the question word, not は: it's <span class='gd-jp'>何がいちばん好きですか</span>, not 何はいちばん. Answers usually use が too: <span class='gd-jp'>夏がいちばん好きです</span>.<br>・The object of 「好き」 and 「上手」 also takes が: <span class='gd-jp'>料理がいちばん上手です</span>, not 料理をいちばん上手.<br>・「いちばん」 already means “the most”, so don't add とても before it.<br>・You can also write it in kanji as <span class='gd-jp'>一番</span>. It's the same word with the same reading.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>The key is <b>how many things you're comparing</b>. Say you're asking what someone likes to drink:<br><b>Comparing two</b> uses 「どちら」 ＋ 「ほうが」 (〜より…ほうが):<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーと紅茶とどちらが好きですか。</span>(Which do you prefer, coffee or tea?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーのほうが好きです。</span>(I prefer coffee.)<br><b>Comparing three or more</b> uses 「どれ／何」 ＋ 「いちばん」:<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーと紅茶とジュースの中で、どれがいちばん好きですか。</span>(Among coffee, tea and juice, which do you like the most?)<br><span class='gd-jp'>コーヒーがいちばん好きです。</span>(I like coffee the most.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if English would use “-er / more / prefer”, you're comparing two, so use ほうが. If it would use “-est / most”, you're comparing three or more (or a whole range), so use いちばん.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「今まで～た中で、いちばん…」 = “the most … I've ever ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>今まで食べた中で、これがいちばんおいしいです。</span>(This is the most delicious thing I've ever eaten.)</div>","n4-4":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AはBより～」 means <b>\"A is more ~ than B\"</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>東京は大阪より人が多い。</span> means \"Tokyo has more people than Osaka.\"<br>The biggest difference is <b>word order</b>. English puts \"than\" before B, but Japanese <span class='gd-jp'>より</span> goes <b>after</b> B, like a \"than\" stuck onto the end of B: \"B-than.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>は</span> to the subject A, add <span class='gd-jp'>より</span> to B (the thing you compare against), and put the adjective last.<br><span class='gd-tag'>い-adjectives</span> just take です after them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は昨日より暑いです。</span>(Today is hotter than yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjectives</span> also just take です. Among friends, use だ or nothing at all.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車はバスより便利です。</span>(Trains are more convenient than buses.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Comparing one aspect</span> To say which aspect you are comparing, use 「～が＋adjective」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄は私より背が高いです。</span>(My older brother is taller than me.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>姉は私より料理が上手です。</span>(My older sister is a better cook than me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Works with actions too</span> What comes last doesn't have to be an adjective. It can also describe how you do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟は私よりよく食べます。</span>(My younger brother eats more than me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You'll also hear <span class='gd-jp'>よりも</span>. The meaning is the same, just a bit more emphatic.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Whatever より attaches to is the one being compared against (the \"lower\" one).</b> If you translate straight from English word order, it's easy to misplace it: <span class='gd-jp'>東京より大阪は人が多い</span> becomes \"Osaka has more people than Tokyo,\" so the meaning flips completely.<br>・The adjective <b>doesn't change form</b>, and you don't need to force in a \"more.\" English builds the comparison into the word (\"taller\"), but in Japanese plain <span class='gd-jp'>高いです</span> is enough. Adding <span class='gd-jp'>もっと</span> actually changes the meaning to \"both are tall, and A is even taller.\"<br>・To name the aspect you're comparing, use <span class='gd-jp'>が</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>背が高い</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>気温が高い</span>. Don't write を.<br>・To say \"A isn't as ~ as B,\" Japanese people usually use <span class='gd-jp'>ほど…ない</span>. 「より～くない」 is less common.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation, comparing the populations of Tokyo and Osaka:<br><b>AはBより</b> = you're talking about A as the subject and bring in B for comparison: <span class='gd-jp'>東京は大阪より人が多いです。</span> (Tokyo has more people than Osaka.)<br><b>BよりAのほうが</b> = you put the two side by side and <b>pick A</b>. It's often used to answer \"Which one is more ~?\": <span class='gd-jp'>大阪より東京のほうが人が多いです。</span> (Compared to Osaka, Tokyo has more people.)<br><b>BほどAない</b> = the reverse, \"not as ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>大阪は東京ほど人が多くないです。</span> (Osaka doesn't have as many people as Tokyo.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to simply describe \"A is more ~ than B,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>AはBより</span>. To pick one of two, use <span class='gd-jp'>のほうが</span>. To say \"not as ~,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>ほど…ない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>To ask \"Which one is more ~?\": <span class='gd-jp'>電車とバスとどちらが便利ですか。</span> (Which is more convenient, the train or the bus?) You answer with <span class='gd-jp'>～より…ほうが</span>. To pick the top one out of three or more, learn <span class='gd-jp'>～でいちばん</span> next.</div>","n4-38":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AはBほど～ない」 means <b>\"A is not as ~ as B\"</b>. The order is almost the same as in English: <span class='gd-jp'>京都は東京ほど人が多くない</span> = Kyoto does <b>not</b> have <b>as</b> many people as Tokyo. <span class='gd-jp'>ほど</span> means \"to that extent,\" and it must always be followed by a negative.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to build it</div><div class='gd-sec'>A (the subject) は B (the thing compared) ほど + negative form<br>Get the negative form ready first:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>い-adjectives</span> Replace the final 「い」 with 「くない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → replace い with くない → <span class='gd-jp'>高くない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くない</span></span><br><b>Exception:</b> <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>よくない</span></span> (you can't say いくない)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店の料理は前ほどよくない。</span>(The food at this restaurant isn't as good as it used to be.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>な-adjectives</span> Add 「じゃない」 after them, or 「ではない」 for a more formal tone.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利じゃない</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアプリは前のほど便利じゃない。</span>(This app isn't as convenient as the previous one.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verbs</span> Just use the ない form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べない</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は兄ほど食べない。</span>(I don't eat as much as my older brother.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To sound polite, just add です after it: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒くない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒くないです</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>思ったほど～ない</span> \"Not as ~ as I thought.\" Extremely common in daily conversation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>テストは思ったほど難しくなかった。</span>(The test wasn't as hard as I thought.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>前ほど～ない</span> \"Not as ~ as before.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>最近は前ほど忙しくないです。</span>(I'm not as busy as I used to be lately.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それほど～ない</span> Leave out B and just say \"not that ~.\" It's handy for answering someone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語、難しい？</span>(Is Japanese hard?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いや、それほど難しくないよ。</span>(Nah, it's not that hard.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This pattern is <b>always followed by a negative</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>京都は東京ほど人が多いです</span> is wrong. To say \"more,\" use より instead.<br>・What goes before <span class='gd-jp'>ほど</span> is the <b>stronger side</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>今日は昨日ほど暑くない</span> means yesterday was hotter, so don't read it backwards.<br>・The negative of <span class='gd-jp'>いい</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>よくない</span>. Writing いくない is wrong.<br>・The tone is softer. <span class='gd-jp'>私の部屋は田中さんの部屋ほど広くない</span> (My room isn't as big as Tanaka's) sounds more polite than bluntly saying \"mine is smaller.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: yesterday was hot, and today is a bit cooler.<br><b>より（～ほうが）</b> = directly says who is \"more\" something, and can take an affirmative:<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日より今日のほうが涼しい。</span> (Today is cooler than yesterday.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は昨日より涼しい。</span> (Today is cooler than yesterday.)<br><b>ほど～ない</b> = says A \"doesn't reach B's level,\" and is always followed by a negative:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今日は昨日ほど暑くない。</span> (Today isn't as hot as yesterday.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if you'd say \"-er than\" in English, use より. If you'd say \"not as ... as,\" use ほど + negative.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>After comparing two things, learn \"the most ~ in a group\": <span class='gd-jp'>クラスでいちばん背が高い。</span> (He's the tallest in the class.)</div>"});
